(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1a.aa28403d75.js"]={"1":[{"id":"sdl1_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the three major hormone classes (peptides, steroids, and amines) and describe their chemical properties and solubility characteristics.","batch":1,"stem":"A pharmacology student is comparing the synthesis and storage properties of different hormone classes. Which of the following best characterizes steroid hormones compared to peptide hormones?","choices":{"B":"Peptide hormones are synthesized from cholesterol esters stored in cytoplasmic vacuoles and are released by simple diffusion across the plasma membrane without exocytosis.","A":"Steroid hormones are synthesized from cholesterol on demand and are not stored in secretory vesicles, diffusing directly into the bloodstream immediately after synthesis.","C":"Steroid hormones are synthesized as preprohormones on the rough endoplasmic reticulum, packaged into Golgi-derived vesicles, and released by regulated exocytosis upon stimulation.","D":"Peptide hormones are synthesized directly from lipid precursors and released by passive diffusion, while steroid hormones require exocytosis for secretion into the bloodstream.","E":"Steroid hormones are stored in large intrafollicular colloid droplets bound to carrier proteins until proteolytic enzymes cleave them for release in response to trophic hormones."},"explanation":"Steroid hormones are derived from cholesterol and are highly lipophilic. Because they can freely diffuse across cell membranes immediately after synthesis, they are not stored in secretory vesicles. In contrast, peptide hormones are hydrophilic and cannot cross membranes; they are synthesized as preprohormones on the rough ER, processed through the Golgi apparatus, stored in secretory vesicles, and released by exocytosis. Storage in intrafollicular colloid bound to thyroglobulin describes thyroid hormones, which are amine derivatives rather than steroids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic trap — steroids are made from cholesterol and NOT stored; peptides are stored in secretory granules and released by exocytosis. Steroid-producing cells contain large cholesterol ester vacuoles for rapid on-demand synthesis. COMLEX: Steroid hormones have anti-inflammatory effects relevant to osteopathic prescribing; they suppress the HPA axis with prolonged use.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — three hormone classes: peptides are hydrophilic stored in vesicles, steroids are lipophilic synthesized from cholesterol, amines from tyrosine span both solubility profiles","isHighYield":false,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"In contrast, peptide hormones ","D":"In contrast, peptide hormones "}},{"id":"sdl1_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the three major hormone classes (peptides, steroids, and amines) and describe their chemical properties and solubility characteristics.","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student is studying amine hormones derived from tyrosine. Which of the following correctly distinguishes thyroid hormones from catecholamines with respect to their chemical properties and storage?","choices":{"A":"Thyroid hormones are lipophilic and stored in follicular colloid bound to thyroglobulin, while catecholamines are hydrophilic and stored in preformed chromaffin cell secretory vesicles.","B":"Catecholamines are lipophilic and diffuse freely across the plasma membrane to bind nuclear receptors, while thyroid hormones are hydrophilic and bind G protein-coupled receptors at the cell surface.","C":"Thyroid hormones are hydrophilic, circulate freely dissolved in plasma without carrier proteins, and act through membrane-bound tyrosine kinase receptors on target tissues.","D":"Both thyroid hormones and catecholamines are hydrophilic, circulate freely dissolved in plasma, and bind membrane-bound receptors on their respective target cells throughout the body.","E":"Both thyroid hormones and catecholamines are lipophilic, are synthesized from cholesterol precursors, and act through intracellular receptors to stimulate nuclear gene transcription."},"explanation":"Both thyroid hormones and catecholamines are tyrosine-derived amines, but they differ markedly in solubility and storage. Thyroid hormones are lipophilic (despite their charged amino acid residues) and are stored as thyroglobulin within follicular colloid; they circulate primarily bound to thyroxine-binding globulin. Catecholamines are hydrophilic, stored in preformed secretory vesicles in adrenal chromaffin cells, and released by exocytosis within seconds of stimulation. Catecholamines bind membrane-bound adrenergic receptors; thyroid hormones bind nuclear receptors like steroids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key exception — T3/T4 are technically amines from tyrosine but act like steroids via nuclear receptors. \"PET CAT in TV\" mnemonic includes T3/T4 as intracellular receptor hormones along with steroids. Catecholamines have half-life of 10–15 seconds versus days for protein-bound T3/T4. COMLEX: Adrenal medulla catecholamine release is triggered by splanchnic nerve stimulation, relevant to adrenal viscerosomatic reflexes: T8–T10 with sympathetic supply T7–T11 (OMM atlas Ch 15) vs sympathetic T10–L1 (Moore Ch 5; Foundations Ch 6).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — three hormone classes: peptides are hydrophilic stored in vesicles, steroids are lipophilic synthesized from cholesterol, amines from tyrosine span both solubility profiles","isHighYield":false,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Catecholamines bind membrane-b","C":"Thyroid hormones are lipophili","D":"Catecholamines bind membrane-b","E":"Catecholamines are hydrophilic"}},{"id":"sdl1_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the three major hormone classes (peptides, steroids, and amines) and describe their chemical properties and solubility characteristics.","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher studying hormone pharmacokinetics observes that cortisol has a plasma half-life of 60–90 minutes, while epinephrine has a half-life of only 10–15 seconds. Which of the following best explains why cortisol has a significantly longer plasma half-life?","choices":{"C":"Cortisol is a lipophilic steroid that is extensively bound to corticosteroid-binding globulin in the plasma, which acts as a circulating reservoir and slows renal and hepatic clearance.","A":"Cortisol is a hydrophilic molecule that dissolves freely in plasma and resists degradation because it lacks the tyrosine residues that enzymes in the blood use to metabolize catecholamines.","B":"Cortisol is stored in large cytoplasmic secretory granules within adrenocortical cells that slowly release stored hormone into the bloodstream to maintain stable plasma concentrations.","D":"Epinephrine is highly lipophilic and is therefore rapidly sequestered into adipose tissue from the circulation, shortening its effective plasma half-life compared to the water-soluble cortisol.","E":"Cortisol binds G protein-coupled receptors on hepatocytes that actively upregulate its own synthesis, maintaining plasma levels and effectively prolonging its measurable half-life in the circulation."},"explanation":"Steroid and thyroid hormones are lipophilic and circulate bound to specific plasma binding proteins, such as corticosteroid-binding globulin for cortisol and sex hormone-binding globulin for sex steroids. Protein binding prevents rapid renal filtration and enzymatic degradation, extending the half-life to 20–100 minutes for adrenal steroids or 1–6 days for thyroid hormones. Epinephrine is hydrophilic, circulates unbound, and is rapidly degraded by monoamine oxidase and catechol-O-methyltransferase in blood and tissues, giving it a half-life of only 10–15 seconds.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Protein-bound steroid = inactive reservoir; free steroid = active. Only free hormone is biologically active. Increased SHBG in males (e.g., cirrhosis) lowers free testosterone relative to estrogen, contributing to gynecomastia; decreased SHBG in females raises free testosterone (hirsutism). COMLEX: Protein binding is clinically relevant when prescribing estrogen-containing OCPs, which increase SHBG and can alter thyroid hormone levels by increasing TBG.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — three hormone classes: peptides are hydrophilic stored in vesicles, steroids are lipophilic synthesized from cholesterol, amines from tyrosine span both solubility profiles","isHighYield":false,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"D":"Epinephrine is hydrophilic, ci"}},{"id":"sdl1_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish between lipophilic and hydrophilic hormones based on their receptor locations and general mechanisms of action.","batch":1,"stem":"During a pharmacology lecture, a professor states that the cellular location of a hormone's receptor is determined primarily by the hormone's lipophilicity. Which of the following correctly matches a hormone to its receptor location based on this principle?","choices":{"D":"Testosterone, a lipophilic steroid, diffuses across the plasma membrane and binds intracellular receptors in the cytoplasm or nucleus to directly regulate gene transcription in target cells.","A":"Insulin, a hydrophilic peptide, crosses the plasma membrane by passive diffusion and binds intranuclear receptors to activate transcription programs that regulate glucose transport and glycogen synthesis.","B":"Cortisol, a lipophilic steroid, binds G protein-coupled receptors on the outer surface of the plasma membrane to activate adenylyl cyclase and generate intracellular cAMP as a second messenger.","C":"Epinephrine, a hydrophilic catecholamine, diffuses freely across the lipid bilayer and binds cytoplasmic steroid-like receptors that translocate to the nucleus to regulate sympathetic gene programs.","E":"Thyrotropin-releasing hormone, a water-soluble tripeptide, diffuses into cells and binds intranuclear receptors that directly stimulate DNA transcription to increase thyroid-stimulating hormone synthesis."},"explanation":"Lipophilic hormones can cross the plasma membrane and therefore bind intracellular receptors — cytoplasmic for steroids, nuclear for thyroid hormones. Hydrophilic hormones cannot cross the lipid bilayer and must bind cell-surface receptors. Testosterone is lipophilic, so it freely diffuses into target cells and binds the androgen receptor in the cytoplasm, which then translocates to the nucleus. Insulin is a hydrophilic peptide and therefore binds the insulin receptor tyrosine kinase on the cell surface. Epinephrine is hydrophilic and binds membrane-bound adrenergic receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"PET CAT in TV\" = intracellular receptor hormones: Progesterone, Estrogen, Testosterone, Cortisol, Aldosterone, T3/T4, Vitamin D. All others are membrane-bound. Receptor location determines mechanism of action — intracellular receptors alter gene transcription; membrane receptors activate second messengers. COMLEX: Understanding receptor location is foundational for prescribing corticosteroids; their intranuclear action explains both their delayed onset and their broad anti-inflammatory effects.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — lipophilic hormones cross membranes to bind intracellular receptors; hydrophilic hormones bind cell-surface receptors and use second messengers","isHighYield":false,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"E":"Hydrophilic hormones cannot cr"}},{"id":"sdl1_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish between lipophilic and hydrophilic hormones based on their receptor locations and general mechanisms of action.","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher adds ACTH to isolated adrenocortical cells in culture and measures a rapid, marked increase in intracellular cAMP within seconds. Which of the following best explains the receptor mechanism responsible for this rapid second messenger response?","choices":{"A":"ACTH is a lipophilic peptide that diffuses through the plasma membrane and binds a cytoplasmic receptor, which then translocates to the nucleus to activate cAMP-responsive binding elements on steroidogenic genes.","B":"ACTH is a hydrophilic peptide that binds a cell-surface G protein-coupled receptor, activating the Gs alpha subunit to stimulate adenylyl cyclase and rapidly generate cAMP within the cytoplasm.","C":"ACTH binds a nuclear receptor that directly activates transcription of the adenylyl cyclase gene, leading to increased intracellular cAMP production over a period of hours as new enzyme protein is synthesized.","D":"ACTH binds a receptor tyrosine kinase that undergoes dimerization and autophosphorylation, generating cAMP through the intrinsic phosphodiesterase activity of the phosphorylated cytoplasmic kinase domain.","E":"ACTH activates an enzyme-linked receptor that phosphorylates phospholipase C to cleave PIP2 into IP3 and DAG, with IP3 subsequently generating cAMP from AMP in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum."},"explanation":"ACTH is a 39-amino-acid peptide hormone and is therefore hydrophilic. It cannot cross the plasma membrane and binds the MC2R, a G protein-coupled receptor on the adrenocortical cell surface. Upon binding, the receptor activates a stimulatory G protein, whose alpha subunit activates adenylyl cyclase to convert ATP to cAMP within seconds. This cAMP activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates cholesterol ester hydrolase (cholesterol esterase) and StAR protein to initiate steroidogenesis. Nuclear receptors and gene transcription are mechanisms of steroid hormones, not peptide hormones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FLAT ChAMPs CHuGG — classic mnemonic for cAMP pathway hormones: FSH, LH, ACTH, TSH, CRH, hCG, ADH V2, MSH, PTH, Calcitonin, Histamine H2, Glucagon, GHRH. All are hydrophilic peptides using membrane GPCRs. COMLEX: ACTH is the link between the HPA axis and adrenal steroidogenesis; loss of ACTH stimulation leads to adrenal atrophy, relevant to managing patients on chronic exogenous steroids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — lipophilic hormones cross membranes to bind intracellular receptors; hydrophilic hormones bind cell-surface receptors and use second messengers","isHighYield":false,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Nuclear receptors and gene tra"}},{"id":"sdl1_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish between lipophilic and hydrophilic hormones based on their receptor locations and general mechanisms of action.","batch":1,"stem":"A physician notes that corticosteroid therapy for asthma typically requires several hours to produce anti-inflammatory effects, while inhaled epinephrine begins to relieve bronchospasm within seconds. Which of the following best explains the difference in onset of action between these two drugs?","choices":{"B":"Corticosteroids are lipophilic and diffuse into cells to bind intracellular glucocorticoid receptors, which alter gene transcription and require hours of new protein synthesis to produce physiologic effects.","A":"Epinephrine binds membrane-bound tyrosine kinase receptors that require prolonged receptor autophosphorylation and downstream MAPK cascade activation before producing clinically measurable bronchodilation.","C":"Corticosteroids are hydrophilic and require slow carrier-mediated transport across the bronchial epithelium to access cytoplasmic receptors, accounting for the delayed onset of their anti-inflammatory action.","D":"Epinephrine is lipophilic and rapidly crosses airway smooth muscle membranes to bind nuclear steroid receptors, producing immediate bronchodilation through direct transcriptional activation of relaxation genes.","E":"Corticosteroids bind cell-surface G protein-coupled receptors but require prolonged receptor internalization and retrograde nuclear signaling before activating anti-inflammatory gene suppression programs."},"explanation":"Corticosteroids are lipophilic steroids that diffuse into the cell and bind glucocorticoid receptors in the cytoplasm. The receptor-hormone complex translocates to the nucleus, binds glucocorticoid response elements, and suppresses transcription of pro-inflammatory genes such as those encoding cytokines and phospholipase A2. This gene transcription mechanism requires hours to produce measurable protein-level changes. In contrast, epinephrine is a hydrophilic catecholamine that binds beta-2 adrenergic receptors on the cell surface and activates adenylyl cyclase to generate cAMP within seconds, producing rapid bronchial smooth muscle relaxation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Steroids work by altering gene transcription — onset is hours, duration is days. Beta-agonists work via cAMP/GPCR — onset is seconds. This onset difference is high yield across multiple organ systems including asthma, autoimmune disease, and adrenal insufficiency. COMLEX: Glucocorticoids are also used in OMM-related inflammatory conditions; their delayed onset means bridging therapy with fast-acting agents is often necessary.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — lipophilic hormones cross membranes to bind intracellular receptors; hydrophilic hormones bind cell-surface receptors and use second messengers","isHighYield":false,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"In contrast, epinephrine is a "}},{"id":"sdl1_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the three major hormone classes (peptides, steroids, and amines) and describe their chemical properties and solubility characteristics.","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus has been taking prednisone 40 mg daily for 8 weeks but stopped it abruptly 3 days ago when her prescription ran out. She is brought to the emergency department after collapsing at work. Vital signs: BP 82/50 mmHg, HR 118 bpm, Temp 37.2°C. Random serum cortisol is undetectable. The attending physician suspects adrenal insufficiency secondary to exogenous steroid use. The mechanism by which prednisone suppresses endogenous cortisol production is most directly explained by which of the following?","choices":{"E":"Prednisone is a lipophilic steroid that diffuses across cell membranes and binds intracellular glucocorticoid receptors, which translocate to the nucleus and suppress CRH and ACTH gene transcription.","A":"Prednisone is a hydrophilic peptide that binds cell-surface receptors on anterior pituitary corticotrophs and activates inhibitory G proteins to acutely reduce adenylyl cyclase activity and ACTH release.","B":"Prednisone is a tyrosine-derived amine that binds nuclear receptors in hypothalamic neurons after facilitated transport into the cell, where it suppresses CRH gene expression through chromatin remodeling.","C":"Prednisone is stored in adrenal cortical secretory granules and provides direct feedback to the zona fasciculata to suppress further steroid synthesis via membrane-bound autocrine receptor signaling.","D":"Prednisone is a water-soluble molecule that binds G protein-coupled receptors on hypothalamic neurons and activates inhibitory Gi proteins to reduce intracellular cAMP and suppress CRH secretion."},"explanation":"Prednisone is converted to prednisolone, a potent synthetic glucocorticoid and lipophilic steroid hormone. Like all steroid hormones, it freely crosses the plasma membrane and binds cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptors. The hormone-receptor complex translocates to the nucleus and suppresses transcription of CRH in the hypothalamus and ACTH in the anterior pituitary, shutting down the HPA axis. With prolonged exogenous steroid use, the adrenal cortex atrophies due to absent ACTH stimulation. Abrupt cessation can precipitate adrenal crisis, as seen here, because the gland cannot produce sufficient cortisol in response to physiologic stress.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HPA suppression by exogenous steroids is a major board topic. Rule of thumb: corticosteroids given for more than 2 weeks can suppress the adrenal (Katzung Ch 39), so taper to allow HPA recovery. Adrenal crisis presents with hypotension and hyponatremia; hyperkalemia is absent in steroid-induced (secondary) AI because aldosterone remains RAAS-driven. Steroid mechanism = lipophilic → intracellular receptor → gene suppression. COMLEX: Adrenal insufficiency from steroid use is relevant in osteopathic practice given the use of corticosteroids for musculoskeletal inflammation; always taper and monitor for HPA axis suppression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — hormone receptor types: steroid class predicts intracellular glucocorticoid receptor; mechanism explains why all steroid effects involve altered gene transcription","isHighYield":false,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl1_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the three major hormone classes (peptides, steroids, and amines) and describe their chemical properties and solubility characteristics.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man presents to the emergency department with episodic severe headaches, diaphoresis, and palpitations. BP is 210/115 mmHg during an episode. A 24-hour urine collection shows markedly elevated metanephrines. CT abdomen reveals a 4 cm right adrenal mass. His intern asks why catecholamine excess causes such rapid and dramatic hemodynamic changes, while elevated cortisol in Cushing syndrome produces gradual metabolic changes over months. Which of the following best explains this difference?","choices":{"D":"Catecholamines are hydrophilic, pre-stored in chromaffin cell vesicles, and released by rapid exocytosis to bind cell-surface adrenergic receptors and activate second messengers within seconds of stimulation.","A":"Cortisol binds cell-surface receptors that require prolonged receptor internalization and nuclear transport before activating steroidogenic genes, adding a delay of hours before downstream proteins are synthesized.","B":"Catecholamines are lipophilic and therefore diffuse rapidly across plasma membranes to bind intranuclear receptors directly, while cortisol requires carrier-protein transport and has a slower cellular uptake rate.","C":"Cortisol acts through receptor tyrosine kinase pathways requiring multiple phosphorylation cascades and new ribosomal translation of downstream enzyme proteins to produce its gradual metabolic effects.","E":"Catecholamines are synthesized from cholesterol on demand and immediately diffuse into the circulation, while cortisol is stored in large secretory vesicles that release it slowly over hours in response to ACTH."},"explanation":"Catecholamines are tyrosine-derived amine hormones that are hydrophilic and pre-stored in chromaffin cell secretory vesicles. Upon stimulation of the adrenal medulla by splanchnic nerves, stored catecholamines are released by exocytosis within milliseconds and bind beta and alpha adrenergic G protein-coupled receptors on target cells, activating second messengers within seconds. Cortisol is a lipophilic steroid hormone that acts through intracellular glucocorticoid receptors and alters gene transcription; the resulting metabolic effects require new protein synthesis and accumulate over hours to days. Neither hormone uses receptor tyrosine kinase pathways.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pheochromocytoma produces episodic hypertension due to catecholamine bursts — classic triad of headache, diaphoresis, palpitations. Rule of 10s: 10% malignant, 10% bilateral, 10% extra-adrenal, 10% calcify, 10% in children; germline mutations (NF1, VHL, RET) are now known to be far more common than 10%. Pre-operatively alpha-block first, then beta-block. COMLEX: Adrenal medulla is innervated by preganglionic sympathetic fibers from T10–L1 via the thoracic splanchnic nerves (Moore Ch 5; the OMM atlas lists T7–T11); pheochromocytoma may produce viscerosomatic changes at these spinal levels.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — lipophilic vs hydrophilic receptor mechanisms: catecholamines use cell-surface GPCRs for rapid effects; cortisol uses intracellular receptors for delayed gene transcription effects","isHighYield":false,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Cortisol is a lipophilic stero","C":"Neither hormone uses receptor "}},{"id":"sdl1_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the three major hormone classes (peptides, steroids, and amines) and describe their chemical properties and solubility characteristics.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman is started on levothyroxine for primary hypothyroidism. Her endocrinologist explains that although thyroid hormones are chemically derived from the amino acid tyrosine, they differ fundamentally from catecholamines in their mechanism of action. Laboratory studies show that greater than 99% of circulating T4 is protein-bound. Which of the following best explains why thyroid hormones behave mechanistically more like steroid hormones than like other amine hormones?","choices":{"C":"Thyroid hormones are lipophilic despite their charged amino acid structure, circulate bound to thyroxine-binding globulin, and act through nuclear receptors to alter gene transcription, similar to steroid hormones.","A":"Thyroid hormones are packaged into secretory vesicles within follicular epithelial cells and released by exocytosis in response to TSH stimulation, similar to catecholamines in the adrenal medulla.","B":"Thyroid hormones are highly water-soluble, circulate freely dissolved in plasma without carrier proteins, and act through membrane-bound tyrosine kinase receptors to produce their metabolic effects.","D":"Thyroid hormones act through G protein-coupled receptors on the outer surface of target cell membranes, generating cAMP as a second messenger to produce their effects on basal metabolic rate.","E":"Thyroid hormones are synthesized directly from cholesterol precursors in thyroid follicular cells and therefore share the steroid biosynthetic pathway and cytoplasmic receptor signaling mechanisms."},"explanation":"Thyroid hormones are technically amine hormones derived from tyrosine, but they are uniquely lipophilic despite their charged residues. More than 99% circulates bound to thyroxine-binding globulin, albumin, and transthyretin, and only free hormone enters cells. Notably, T3 and T4 require specific membrane transporters for cellular entry rather than simple passive diffusion. Inside the cell, they bind nuclear thyroid hormone receptors and alter transcription of metabolic genes. This mechanism is analogous to that of steroid hormones. Catecholamines, in contrast, are hydrophilic amines that act through membrane-bound adrenergic receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T3/T4 are the only lipophilic hormones with charged amino acids — they need transporter proteins for cell entry, unlike steroids that freely diffuse. T3 binds nuclear receptor with 10x greater affinity than T4; most physiologic activity comes from T4-to-T3 conversion peripherally. TBG is increased by estrogen, pregnancy, OCPs; decreased in nephrotic syndrome, steroids, liver disease. COMLEX: Thyroid viscerosomatic findings at C4–C6 and T2 with sympathetic supply T1–T4 (OMM atlas Ch 15) vs the head-and-neck range T1–T5 (OMM course map) may reflect viscerosomatic changes from thyroid disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — hormone receptor types: thyroid hormones use nuclear receptors despite being amines, classifying them with the \"intracellular receptor\" group alongside steroids and Vitamin D","isHighYield":false,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"D":"Inside the cell, they bind nuc"}},{"id":"sdl1_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish between lipophilic and hydrophilic hormones based on their receptor locations and general mechanisms of action.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with a new diagnosis of systemic lupus erythematosus is started on methylprednisolone. Her rheumatologist explains that the drug works through a mechanism fundamentally different from that of NSAIDs, which block cyclooxygenase enzymes at the cell membrane. Methylprednisolone is found to suppress transcription of genes encoding interleukin-1, interleukin-6, and phospholipase A2 in activated macrophages. Which of the following best describes the receptor mechanism responsible for this effect?","choices":{"E":"Methylprednisolone is lipophilic and diffuses freely across the plasma membrane to bind cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptors, which translocate to the nucleus and suppress transcription of pro-inflammatory cytokine genes.","A":"Methylprednisolone is a hydrophilic molecule that binds cell-surface G protein-coupled glucocorticoid receptors and activates inhibitory Gi proteins to reduce cAMP production and downstream NF-kB activation.","B":"Methylprednisolone binds membrane-bound receptor tyrosine kinases on macrophage surfaces, inducing receptor dimerization and autophosphorylation that activates the JAK-STAT pathway to suppress cytokine gene transcription.","C":"Methylprednisolone is stored in adrenal cortical secretory granules and released into the circulation where it binds soluble plasma proteins that carry it to membrane-bound mineralocorticoid receptors on immune cells.","D":"Methylprednisolone activates adenylyl cyclase through a stimulatory G protein to increase cAMP, which activates protein kinase A to phosphorylate and inactivate NF-kB transcription factor in macrophages."},"explanation":"Methylprednisolone is a synthetic glucocorticoid and therefore lipophilic, like all steroid hormones. It freely diffuses across the plasma membrane and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor. The receptor undergoes conformational change, dissociates from heat-shock proteins, and the hormone-receptor complex translocates into the nucleus. There, it binds glucocorticoid response elements and suppresses transcription of pro-inflammatory genes including cytokines and phospholipase A2. This is mechanistically distinct from NSAIDs, which act on cyclooxygenase at the membrane level. The gene transcription mechanism explains both the delayed onset and the broad anti-inflammatory spectrum of corticosteroids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucocorticoid mechanism = lipophilic steroid → cytoplasmic receptor → nuclear translocation → suppress NF-kB, decrease cytokine transcription, decrease phospholipase A2 (blocks arachidonic acid cascade upstream of both COX and LOX). FA: Cortisol is A BIG FIB — ↑Appetite, ↑Blood pressure, ↑Insulin resistance, ↑Gluconeogenesis, ↓Fibroblast activity, ↓Inflammatory/Immune responses, ↓Bone formation. COMLEX: In osteopathic practice, corticosteroids are used for musculoskeletal inflammation; understanding their receptor mechanism reinforces why they require tapering and why abrupt cessation causes adrenal insufficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — GPCR signaling and second messenger systems: contrasts with membrane receptor mechanisms used by hydrophilic hormones such as ACTH, which activates cAMP via GPCR to stimulate steroidogenesis","isHighYield":false,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl1_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish between lipophilic and hydrophilic hormones based on their receptor locations and general mechanisms of action.","batch":2,"stem":"A research scientist adds insulin and dexamethasone separately to isolated hepatocytes in culture. Insulin produces a measurable increase in glucose uptake and glycogen synthesis within 5 minutes. Dexamethasone produces increased expression of gluconeogenic enzymes, but only after 6–8 hours. Which of the following best explains the fundamental difference in onset of action between these two hormones?","choices":{"A":"Insulin binds cell-surface receptor tyrosine kinases that trigger rapid signaling and GLUT4 translocation, while dexamethasone must diffuse into cells to bind nuclear receptors and induce new protein synthesis.","B":"Insulin acts through cytoplasmic nuclear receptors to rapidly activate pre-stored glucokinase protein, while dexamethasone binds G protein-coupled receptors requiring prolonged second messenger cascades for enzyme induction.","C":"Insulin is lipophilic and diffuses across the hepatocyte membrane to directly phosphorylate glycogen synthase, while dexamethasone is hydrophilic and requires receptor-mediated endocytosis before reaching its nuclear target.","D":"Insulin is a lipophilic peptide that activates nuclear receptors to transcribe new GLUT4 gene copies, while dexamethasone is hydrophilic and uses membrane receptors to activate rapid adenylyl cyclase pathways.","E":"Insulin and dexamethasone both bind membrane-bound receptors but differ only in their downstream G protein coupling, with insulin activating stimulatory pathways and dexamethasone activating inhibitory pathways."},"explanation":"Insulin is a hydrophilic peptide and cannot cross the plasma membrane, so it binds the insulin receptor tyrosine kinase on the cell surface. Activation triggers rapid downstream signaling through the PI3K-AKT pathway, including translocation of GLUT4 transporter vesicles to the plasma membrane within minutes. Dexamethasone is a lipophilic synthetic glucocorticoid that diffuses into the cell, binds cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptors, and the complex translocates to the nucleus to increase transcription of gluconeogenic enzyme genes such as PEPCK and glucose-6-phosphatase. New mRNA must be translated into new enzyme protein before the effect is measurable, requiring 6–8 hours.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Insulin receptor is a receptor tyrosine kinase — the only major metabolic hormone that uses RTK signaling along with IGF-1. Key downstream effect is GLUT4 translocation to skeletal muscle and adipose membranes. Dexamethasone increases glucose by inducing gluconeogenic enzymes — opposite direction to insulin. This antagonism is why steroids cause steroid-induced hyperglycemia. COMLEX: Steroid-induced hyperglycemia is a common complication in patients receiving corticosteroid injections for musculoskeletal conditions; monitoring blood glucose is important in diabetic patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — tyrosine kinase receptor mechanism: insulin receptor dimerization and autophosphorylation contrasts with the nuclear receptor mechanism used by lipophilic dexamethasone","isHighYield":false,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"E":"Dexamethasone is a lipophilic "}},{"id":"sdl1_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish between lipophilic and hydrophilic hormones based on their receptor locations and general mechanisms of action.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with estrogen receptor-positive metastatic breast cancer is switched from tamoxifen to fulvestrant, a selective estrogen receptor degrader. Her oncologist explains that estrogen drives tumor proliferation through a mechanism that directly alters gene expression in breast epithelial cells. Which of the following best describes the normal mechanism by which estrogen exerts its proliferative effect in breast tissue?","choices":{"D":"Estrogen is a lipophilic steroid hormone that diffuses freely across the plasma membrane and binds intranuclear estrogen receptors, which activate estrogen-responsive elements on DNA to drive transcription of proliferation genes.","A":"Estrogen is a hydrophilic amine hormone that binds G protein-coupled receptors on the outer surface of breast epithelial cells and activates cAMP to drive cell cycle entry and proliferation through PKA.","B":"Estrogen binds membrane-bound receptor tyrosine kinases on breast ductal cells, inducing dimerization and autophosphorylation that activates the MAPK pathway to upregulate proliferative gene expression.","C":"Estrogen is a peptide hormone derived from amino acid precursors stored in ovarian secretory vesicles and released by exocytosis to bind membrane-bound receptors on estrogen-responsive breast tissue.","E":"Estrogen binds JAK-associated cytokine receptors on breast epithelial cells; activated JAK kinases phosphorylate STAT proteins that enter the nucleus to activate estrogen-responsive proliferation elements."},"explanation":"Estrogen is a steroid hormone derived from cholesterol and is therefore lipophilic. It diffuses freely across the plasma membrane and binds estrogen receptor alpha or beta, which are found in the cytoplasm or nucleus depending on the cell type. The hormone-receptor complex binds estrogen response elements and activates transcription of genes driving cell proliferation, including cyclin D1. Fulvestrant binds the estrogen receptor with high affinity and targets it for proteasomal degradation, eliminating estrogen signaling entirely. Tamoxifen is a partial antagonist that blocks estrogen binding at breast tissue receptors. Neither cAMP nor JAK-STAT pathways are the primary mechanism of estrogen action in breast tissue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"PET CAT in TV\" includes Estrogen as an intracellular receptor hormone. ER-positive breast cancer depends on nuclear estrogen receptor signaling; tamoxifen blocks it as a competitive antagonist, while fulvestrant degrades it. Aromatase inhibitors block peripheral estrogen synthesis. COMLEX: Phytoestrogens in soy have weak estrogenic activity through the same nuclear receptor mechanism; relevant in patients asking about dietary alternatives to hormone therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — hormone classification: estrogen is a steroid hormone derived from cholesterol; its lipophilic nature determines both its transport bound to SHBG and its intranuclear receptor mechanism","isHighYield":false,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Neither cAMP nor JAK-STAT path","E":"Neither cAMP nor JAK-STAT path"}}],"2":[{"id":"sdl2_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Match major endocrine hormones to their corresponding receptor types, including G protein-coupled receptors, tyrosine kinase receptors, and nuclear receptors.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following hormones mediates its intracellular effects primarily through a cell-surface receptor with intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity that undergoes autophosphorylation upon ligand binding?","choices":{"B":"Insulin binds a heterotetrameric receptor with intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity in its intracellular domain; binding triggers autophosphorylation of tyrosine residues, initiating downstream PI3K and MAPK signaling cascades.","A":"Cortisol binds a cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor that translocates to the nucleus and binds hormone response elements on DNA to regulate gene transcription directly without involving membrane-bound receptor tyrosine kinase activity.","C":"TSH binds a seven-transmembrane G protein-coupled receptor on thyroid follicular cells and activates Gs protein to stimulate adenylyl cyclase, raising intracellular cAMP and driving thyroid hormone synthesis via protein kinase A.","D":"Testosterone crosses the cell membrane and binds an intracellular androgen receptor in the cytoplasm; the hormone-receptor complex translocates to the nucleus and binds androgen response elements to alter target gene transcription.","E":"Oxytocin binds a seven-transmembrane Gq-coupled receptor on uterine myometrial cells and activates phospholipase C, generating IP3 and DAG to raise intracellular calcium and activate protein kinase C."},"explanation":"Insulin is the prototypical receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK) hormone. Its receptor is a heterotetrameric protein (2α + 2β subunits) with extracellular α subunits that bind insulin and intracellular β subunits containing tyrosine kinase domains. Binding triggers autophosphorylation, activating PI3K→Akt (metabolic effects) and RAS→MAPK (growth effects). Cortisol, testosterone, and aldosterone all use intracellular nuclear receptors. TSH and oxytocin both use GPCRs (Gs and Gq, respectively). First Aid mnemonic for RTKs: \"Get Found In the MAP\" — IGF-1, FGF, PDGF, EGF, Insulin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the four receptor classes and their hormone assignments. Insulin/IGF-1/PDGF/EGF/FGF = RTKs. Classic USMLE trap: insulin is a peptide (hydrophilic) but does NOT use a GPCR — it uses an RTK. COMLEX: insulin resistance in T2DM occurs at the RTK level, impairing downstream glucose uptake; viscerosomatic reflex for pancreas at T5–T9, alternate T6–T10 (OMM course map) vs reflex T5–T9 with sympathetic T5–T11 (OMM atlas Ch 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — three receptor classes: GPCRs (FLAT ChAMPs CHuGG use cAMP; GOAT HAG use IP3), receptor tyrosine kinases (insulin, IGF-1), and nuclear/intracellular receptors (PET CAT in TV); JAK-STAT for GH and cytokines","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Cortisol, testosterone, and al","C":"TSH and oxytocin both use GPCR","D":"Cortisol, testosterone, and al","E":"TSH and oxytocin both use GPCR"}},{"id":"sdl2_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Match major endocrine hormones to their corresponding receptor types, including G protein-coupled receptors, tyrosine kinase receptors, and nuclear receptors.","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher studying adrenocortical signaling observes that a particular hormone diffuses across the plasma membrane, binds a cytoplasmic receptor protein, and the resulting hormone-receptor complex translocates to the nucleus where it binds a specific DNA sequence to regulate gene transcription. Which hormone most likely demonstrates this mechanism?","choices":{"D":"ACTH is a hydrophilic peptide hormone that binds the melanocortin 2 receptor (MC2R), a Gs-coupled GPCR on adrenocortical cells, and stimulates cAMP production to promote cortisol synthesis without entering the cell directly.","A":"PTH is a hydrophilic peptide hormone that cannot cross the plasma membrane and instead binds a cell-surface Gs-coupled GPCR on osteoblasts and renal tubular cells, raising intracellular cAMP to mediate its effects on calcium and phosphate homeostasis.","B":"Glucagon is a hydrophilic peptide hormone that binds a seven-transmembrane Gs-coupled receptor on hepatocytes and activates adenylyl cyclase to increase cAMP, stimulating glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis via protein kinase A.","C":"Epinephrine is an amine hormone that acts primarily through cell-surface adrenergic receptors (beta-1 and beta-2 = Gs; alpha-1 = Gq) on the plasma membrane and cannot cross the lipid bilayer to bind intracellular receptors.","E":"Cortisol is a lipophilic steroid hormone that diffuses across cell membranes and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor; the complex translocates to the nucleus and binds glucocorticoid response elements to regulate gene transcription."},"explanation":"Nuclear/intracellular receptors are used exclusively by lipophilic hormones — steroids (cortisol, aldosterone, testosterone, estrogen, progesterone), thyroid hormones (T3/T4), and vitamin D. First Aid mnemonic: \"PET CAT in TV\" — Progesterone, Estrogen, Testosterone, Cortisol, Aldosterone, T3/T4, Vitamin D. The activated receptor complex binds a hormone response element (HRE) on DNA, altering transcription of specific genes. PTH, glucagon, ACTH, and epinephrine are all hydrophilic hormones that use membrane-bound GPCRs and cannot penetrate the lipid bilayer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PET CAT in TV is the HY mnemonic for nuclear receptor hormones. Board trap: thyroid hormones (T3/T4) use nuclear receptors despite being amines derived from tyrosine — they are iodinated and lipophilic, behaving like steroids. COMLEX: nuclear receptor activation explains why glucocorticoids cause delayed therapeutic effects (hours to days), a key pharmacologic principle in steroid therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — three receptor classes: GPCRs (FLAT ChAMPs CHuGG use cAMP; GOAT HAG use IP3), receptor tyrosine kinases (insulin, IGF-1), and nuclear/intracellular receptors (PET CAT in TV); JAK-STAT for GH and cytokines","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"PTH, glucagon, ACTH, and epine","B":"PTH, glucagon, ACTH, and epine","D":"PTH, glucagon, ACTH, and epine"}},{"id":"sdl2_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Match major endocrine hormones to their corresponding receptor types, including G protein-coupled receptors, tyrosine kinase receptors, and nuclear receptors.","batch":1,"stem":"The leptin receptor lacks intrinsic enzymatic activity but activates associated intracellular kinases upon ligand-induced dimerization. Which of the following hormones uses this same class of receptor signaling mechanism — activation of associated non-receptor tyrosine kinases rather than intrinsic kinase activity?","choices":{"E":"Growth hormone (GH) binds a homodimeric receptor lacking intrinsic kinase activity and activates associated JAK2 kinases; JAK2 phosphorylates STAT5 proteins, which dimerize and translocate to the nucleus to regulate transcription of IGF-1 and other GH-target genes.","A":"Insulin binds a heterotetrameric receptor (2α + 2β subunits) that contains intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity within its intracellular beta subunit domains; ligand binding causes autophosphorylation of tyrosine residues and directly activates downstream PI3K and MAPK signaling.","B":"Estrogen diffuses across the cell membrane and binds an intracellular receptor that translocates to the nucleus; the hormone-receptor complex binds estrogen response elements on DNA and regulates transcription of target genes directly without involving tyrosine kinase pathways.","C":"ACTH binds a seven-transmembrane Gs-coupled GPCR (MC2R) on adrenocortical cells and activates adenylyl cyclase to increase cAMP and stimulate steroidogenesis via protein kinase A, without involving any tyrosine kinase mechanism.","D":"TSH binds a seven-transmembrane Gs-coupled GPCR on thyroid follicular cells, activating adenylyl cyclase to raise cAMP, which then stimulates thyroid hormone synthesis and release via protein kinase A phosphorylation of downstream targets."},"explanation":"Non-receptor (associated) tyrosine kinases include the JAK/STAT pathway used by GH, prolactin, and cytokines (IL-2, IL-6, IFN, G-CSF, erythropoietin, thrombopoietin). First Aid mnemonic: \"GET a JAKed PIG\" — G-CSF, Erythropoietin, Thrombopoietin, Prolactin, Immunomodulators, GH. These receptors are homodimers (like leptin and GH receptors) that lack intrinsic kinase activity but recruit JAK2 after dimerization. In contrast, insulin/IGF-1/EGF/PDGF/FGF use RTKs with intrinsic kinase activity. Steroid hormones and thyroid hormones use nuclear receptors. ACTH and TSH use GPCRs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguish RTKs (insulin, IGF-1, EGF, PDGF, FGF — \"Get Found In the MAP\") from JAK-STAT (GH, prolactin, cytokines — \"GET a JAKed PIG\"). COMLEX: GH deficiency and excess (gigantism/acromegaly) are CP3 named conditions; understanding JAK-STAT helps explain why GH stimulates IGF-1 production in the liver hours after secretion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — three receptor classes: GPCRs (FLAT ChAMPs CHuGG use cAMP; GOAT HAG use IP3), receptor tyrosine kinases (insulin, IGF-1), and nuclear/intracellular receptors (PET CAT in TV); JAK-STAT for GH and cytokines","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"In contrast, insulin/IGF-1/EGF","B":"Steroid hormones and thyroid h","C":"ACTH and TSH use GPCRs.","D":"ACTH and TSH use GPCRs."}},{"id":"sdl2_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain G protein-coupled receptor signaling pathways and describe the role of second messenger systems including cAMP, IP3, and DAG.","batch":1,"stem":"ADH binding to V2 receptors in the renal collecting duct raises intracellular cAMP, ultimately inserting aquaporin-2 channels and increasing water reabsorption. Which of the following correctly describes the sequence of molecular events downstream of V2 receptor activation by ADH?","choices":{"A":"V2 receptor activation couples to Gs; Gs-alpha exchanges GDP for GTP and activates adenylyl cyclase to convert ATP to cAMP; cAMP activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates aquaporin-2 vesicles and drives their insertion into the apical membrane.","B":"V2 receptor activation couples to Gq protein; the Gq-alpha subunit activates phospholipase C, which cleaves PIP2 into IP3 and DAG; IP3 triggers Ca2+ release from the endoplasmic reticulum, and DAG activates protein kinase C to mediate aquaporin-2 insertion.","C":"V2 receptor activation couples to Gi protein; the Gi-alpha subunit inhibits adenylyl cyclase, reducing cAMP and decreasing protein kinase A activity, ultimately causing aquaporin-2 channels to be withdrawn from the apical membrane.","D":"V2 receptor activation triggers receptor dimerization and intrinsic tyrosine kinase autophosphorylation, activating the PI3K-Akt pathway that phosphorylates aquaporin-2-containing vesicles and promotes their fusion with the apical membrane.","E":"V2 receptor activation allows ADH to enter the cell and bind an intracellular glucocorticoid receptor homolog; the hormone-receptor complex translocates to the nucleus and increases transcription of aquaporin-2 genes to raise channel expression."},"explanation":"ADH (vasopressin) V2 receptors in the renal collecting duct are Gs-coupled GPCRs. Gs activation → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA → phosphorylation of aquaporin-2 vesicles → apical membrane insertion → water reabsorption. ADH also has a V1 receptor (on vascular smooth muscle) that is Gq-coupled → IP3/DAG → Ca2+ → vasoconstriction. This V1 vs V2 distinction (Gq vs Gs) is a classic board discriminator. FLAT ChAMPs CHuGG includes ADH (V2 receptor) in the cAMP group.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ADH has two receptor subtypes with opposite G proteins — V1 (vascular, Gq → IP3 → vasoconstriction) and V2 (renal, Gs → cAMP → aquaporin-2 insertion). Classic USMLE trap: lithium causes nephrogenic DI by blocking cAMP signaling downstream of V2 receptors. COMLEX: diabetes insipidus (central vs nephrogenic) is a CP3 named condition; central DI responds to desmopressin (V2 agonist) while nephrogenic DI does not.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — GPCR cascade: Gs → adenylyl cyclase → ATP→cAMP → PKA; Gq → phospholipase C → PIP2 → IP3 + DAG; Gi inhibits adenylyl cyclase and reduces cAMP","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl2_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain G protein-coupled receptor signaling pathways and describe the role of second messenger systems including cAMP, IP3, and DAG.","batch":1,"stem":"GnRH released from the hypothalamus binds its receptor on anterior pituitary gonadotrophs, triggering a second messenger cascade that leads to FSH and LH secretion. Which of the following best describes the intracellular signaling pathway activated by GnRH receptor binding?","choices":{"C":"GnRH receptor couples to Gq protein; Gq-alpha activates phospholipase C, which cleaves membrane PIP2 into IP3 and DAG; IP3 triggers Ca2+ release from the endoplasmic reticulum and DAG activates protein kinase C, together stimulating FSH and LH vesicle exocytosis.","A":"GnRH receptor couples to Gs protein; Gs-alpha activates adenylyl cyclase to convert ATP to cAMP, which activates protein kinase A; PKA phosphorylates key proteins in gonadotrophs to stimulate FSH and LH synthesis and secretion in a process requiring 30 to 45 minutes.","B":"GnRH receptor couples to Gi protein; Gi-alpha inhibits adenylyl cyclase, reducing intracellular cAMP and allowing a disinhibited calcium release channel in the ER membrane to open spontaneously and trigger exocytosis of FSH and LH granules.","D":"GnRH receptor undergoes dimerization and activates intrinsic tyrosine kinase domains in its intracellular region; tyrosine autophosphorylation recruits IRS-1 adapter proteins and activates MAPK, which translocates to the nucleus to increase FSH and LH gene transcription.","E":"GnRH crosses the portal blood and acts directly on gonadotrophs as a lipophilic molecule, binding an intracellular receptor that translocates to the nucleus and binds a hormone response element on the FSH and LH gene promoters."},"explanation":"GnRH uses the Gq/IP3/DAG pathway (not Gs/cAMP). The IP3/DAG system arises from Gq-alpha activating phospholipase C, which cleaves PIP2 → IP3 + DAG. IP3 releases Ca2+ from ER (mobilizes intracellular stores), and DAG activates protein kinase C at the plasma membrane. First Aid mnemonic \"GOAT HAG\" covers Gq-using hormones: GnRH, Oxytocin, ADH (V1), TRH, Histamine (H1), Angiotensin II, Gastrin. This is the opposite of the Gs/cAMP \"FLAT ChAMPs CHuGG\" group.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memorize both GPCR mnemonics: Gs/cAMP = \"FLAT ChAMPs CHuGG\" and Gq/IP3 = \"GOAT HAG.\" Board trap: GnRH itself uses Gq, but the downstream LH/FSH release stimulates gonads via Gs/cAMP receptors — two different G proteins in one axis. COMLEX: pulsatile GnRH stimulates; continuous GnRH (GnRH analogs like leuprolide) paradoxically suppresses LH/FSH after initial stimulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — GPCR cascade: Gs → adenylyl cyclase → ATP→cAMP → PKA; Gq → phospholipase C → PIP2 → IP3 + DAG; Gi inhibits adenylyl cyclase and reduces cAMP","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl2_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain G protein-coupled receptor signaling pathways and describe the role of second messenger systems including cAMP, IP3, and DAG.","batch":1,"stem":"Somatostatin inhibits secretion of both growth hormone and TSH by acting on hypothalamic and pituitary cells. Its mechanism involves reducing intracellular cAMP in the target cells. Which G protein subtype mediates somatostatin's inhibitory effect on adenylyl cyclase?","choices":{"B":"Gi (inhibitory G protein) couples to the somatostatin receptor; upon activation, the Gi-alpha subunit exchanges GDP for GTP and directly inhibits adenylyl cyclase, reducing conversion of ATP to cAMP and thereby decreasing protein kinase A activity and hormone secretion.","A":"Gs (stimulatory G protein) couples to the somatostatin receptor; upon activation, the Gs-alpha subunit exchanges GDP for GTP and activates adenylyl cyclase, but the resulting cAMP increase is limited by concomitant phosphodiesterase activation that rapidly degrades cAMP.","C":"Gq (phospholipase C-activating G protein) couples to the somatostatin receptor; Gq-alpha activates phospholipase C to generate IP3 and DAG, and the resulting calcium release from the ER causes a negative feedback loop that suppresses adenylyl cyclase.","D":"G12/13 protein couples to the somatostatin receptor; the active G12/13-alpha subunit activates RhoGEF and Rho GTPase, indirectly suppressing adenylyl cyclase through actin cytoskeleton rearrangements that reduce membrane accessibility of the enzyme.","E":"G-beta/gamma subunits are released when the somatostatin receptor activates its G protein, and these free beta/gamma dimers directly sequester adenylyl cyclase and prevent its activation regardless of whether the alpha subunit is inhibitory or stimulatory."},"explanation":"Gi (inhibitory G protein) is the G protein subtype that inhibits adenylyl cyclase and reduces intracellular cAMP. Hormones and neurotransmitters using Gi include somatostatin, M2 muscarinic receptors, alpha-2 adrenergic receptors, and D2 dopamine receptors. In contrast, Gs activates adenylyl cyclase (FLAT ChAMPs CHuGG), and Gq activates phospholipase C (GOAT HAG). Note that Gi's mechanism is important in pharmacology: opioids (mu receptors), SST analogs (octreotide), and others clinically exploit Gi coupling to reduce cAMP-driven secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know Gs (cAMP up), Gi (cAMP down), Gq (IP3/DAG up). Key Gi examples: somatostatin, M2/M4 receptors, alpha-2 adrenergic, D2 dopamine, opioids. Octreotide (somatostatin analog) treats acromegaly by reducing GH secretion via Gi. COMLEX: somatostatin's Gi mechanism is also the basis for its inhibition of pancreatic exocrine secretion, relevant to COMLEX GI-endocrine integration questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — GPCR cascade: Gs → adenylyl cyclase → ATP→cAMP → PKA; Gq → phospholipase C → PIP2 → IP3 + DAG; Gi inhibits adenylyl cyclase and reduces cAMP","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl2_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Match major endocrine hormones to their corresponding receptor types, including G protein-coupled receptors, tyrosine kinase receptors, and nuclear receptors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with a BMI of 34 kg/m² and fasting blood glucose of 218 mg/dL is diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus. His physician explains that despite normal or elevated insulin levels, his cells are unable to respond appropriately. Downstream signaling studies show impaired phosphorylation of the insulin receptor substrate-1 (IRS-1). Which of the following correctly describes the primary mechanism by which insulin normally mediates its intracellular effects?","choices":{"D":"Insulin binds extracellular alpha subunits of its heterotetrameric receptor, inducing a conformational change that activates intrinsic tyrosine kinase domains in the intracellular beta subunits; the beta subunits cross-phosphorylate each other on tyrosine residues, recruiting IRS-1 adapter proteins and initiating downstream PI3K-Akt and MAPK signaling cascades.","A":"Insulin binds a seven-transmembrane Gs-coupled GPCR on adipocytes and skeletal muscle cells, activating adenylyl cyclase to increase intracellular cAMP; elevated cAMP activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates key metabolic enzymes to promote glucose uptake through GLUT4 translocation.","B":"Insulin crosses the plasma membrane due to its small size and lipophilic modifications, binds an intracellular receptor in the cytoplasm, and the hormone-receptor complex translocates to the nucleus to activate transcription of GLUT4 and glycogen synthase genes.","C":"Insulin binds a homodimeric receptor that lacks intrinsic kinase activity; ligand binding recruits JAK2 kinases that phosphorylate tyrosine residues on associated STAT5 proteins, which dimerize and translocate to the nucleus to regulate glucose metabolism gene transcription.","E":"Insulin binds a Gq-coupled seven-transmembrane receptor on hepatocytes, activating phospholipase C to generate IP3 and DAG; IP3-mediated Ca2+ release activates calmodulin-dependent kinases that promote glycogen synthesis and inhibit gluconeogenesis."},"explanation":"Insulin's receptor is a heterotetrameric RTK consisting of 2α and 2β subunits linked by disulfide bonds. Insulin binds the extracellular α subunits, causing a conformational change that activates intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity in the cytoplasmic β subunits. Trans-autophosphorylation on tyrosine residues recruits IRS-1, activating PI3K→PDK1→Akt (promotes GLUT4 translocation, glycogen synthesis, protein synthesis) and RAS→MAPK (promotes cell growth). In T2DM, post-receptor signaling defects — particularly at IRS-1 and PI3K — impair glucose uptake despite adequate insulin binding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Insulin is the #1 RTK hormone to know. T2DM involves post-receptor insulin resistance at IRS-1/PI3K level, not reduced insulin production. RTK mnemonic: \"Get Found In the MAP\" = IGF-1, FGF, PDGF (VEGF), EGF, Insulin. COMLEX: insulin resistance is associated with obesity (BMI > 30) and visceral adiposity; COMLEX may ask about OMM treatment for metabolic syndrome and parasympathetic tone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — tyrosine kinase receptor mechanism: dimerization and autophosphorylation cascade","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl2_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Match major endocrine hormones to their corresponding receptor types, including G protein-coupled receptors, tyrosine kinase receptors, and nuclear receptors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus is started on oral prednisone 40 mg daily. Her rheumatologist advises her that significant anti-inflammatory benefit typically begins only after a delay of hours and becomes maximal over several days. A hospital nurse asks the pharmacist why the effect is not immediate. Which of the following best accounts for the delayed onset of prednisone's therapeutic action?","choices":{"E":"Prednisone is a prodrug that requires sequential activation by intestinal, hepatic, and renal enzymes over many hours before the active metabolite reaches sufficient plasma concentrations to occupy more than 50% of peripheral glucocorticoid receptors and exert measurable effects.","A":"Prednisone must first be absorbed from the GI tract, distributed to target tissues, and activate surface Gs-coupled GPCRs; the resulting cAMP accumulation requires multiple rounds of protein kinase A amplification before anti-inflammatory gene expression changes become measurable, accounting for the delay of many hours.","B":"Prednisone binds Gq-coupled receptors on immune cells, activating phospholipase C to generate IP3 and DAG; however, IP3-mediated Ca2+ signals are transient, so sustained protein kinase C activation requires repeated receptor occupancy over many hours before anti-inflammatory effects accumulate.","C":"Prednisone activates receptor tyrosine kinases on lymphocytes; the downstream PI3K-Akt cascade must phosphorylate a large pool of NF-kB inhibitory proteins before anti-inflammatory transcription factor suppression is effective, requiring a prolonged induction period.","D":"Prednisone is a lipophilic steroid that is converted to prednisolone by hepatic 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase; prednisolone freely diffuses across cell membranes and binds intracellular glucocorticoid receptors in the cytoplasm; the activated hormone-receptor complex translocates to the nucleus and binds glucocorticoid response elements, altering transcription of inflammatory mediator genes, a process requiring hours to days to produce new or reduced proteins."},"explanation":"Glucocorticoids (cortisol, prednisone/prednisolone) are lipophilic steroids that act through intracellular nuclear receptors — this is the mechanistic basis for their delayed onset. After nuclear receptor binding and translocation, the hormone-receptor complex alters transcription: it decreases expression of pro-inflammatory genes (COX-2, phospholipase A2, cytokines IL-1/IL-6/TNF) and increases expression of anti-inflammatory proteins (lipocortin/annexin A1). This new gene transcription and translation requires hours to days, in marked contrast to the rapid (minutes) effects of peptide hormones and catecholamines acting via membrane GPCRs. Prednisone is a prodrug converted to prednisolone, but the primary reason for delayed onset is the nuclear receptor mechanism, not absorption kinetics.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nuclear receptor mechanism = delayed onset (hours to days) because new protein synthesis is required. Contrast with GPCR/second messenger hormones that act in minutes. Classic board trap: glucocorticoids have delayed anti-inflammatory effects but can have rapid non-genomic effects (membrane stabilization, ion flux changes) that are less clinically significant. COMLEX: long-term steroid use suppresses HPA axis via negative feedback → adrenal atrophy → risk of adrenal crisis on abrupt discontinuation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — lipophilic hormones bind intracellular receptors; onset determined by nuclear transcription, not second messenger speed","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"E":"Prednisone is a prodrug conver"}},{"id":"sdl2_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Match major endocrine hormones to their corresponding receptor types, including G protein-coupled receptors, tyrosine kinase receptors, and nuclear receptors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl is evaluated for breast development and menstrual bleeding. Physical examination reveals café-au-lait macules with irregular (\"coast of Maine\") borders and polyostotic fibrous dysplasia on skeletal survey. Genetic testing identifies a somatic gain-of-function mutation in the GNAS1 gene encoding Gs-alpha protein. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which this mutation leads to autonomous hormone overproduction across multiple endocrine glands?","choices":{"C":"The activating GNAS1 point mutation impairs the intrinsic GTPase activity of the Gs-alpha subunit; without GTP hydrolysis, Gs-alpha stays GTP-bound and constitutively stimulates adenylyl cyclase, driving excess cAMP production in the affected endocrine cells independent of any trophic hormone binding.","A":"The GNAS1 mutation creates a constitutively active Gq-alpha subunit that permanently stimulates phospholipase C across all endocrine cell types; excess IP3 and DAG production drives autonomous hormone secretion from the ovary, adrenal, thyroid, and pituitary gland simultaneously without ligand-receptor interaction.","B":"The mutant Gs-alpha protein translocates to the nucleus and directly activates hormone response elements on DNA, bypassing membrane receptor signaling entirely; this allows autonomous transcription of steroidogenesis genes in the ovary and adrenal cortex without the need for LH, FSH, or ACTH receptor activation.","D":"The GNAS1 mutation produces a constitutively active receptor tyrosine kinase by fusing Gs-alpha to the intracellular domain of a growth factor receptor; constitutive RTK autophosphorylation activates PI3K-Akt signaling in multiple endocrine cells, driving autonomous hormone synthesis and bone matrix replacement with fibrous tissue.","E":"The mutation prevents Gs-alpha from dissociating from the beta-gamma subunit complex; this locks the heterotrimer in an inactive state but paradoxically activates the receptor by causing prolonged receptor occupancy, which is misinterpreted by the cell as sustained hormonal stimulation."},"explanation":"McCune-Albright syndrome is caused by a somatic gain-of-function mutation in GNAS1 that impairs the intrinsic GTPase activity of Gs-alpha. Normally, Gs-alpha hydrolyzes GTP to GDP, turning off the signal. Without this \"off switch,\" adenylyl cyclase is constitutively active, producing excess cAMP that mimics constant hormone stimulation (LH → ovary → estrogen → precocious puberty; TSH → thyroid → hyperthyroidism; ACTH → adrenal → hypercortisolism; MSH → melanocytes → café-au-lait spots). This is also the mechanism of cholera toxin (which also prevents GTP hydrolysis by Gs-alpha). The same Gs-alpha mutation in somatotrophs causes GH-secreting pituitary adenomas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: McCune-Albright = Gs mutation + café-au-lait (irregular borders) + polyostotic fibrous dysplasia + precocious puberty. Compare: NF1 café-au-lait spots have smooth \"coast of California\" borders. Gs-alpha mutation is also seen in GH-secreting pituitary adenomas causing acromegaly. COMLEX: acromegaly is a CP3 named condition; the Gs mutation link connects GPCR receptor biology directly to a testable pathology.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — HPA axis: ACTH acts via Gs-cAMP on adrenocortical MC2R receptors; constitutive Gs activation mimics persistent ACTH stimulation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl2_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain G protein-coupled receptor signaling pathways and describe the role of second messenger systems including cAMP, IP3, and DAG.","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old man returning from a humanitarian mission in Haiti presents with sudden-onset, profuse watery \"rice-water\" diarrhea and severe dehydration. Stool cultures grow Vibrio cholerae. The pathogenic mechanism involves a toxin that ADP-ribosylates the Gs-alpha subunit in intestinal epithelial cells, permanently modifying its function. Which of the following best explains how this toxin modification leads to the massive secretory diarrhea?","choices":{"D":"ADP-ribosylation prevents Gs-alpha from hydrolyzing GTP to GDP, locking it in the active state; constitutively active Gs-alpha continuously stimulates adenylyl cyclase, and the excess cAMP activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates CFTR chloride channels and drives massive Cl- and water secretion.","A":"ADP-ribosylation activates Gq-alpha in intestinal epithelial cells, causing phospholipase C to constitutively generate IP3 and DAG; excess IP3 triggers sustained Ca2+ release from the endoplasmic reticulum, which activates calmodulin-dependent channels and drives pathologic water secretion independent of cAMP.","B":"ADP-ribosylation blocks Gi-alpha function in intestinal cells, preventing inhibition of adenylyl cyclase; the resulting loss of cAMP inhibition means that normal Gs-alpha signals are amplified manyfold and trigger abnormal CFTR-independent chloride transport through altered calcium-activated chloride channels.","C":"ADP-ribosylation converts the Gs-alpha subunit into a constitutively active receptor tyrosine kinase-like molecule; this aberrant kinase activity phosphorylates CFTR directly on tyrosine residues, opening the channel constitutively and driving massive chloride and water secretion into the bowel lumen.","E":"ADP-ribosylation destabilizes the Gs-alpha GTP-binding domain, causing it to release GDP constitutively and recruit excessive hormone-receptor complexes; this \"super-recruitment\" effect multiplies normal receptor signaling 100-fold and activates all downstream cAMP-independent secretory pathways simultaneously."},"explanation":"Cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates Gs-alpha, locking it on much as the activating GNAS mutation of McCune-Albright does. This prevents GTP hydrolysis → permanent Gs-alpha activation → constitutive adenylyl cyclase activity → massively elevated cAMP → PKA activation → phosphorylation of CFTR → unregulated Cl- secretion into the lumen → osmotic water loss = secretory diarrhea. This is a pure secretory diarrhea: stool osmolality gap is absent and diarrhea persists during fasting. Pertussis toxin uses the opposite mechanism — it ADP-ribosylates Gi-alpha, preventing Gi from inhibiting adenylyl cyclase (net effect also raises cAMP, contributing to pertussis pathology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cholera toxin = ADP-ribosylates Gs → cAMP up → CFTR → secretory diarrhea. Pertussis toxin = ADP-ribosylates Gi → cannot inhibit adenylyl cyclase → cAMP up. Both toxins raise cAMP but by different mechanisms. E. coli heat-labile toxin also ADP-ribosylates Gs. These are classic USMLE microbiology-physiology integration questions. COMLEX: secretory diarrhea is high-output, isotonic, and persists during fasting — contrast with osmotic diarrhea which stops with fasting.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — negative feedback regulation: loss of self-limiting GTPase activity in Gs illustrates what happens when normal receptor termination is disrupted","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"B":"Pertussis toxin uses the oppos"}},{"id":"sdl2_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain G protein-coupled receptor signaling pathways and describe the role of second messenger systems including cAMP, IP3, and DAG.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman at 41 weeks gestation is admitted for labor induction. IV oxytocin is initiated and she begins having regular uterine contractions within 5 minutes. Oxytocin mediates uterine contractions through Gq-coupled receptors on myometrial smooth muscle. Which of the following correctly describes the sequence of intracellular events that produces myometrial contraction after oxytocin receptor activation?","choices":{"A":"Gq-alpha activation → phospholipase C stimulation → PIP2 hydrolysis → IP3 and DAG; IP3 binds IP3R receptors on the endoplasmic reticulum, releasing Ca2+ into the cytoplasm; elevated Ca2+ binds calmodulin → Ca2+-calmodulin activates myosin light chain kinase (MLCK) → MLCK phosphorylates myosin light chains → actin-myosin cross-bridge cycling → smooth muscle contraction.","B":"Gs-alpha activation → adenylyl cyclase → ATP to cAMP → protein kinase A → phosphorylation of myosin light chain kinase at a regulatory site that renders MLCK inactive; resulting inhibition of myosin-actin cross-bridge cycling produces uterine smooth muscle relaxation rather than contraction, explaining why beta-2 agonists (tocolytics) can be used to suppress premature labor.","C":"Receptor tyrosine kinase autophosphorylation → PI3K-Akt cascade → phosphorylation of cytoskeletal regulatory proteins → direct activation of sarcomeric actin-myosin interaction in myometrial cells; the RTK pathway is 3 to 5 minutes slower than GPCR pathways due to the additional phosphorylation steps required before force generation.","D":"Gi-alpha activation → inhibition of adenylyl cyclase → decreased cAMP → disinhibition of phospholipase C → IP3 and DAG production; this indirect pathway is less efficient than direct Gq activation, explaining the 5-minute latency before regular contractions are observed following IV oxytocin administration.","E":"Oxytocin binds an intracellular receptor after partial lipophilic modification in the bloodstream; the oxytocin-receptor complex enters the nucleus and increases transcription of smooth muscle contractile proteins including alpha-actin and smooth muscle myosin heavy chain, producing new contractile machinery within 5 minutes."},"explanation":"Oxytocin uses a Gq-coupled GPCR: Gq → PLC → PIP2 → IP3 + DAG. IP3 binds ER receptors and releases Ca2+. Ca2+ + calmodulin → activates MLCK → phosphorylates myosin light chains → actomyosin contraction. DAG simultaneously activates PKC, which further amplifies contraction signals. This is the same Gq/IP3/Ca2+ pathway used by all \"GOAT HAG\" hormones. The Gs/cAMP/PKA distractor is mechanistically accurate regarding beta-2 agonists and cAMP relaxation (tocolysis), but it describes relaxation, not oxytocin-driven contraction — recognizing this illustrates why cAMP (Gs) and IP3/Ca2+ (Gq) have opposite effects on smooth muscle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Oxytocin is in \"GOAT HAG\" (Gq), contrasted with ADH V2 (Gs/cAMP). Knowing that Gs/cAMP relaxes smooth muscle (beta-2 tocolytics) while Gq/IP3/Ca2+ contracts it is a high-yield discriminator. COMLEX: oxytocin receptors are also found in the breast (milk ejection reflex); COMLEX may test neurohypophysis anatomy and oxytocin synthesis in the hypothalamic paraventricular nucleus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — oxytocin mechanism of action in uterine contraction and milk ejection; synthesized in hypothalamic paraventricular nucleus","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl2_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain G protein-coupled receptor signaling pathways and describe the role of second messenger systems including cAMP, IP3, and DAG.","batch":2,"stem":"Bordetella pertussis produces a toxin that ADP-ribosylates Gi-alpha, preventing it from responding to receptor activation. A pharmacology professor uses this as a model to explain a class of drugs. In the context of endocrine signaling, if a drug similarly inactivated Gi-alpha in a somatotroph, which of the following would be the expected consequence for intracellular cAMP and downstream growth hormone secretion?","choices":{"B":"Inactivation of Gi-alpha removes the inhibitory brake on adenylyl cyclase, so basal and GHRH-stimulated cyclase activity is unopposed, raising intracellular cAMP and PKA activity; GH secretion therefore increases because somatostatin can no longer lower cAMP to suppress GH release.","A":"Inactivation of Gi-alpha would reduce phospholipase C activity in somatotrophs because Gi-alpha normally recruits and stabilizes Gq-alpha; loss of Gi scaffolding leads to decreased IP3 and DAG production, impairing the calmodulin-dependent Ca2+ pathway that is required for GH vesicle exocytosis.","C":"Inactivation of Gi-alpha would have no effect on GH secretion because growth hormone release is entirely regulated by the GHRH–Gs–cAMP pathway, and the Gi-dependent somatostatin pathway is redundant; basal adenylyl cyclase activity and GH secretion would be maintained unchanged.","D":"Inactivation of Gi-alpha causes the beta-gamma subunits to constitutively activate inwardly rectifying potassium channels on somatotrophs; hyperpolarization of the somatotroph cell membrane prevents action potentials and voltage-gated Ca2+ influx, leading to decreased GH vesicle fusion and reduced secretion.","E":"Inactivation of Gi-alpha disrupts the normal stoichiometric ratio between Gs-alpha and Gi-alpha; the excess free Gs-alpha is ubiquitinated and degraded by the proteasome, reducing total available Gs for GHRH-receptor coupling and paradoxically decreasing cAMP-driven GH secretion."},"explanation":"Gi (inhibitory G protein) normally suppresses adenylyl cyclase when activated by inhibitory ligands (somatostatin in somatotrophs). Inactivating Gi-alpha (e.g., by pertussis toxin ADP-ribosylation) removes this inhibitory input, leaving adenylyl cyclase activity unopposed by somatostatin signaling. The result is elevated cAMP, enhanced PKA activity, and increased GH secretion. This is conceptually similar to how pertussis toxin raises cAMP in respiratory epithelial cells. Clinically, octreotide (somatostatin analog) exploits Gi coupling to suppress GH in acromegaly — Gi inactivation would render octreotide ineffective. Contrast with McCune-Albright (Gs gain-of-function, also raises cAMP in somatotrophs via the stimulatory side).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pertussis toxin = ADP-ribosylates Gi → cannot inhibit adenylyl cyclase → cAMP up. This contrasts with cholera toxin (Gs). Remember both toxins RAISE cAMP but through opposite G proteins. In somatotrophs: GHRH → Gs → cAMP up → GH secretion; Somatostatin → Gi → cAMP down → GH suppression. Inactivating Gi mimics continuous somatostatin absence. COMLEX: somatostatin analogs (octreotide) treat acromegaly by activating Gi in GH-secreting adenoma cells.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — somatostatin inhibits GH secretion via Gi-coupled receptors; GHRH stimulates via Gs-coupled receptors","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"}],"3":[{"id":"sdl3_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the tyrosine kinase receptor mechanism including hormone binding, receptor dimerization, and autophosphorylation.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structural feature most clearly distinguishes enzyme-linked receptors from G protein-coupled receptors?","choices":{"E":"Enzyme-linked receptors span the plasma membrane a single time, with an extracellular ligand-binding domain linked directly to an intracellular enzymatic domain","A":"Enzyme-linked receptors consist of seven transmembrane alpha-helices connected by alternating extracellular and intracellular loops that together bind a heterotrimeric G protein","B":"Enzyme-linked receptors reside entirely in the cytoplasm and bind lipophilic ligands that diffuse freely across the plasma membrane before initiating downstream signaling","C":"Enzyme-linked receptors are anchored in the nuclear envelope and regulate gene transcription by binding hormone-receptor complexes arriving directly from the cytosol","D":"Enzyme-linked receptors form a non-covalent complex with G-alpha, G-beta, and G-gamma subunits that dissociate from each other upon hormonal activation of the receptor"},"explanation":"Enzyme-linked receptors are single-pass transmembrane proteins: one hydrophobic segment crosses the lipid bilayer, placing the ligand-binding domain extracellularly and the enzymatic (kinase) domain intracellularly. GPCRs, by contrast, thread through the membrane seven times. Nuclear receptors bind intracellular lipophilic hormones and are not membrane-spanning in the same sense. Enzyme-linked receptors do not associate with heterotrimeric G proteins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the three receptor classes — GPCR (7 TM, second messengers), enzyme-linked RTK (single TM, intrinsic or associated kinase), and nuclear (intracellular). COMLEX: receptor type determines both mechanism and drug target; knowing single-pass vs seven-pass is foundational for pharmacology questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — enzyme-linked RTK: single-pass transmembrane receptor; ligand binding induces receptor dimerization and trans-autophosphorylation of intracellular tyrosine kinase domains, recruiting IRS-1 and activating PI3K-Akt and MAPK cascades","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"GPCRs, by contrast, thread thr","B":"Nuclear receptors bind intrace","C":"Nuclear receptors bind intrace","D":"Enzyme-linked receptors do not"}},{"id":"sdl3_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the tyrosine kinase receptor mechanism including hormone binding, receptor dimerization, and autophosphorylation.","batch":1,"stem":"Which event occurs immediately after a polypeptide hormone binds to its receptor tyrosine kinase?","choices":{"B":"The two receptor monomers are brought into proximity, dimerize, and trans-phosphorylate each other's intracellular tyrosine kinase domains, achieving full activation of the receptor","A":"The receptor activates an associated Gs-alpha subunit, which dissociates and stimulates adenylyl cyclase to generate cAMP and activate protein kinase A throughout the cytoplasm","C":"The hormone-receptor complex translocates directly into the nucleus, where the intracellular domain binds hormone response elements on DNA to initiate target gene transcription","D":"The receptor activates phospholipase C-beta, cleaving PIP2 into IP3 and DAG, which mobilize intracellular calcium and activate protein kinase C to phosphorylate downstream substrates","E":"The receptor recruits beta-arrestin, triggers clathrin-coated pit formation, and is rapidly internalized into early endosomes before any intracellular phosphorylation event occurs"},"explanation":"For receptor tyrosine kinases, ligand binding is immediately followed by receptor dimerization (bringing two receptor chains together), then mutual trans-autophosphorylation of tyrosine residues in each other's intracellular kinase domains. This creates docking sites for downstream adaptor proteins such as IRS-1. cAMP generation is a GPCR-Gs pathway event. Nuclear translocation is a nuclear receptor event. PLC-IP3/DAG is a GPCR-Gq pathway event. Beta-arrestin recruitment is a desensitization event that occurs after initial signaling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RTK sequence — ligand binding → dimerization → autophosphorylation → adaptor recruitment (IRS-1, Grb2) → PI3K/Akt and Ras/MAPK. This is the insulin and IGF-1 signaling backbone. COMLEX: RTK autophosphorylation is the First Aid HY concept for tyrosine kinase receptors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — enzyme-linked RTK: single-pass transmembrane receptor; ligand binding induces receptor dimerization and trans-autophosphorylation of intracellular tyrosine kinase domains, recruiting IRS-1 and activating PI3K-Akt and MAPK cascades","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"This creates docking sites for","C":"Nuclear translocation is a nuc","D":"PLC-IP3/DAG is a GPCR-Gq pathw"}},{"id":"sdl3_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the tyrosine kinase receptor mechanism including hormone binding, receptor dimerization, and autophosphorylation.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the sequence of events following insulin binding to its receptor tyrosine kinase?","choices":{"C":"Ligand binding induces receptor dimerization and trans-autophosphorylation of intracellular tyrosine residues, followed by IRS-1 recruitment and activation of the PI3K-Akt and Ras-MAPK cascades","A":"Ligand binding activates adenylyl cyclase through a stimulatory Gs-alpha subunit, elevating cAMP and enabling protein kinase A to phosphorylate downstream nuclear and cytoplasmic targets","B":"Ligand binding causes rapid receptor internalization into early endosomes before any autophosphorylation occurs, and signaling is initiated from endosomal STAT protein complexes inside the cell","D":"Ligand binding activates phospholipase C-gamma, generating IP3 and DAG, which mobilize intracellular calcium stores from the endoplasmic reticulum and activate protein kinase C isoforms","E":"Ligand binding activates a trimeric G-protein complex that sequentially activates RAF, MEK, and ERK to phosphorylate nuclear transcription factors and regulate metabolic gene programs"},"explanation":"The insulin RTK sequence is: insulin binds extracellular alpha subunits → receptor dimerization of two alpha-beta half-receptors → trans-autophosphorylation of beta-subunit tyrosines → IRS-1 docking → PI3K-Akt (metabolic effects, GLUT4) and Ras-MAPK (growth effects). The Gs-cAMP-PKA pathway is GPCR-mediated. PLC-IP3/DAG is the Gq pathway. Ras-Raf-MEK-ERK can be activated downstream of RTKs through Grb2-SOS, but the Ras activation requires prior RTK autophosphorylation and adaptor recruitment, so cAMP-PKA and Gq are both wrong first steps.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Insulin receptor is an RTK — alpha2-beta2 heterotetramer with intrinsic beta-subunit TK. Contrast with leptin/GH receptors which lack intrinsic TK and use JAK2. COMLEX: The order of events is testable; dimerization always precedes autophosphorylation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — enzyme-linked RTK: single-pass transmembrane receptor; ligand binding induces receptor dimerization and trans-autophosphorylation of intracellular tyrosine kinase domains, recruiting IRS-1 and activating PI3K-Akt and MAPK cascades","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"The Gs-cAMP-PKA pathway is GPC","D":"PLC-IP3/DAG is the Gq pathway."}},{"id":"sdl3_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe negative and positive feedback regulation of hormone secretion and provide clinical examples of each mechanism.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the general principle of negative feedback regulation of hormone secretion?","choices":{"E":"Rising levels of the target hormone or its metabolic product suppress the upstream gland or hypothalamic-pituitary axis that stimulated its release, preventing excessive hormone accumulation","A":"A transient increase in hormone concentration amplifies secretion from the upstream releasing gland, producing a short-lived overshoot that then triggers receptor down-regulation to restore baseline levels","B":"A regulatory hormone binds nuclear receptors in peripheral target tissues and permanently silences the genes encoding the upstream hypothalamic releasing factors throughout the reproductive lifespan","C":"High hormone concentrations stimulate accelerated synthesis of new receptors in target cells, increasing tissue sensitivity and further up-regulating downstream effector organ responses","D":"Peripheral target cells synthesize diffusible inhibitory proteins that travel through the lymphatic system to suppress upstream gland activity over a period of weeks to months"},"explanation":"Negative feedback is the primary mechanism that prevents hormone hypersecretion: the end-product of a hormonal axis feeds back to suppress the upstream driver. Classic examples include cortisol suppressing CRH/ACTH, T3/T4 suppressing TRH/TSH, and estrogen/progesterone suppressing GnRH/LH/FSH during most of the menstrual cycle. The transient-amplification choice describes positive feedback. Receptor up-regulation and lymph-borne inhibitory proteins are not classical negative feedback, and hormones do not permanently silence the genes for hypothalamic releasing factors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know all three major HPT/HPA/HPG axis feedback loops. Negative feedback is the rule; positive feedback (LH surge) is the exception. COMLEX: Understanding feedback enables you to predict lab values — primary gland failure causes high tropic hormone because feedback is broken.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — feedback regulation: negative feedback (hormone or its product suppresses the upstream secreting axis) prevents oversecretion; positive feedback (estrogen-driven LH surge) drives brief amplification; receptor down-regulation attenuates target-tissue responsiveness with chronic stimulation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"The transient-amplification ch","C":"Receptor up-regulation and lym","D":"Receptor up-regulation and lym"}},{"id":"sdl3_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe negative and positive feedback regulation of hormone secretion and provide clinical examples of each mechanism.","batch":1,"stem":"In the context of menstrual cycle physiology, which of the following best exemplifies positive feedback regulation of hormone secretion?","choices":{"D":"Declining estrogen and progesterone concentrations during menstruation remove tonic inhibition from the hypothalamus, permitting GnRH-driven FSH release and initiation of a new follicular phase","A":"Elevated progesterone concentrations during the luteal phase suppress GnRH pulse frequency from the hypothalamus, reducing FSH and LH secretion to prevent recruitment of a new dominant follicle","B":"Sustained estradiol above a critical threshold just before ovulation switches pituitary gonadotrophs from inhibitory to stimulatory sensitivity, triggering the LH surge that ruptures the follicle","C":"Granulosa cell secretion of inhibin B selectively suppresses FSH release from anterior pituitary gonadotrophs while leaving LH secretion relatively unchanged throughout the follicular phase","E":"Follicle-stimulating hormone promotes granulosa cell aromatase activity, converting androgens to estrogen in a self-limiting paracrine loop that maintains stable estradiol levels during follicular growth"},"explanation":"The LH surge is the archetypal example of positive feedback in endocrinology: when estradiol rises above a threshold for a sustained period (Guyton: estrogen above a critical rate for 2 to 3 days; Costanzo: at least 200 pg/mL), it switches the pituitary response to GnRH from inhibition to amplification, producing a massive LH surge. The luteal progesterone, menstrual-withdrawal, and inhibin B choices all describe negative feedback or the removal of inhibition. The FSH-aromatase choice describes a self-limiting paracrine loop, not positive feedback on hormone secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Positive feedback is rare — LH surge (estrogen-driven) and oxytocin during labor are the two canonical examples. Everything else in endocrine is negative feedback. COMLEX: Disruption of the LH surge is the basis for combined oral contraceptive efficacy at suppressing ovulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — feedback regulation: negative feedback (hormone or its product suppresses the upstream secreting axis) prevents oversecretion; positive feedback (estrogen-driven LH surge) drives brief amplification; receptor down-regulation attenuates target-tissue responsiveness with chronic stimulation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"The luteal progesterone, menst","C":"The luteal progesterone, menst","D":"The luteal progesterone, menst"}},{"id":"sdl3_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe negative and positive feedback regulation of hormone secretion and provide clinical examples of each mechanism.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient chronically treated with a high-dose long-acting GLP-1 receptor agonist develops a progressively blunted glycemic response to the medication over several months. Which receptor-level mechanism most likely underlies this loss of efficacy?","choices":{"B":"Prolonged receptor occupancy promotes receptor phosphorylation, beta-arrestin recruitment, clathrin-mediated endocytosis, and lysosomal degradation, reducing the number of surface receptors available for ligand binding","A":"Sustained receptor activation triggers compensatory overexpression of Gs-alpha subunits, allowing the pathway to remain constitutively active through increased cAMP generation despite reduced receptor occupancy at the membrane","C":"Chronic agonist stimulation causes irreversible covalent modification of adenylyl cyclase by the sustained cAMP signal, depleting cellular ATP stores and rendering downstream PKA permanently unresponsive","D":"Prolonged exposure causes cross-phosphorylation of adjacent unrelated GPCRs by the activated receptor complex, redirecting intracellular signaling from the cAMP-PKA pathway toward JAK-STAT gene transcription programs","E":"High circulating agonist concentrations saturate plasma albumin and GLP-1 carrier proteins, paradoxically reducing the fraction of bioavailable free ligand and lowering effective receptor occupancy over time"},"explanation":"Receptor down-regulation in response to chronic agonist exposure involves five overlapping mechanisms: receptor phosphorylation by GRKs, recruitment of beta-arrestin blocking G-protein coupling, receptor sequestration away from the membrane, clathrin-mediated endocytosis into early endosomes, and lysosomal degradation (or reduced transcription of receptor gene). The net effect is fewer functional receptors on the cell surface, producing a blunted response. Covalent modification of adenylyl cyclase and cross-phosphorylation of unrelated GPCRs toward JAK-STAT are mechanistically incorrect. Gs-alpha up-regulation does not occur as a compensation mechanism, and carrier-protein saturation is not how receptors down-regulate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Down-regulation vs desensitization — desensitization is acute (phosphorylation, arrestin) and reversible; down-regulation is chronic (internalization, degradation) and slower to reverse. COMLEX: Clinical consequence of down-regulation = drug tolerance and reduced therapeutic response with chronic use.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — feedback regulation: negative feedback (hormone or its product suppresses the upstream secreting axis) prevents oversecretion; positive feedback (estrogen-driven LH surge) drives brief amplification; receptor down-regulation attenuates target-tissue responsiveness with chronic stimulation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Gs-alpha up-regulation does no","E":"Gs-alpha up-regulation does no"}},{"id":"sdl3_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe enzyme-linked receptor signaling that occurs through associated intracellular kinases, using the leptin receptor (JAK-STAT pathway) as a model, including receptor dimerization, kinase activation, and gene transcription.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best distinguishes the leptin receptor from the insulin receptor in terms of intrinsic enzymatic activity?","choices":{"E":"The leptin receptor lacks intrinsic kinase activity and relies on the constitutively associated cytoplasmic kinase JAK2 to phosphorylate tyrosine residues on the receptor and on downstream STAT proteins","A":"The leptin receptor contains two alpha subunits and two beta subunits, with intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity located within the cytoplasmic portions of its beta subunits, similar to the insulin receptor","B":"The leptin receptor is a seven-transmembrane G-protein-coupled receptor that activates adenylyl cyclase through a Gs-alpha subunit and raises intracellular cAMP upon leptin binding","C":"The leptin receptor is a nuclear receptor that binds the leptin molecule after it diffuses through the plasma membrane, dimerizes in the cytoplasm, and then translocates to regulate gene transcription","D":"The leptin receptor autophosphorylates its own intracellular tyrosine residues immediately upon dimerization and does not require any associated cytoplasmic or membrane-anchored kinase partner"},"explanation":"The insulin receptor (RTK) has intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity in the cytoplasmic beta-subunit domains and can autophosphorylate directly. The leptin receptor belongs to the cytokine receptor superfamily: it has no intrinsic enzymatic domain, but constitutively binds JAK2 in its intracellular box1/box2 motifs. After leptin-induced dimerization, the two JAK2 molecules trans-phosphorylate each other and then phosphorylate STAT3. Leptin is a protein hormone and cannot diffuse through the membrane, ruling out nuclear receptor classification.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cytokine receptor family uses associated JAKs. FA mnemonic \"GET a JAKed PIG\": G-CSF, Erythropoietin, Thrombopoietin, Prolactin, Immunomodulators (IL-2, IL-6, IFN), GH. COMLEX: JAK inhibitors (e.g., tofacitinib) target this associated kinase mechanism and are used in autoimmune and inflammatory conditions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — JAK-STAT via leptin receptor: homodimeric receptor lacks intrinsic kinase; ligand binding causes receptor dimerization and conformational change; JAK2 trans-phosphorylates and activates STAT3; phospho-STAT3 dimerizes and translocates to the nucleus to drive target gene transcription","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"The leptin receptor belongs to","D":"The leptin receptor belongs to"}},{"id":"sdl3_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe enzyme-linked receptor signaling that occurs through associated intracellular kinases, using the leptin receptor (JAK-STAT pathway) as a model, including receptor dimerization, kinase activation, and gene transcription.","batch":1,"stem":"After JAK2 is activated by dimerized leptin receptors, which of the following is the immediate next event in the JAK-STAT signaling cascade?","choices":{"C":"JAK2 phosphorylates tyrosine residues on the receptor and on cytoplasmic STAT3, allowing phospho-STAT3 monomers to dimerize and translocate into the nucleus to drive target gene transcription","A":"Activated JAK2 phosphorylates phospholipase C-gamma, generating IP3 and DAG that mobilize calcium from endoplasmic reticulum stores and activate protein kinase C to phosphorylate IRS-1","B":"Activated JAK2 stimulates adenylyl cyclase directly, elevating intracellular cAMP and enabling protein kinase A to phosphorylate the CREB transcription factor on a serine residue in the nucleus","D":"Activated JAK2 dissociates from the receptor complex and migrates independently to the nucleus, where it directly binds STAT-response elements on DNA to initiate leptin-responsive gene transcription","E":"Activated JAK2 phosphorylates and activates the small GTPase Ras, which recruits Raf to the inner membrane leaflet and initiates a sequential MAP kinase phosphorylation cascade"},"explanation":"In the canonical JAK-STAT pathway: (1) leptin binds, (2) receptor dimerizes, (3) constitutively associated JAK2 molecules trans-phosphorylate each other, (4) activated JAK2 phosphorylates tyrosine residues on the intracellular receptor tail creating docking sites, (5) STAT3 docks and is phosphorylated by JAK2, (6) phospho-STAT3 dimerizes and translocates to the nucleus to transcribe target genes (POMC, SOCS3, neuropeptide Y suppression). PLC and adenylyl cyclase are downstream of GPCR-Gq and GPCR-Gs respectively. JAK2 does not enter the nucleus. Ras activation is downstream of RTKs via Grb2-SOS, not JAK-STAT.","boardPrep":"Step 1: JAK-STAT sequence — receptor dimerization → JAK2 trans-phosphorylation → STAT phosphorylation → STAT dimerization → nuclear translocation → gene transcription. COMLEX: SOCS proteins are the negative regulators of JAK-STAT; SOCS3 up-regulation is the mechanism of leptin resistance in obesity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — JAK-STAT via leptin receptor: homodimeric receptor lacks intrinsic kinase; ligand binding causes receptor dimerization and conformational change; JAK2 trans-phosphorylates and activates STAT3; phospho-STAT3 dimerizes and translocates to the nucleus to drive target gene transcription","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"PLC and adenylyl cyclase are d","B":"PLC and adenylyl cyclase are d","D":"JAK2 does not enter the nucleu","E":"Ras activation is downstream o"}},{"id":"sdl3_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe enzyme-linked receptor signaling that occurs through associated intracellular kinases, using the leptin receptor (JAK-STAT pathway) as a model, including receptor dimerization, kinase activation, and gene transcription.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following hormones signals through a mechanism most analogous to leptin, relying on an associated cytoplasmic kinase rather than intrinsic receptor tyrosine kinase activity?","choices":{"A":"Growth hormone signals through a homodimeric receptor constitutively associated with JAK2; ligand-induced dimerization activates JAK2 to phosphorylate STAT5b, driving hepatic IGF-1 gene transcription","B":"Insulin signals through a disulfide-linked alpha2-beta2 heterotetrameric receptor in which the cytoplasmic beta subunits contain intrinsic tyrosine kinase domains that trans-autophosphorylate upon ligand binding","C":"Cortisol signals through a cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor held in an inactive complex by heat-shock proteins, which is released upon steroid binding to allow nuclear translocation and DNA binding","D":"Glucagon signals through a seven-transmembrane receptor coupled to a stimulatory Gs-alpha protein, which activates adenylyl cyclase to generate cAMP and activate protein kinase A in hepatocytes","E":"Epinephrine signals through beta-adrenergic receptors that couple to Gs-alpha proteins, modulating adenylyl cyclase activity and cyclic AMP levels in cardiac muscle and adipose tissue"},"explanation":"Growth hormone, like leptin, uses the cytokine receptor superfamily signaling mechanism: a homodimeric receptor lacking intrinsic kinase activity binds JAK2 constitutively, and GH-induced receptor dimerization causes JAK2 trans-phosphorylation followed by STAT5b phosphorylation, leading to hepatic IGF-1 transcription. Insulin has intrinsic beta-subunit TK activity — it is an RTK, not analogous. Cortisol uses a nuclear receptor. Glucagon and epinephrine use GPCRs with Gs-cAMP-PKA signaling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH and leptin both use JAK2-STAT pathway via cytokine receptor superfamily. GH uses STAT5b; leptin uses STAT3. Know which STATs downstream of which hormone. COMLEX: Laron syndrome (GH receptor mutation) and leptin receptor mutations both cause growth/metabolic failure despite normal or elevated upstream hormone levels.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — JAK-STAT via leptin receptor: homodimeric receptor lacks intrinsic kinase; ligand binding causes receptor dimerization and conformational change; JAK2 trans-phosphorylates and activates STAT3; phospho-STAT3 dimerizes and translocates to the nucleus to drive target gene transcription","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Insulin has intrinsic beta-sub","C":"Cortisol uses a nuclear recept","D":"Glucagon and epinephrine use G","E":"Glucagon and epinephrine use G"}},{"id":"sdl3_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the tyrosine kinase receptor mechanism including hormone binding, receptor dimerization, and autophosphorylation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with a 40 pack-year smoking history presents with 3 months of progressive dyspnea, weight loss, and hemoptysis. Chest CT shows a 4.2-cm right upper lobe mass with mediastinal lymphadenopathy. Biopsy confirms non-small cell lung carcinoma. Molecular testing reveals an activating point mutation in the epidermal growth factor receptor gene. She is started on gefitinib. Which of the following best describes the molecular mechanism that makes this tumor susceptible to gefitinib?","choices":{"B":"The mutation prevents normal receptor dimerization by altering extracellular domain contacts, trapping the receptor as a ligand-occupied monomer in which gefitinib acts as an allosteric activator that restores normal downstream Ras signaling","A":"The activating mutation stabilizes the kinase domain in its active conformation, producing constitutive autophosphorylation independent of ligand binding, and gefitinib occupies the ATP-binding cleft to block this kinase activity","C":"The mutation causes dramatic overexpression of inhibitory Gs-alpha subunits that constitutively activate adenylyl cyclase, and gefitinib inhibits cyclic nucleotide phosphodiesterase to reduce the resulting elevated intracellular cAMP","D":"The mutation eliminates the extracellular EGF-binding domain entirely, and gefitinib acts as a partial agonist that restores low-level receptor activation sufficient to prevent unregulated PI3K pathway signaling","E":"The mutation enhances endosomal recycling of activated receptors back to the plasma membrane, prolonging net signaling duration, and gefitinib promotes clathrin-independent lysosomal targeting to counteract this recycling"},"explanation":"Activating EGFR mutations (most commonly exon 19 deletions or L858R in exon 21) lock the receptor kinase domain in its active conformation, driving constitutive autophosphorylation and downstream Ras-MAPK and PI3K-Akt signaling without requiring EGF binding. Gefitinib (and erlotinib) are small-molecule ATP-competitive inhibitors that occupy the kinase ATP-binding pocket, blocking autophosphorylation. The mutation does not involve Gs-alpha, adenylyl cyclase, or endosomal recycling. The concept directly extends RTK autophosphorylation from Obj 1 into oncology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EGFR exon 19 del and L858R are the two most common sensitizing mutations; both respond to first-generation TKIs (gefitinib, erlotinib). T790M is the acquired resistance mutation addressed by osimertinib (3rd gen). COMLEX: RTK inhibitor mechanism tested as a pharmacology concept; constitutive autophosphorylation is the unifying principle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — matching endocrine hormones to receptor tyrosine kinase receptor class","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"E":"The mutation does not involve "}},{"id":"sdl3_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the tyrosine kinase receptor mechanism including hormone binding, receptor dimerization, and autophosphorylation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with a 10-year history of type 2 diabetes mellitus presents for routine follow-up. His BMI is 35 kg/m2, fasting glucose is 198 mg/dL, and fasting insulin level is markedly elevated at 42 mIU/L. Despite this high endogenous insulin level, his tissues show a diminished biological response to insulin action. Which of the following best describes the cellular receptor mechanism that most directly underlies his insulin resistance?","choices":{"C":"Chronically elevated insulin concentrations promote phosphorylation of intracellular receptor serine residues by inflammatory kinases, as well as clathrin-mediated endocytosis and lysosomal degradation of insulin receptors, collectively reducing functional receptor density on adipocyte and skeletal muscle cell surfaces","A":"Elevated circulating free fatty acids in obesity phosphorylate and constitutively activate adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP levels that activate protein kinase A, which in turn phosphorylates IRS-1 serine residues and competitively blocks tyrosine phosphorylation by the insulin receptor","B":"Elevated serum leptin concentrations in obesity activate JAK2-mediated phosphorylation of IRS-1 serine residues, diverting insulin receptor substrate signaling away from PI3K-Akt and toward STAT3-dependent transcription programs in hepatocytes","D":"Persistent hyperinsulinemia activates intracellular glucocorticoid receptors that directly suppress GLUT4 gene transcription in adipocytes, reducing glucose transporter expression independent of any change in insulin receptor surface density or phosphorylation status","E":"Prolonged receptor occupancy causes irreversible covalent cross-linking between the alpha subunit extracellular domains of adjacent receptors, physically preventing subsequent insulin binding without altering beta-subunit tyrosine kinase activity or receptor internalization rates"},"explanation":"Insulin resistance in obesity and type 2 diabetes is primarily a receptor down-regulation and post-receptor signaling impairment phenomenon. Chronic hyperinsulinemia drives receptor internalization (clathrin-coated pits → endosomes → lysosomes), reducing surface receptor number — a direct application of the down-regulation mechanism from Obj 2. Additionally, inflammatory signals (TNF-alpha, free fatty acid-activated IKK/JNK) phosphorylate IRS-1 on serine residues rather than tyrosines, blocking downstream PI3K-Akt signaling. Adenylyl cyclase is not involved. Leptin-JAK2 and glucocorticoid receptor-GLUT4 suppression are ancillary mechanisms, not the primary receptor-level explanation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Insulin resistance = reduced RTK surface receptors plus serine phosphorylation of IRS-1 blocking tyrosine phosphorylation. Understand this for the insulin receptor and its downstream IRS-1 → PI3K → GLUT4 pathway. COMLEX: Type 2 DM pathophysiology integrates RTK down-regulation, beta-cell failure, and peripheral resistance — all testable together.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — receptor down-regulation as a negative feedback mechanism with chronic stimulation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"B":"Leptin-JAK2 and glucocorticoid","D":"Leptin-JAK2 and glucocorticoid"}},{"id":"sdl3_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the tyrosine kinase receptor mechanism including hormone binding, receptor dimerization, and autophosphorylation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy is referred to a pediatric endocrinologist for evaluation of severe short stature. His height is at the 1st percentile and growth velocity is markedly reduced. Serum growth hormone is markedly elevated at 32 ng/mL, yet serum IGF-1 is undetectable at less than 25 ng/mL. Genetic testing reveals a homozygous loss-of-function mutation in the growth hormone receptor gene. Binding studies show the mutant receptor is expressed on hepatocyte membranes and binds GH with normal affinity. Which of the following best explains the paradoxical finding of high GH with undetectable IGF-1?","choices":{"A":"The mutant growth hormone receptor cannot undergo the productive dimerization required to activate JAK2, so STAT5b is not phosphorylated and the liver fails to transcribe the IGF-1 gene despite abundant circulating GH","B":"Excess circulating GH competitively inhibits IGF-1 binding to its own receptor tyrosine kinase in hepatocytes, creating a state of IGF-1 receptor resistance that accelerates IGF-1 clearance and prevents measurable serum accumulation","C":"The mutant GH receptor binds GH normally but cannot be internalized via clathrin-coated pits, trapping the GH-receptor complex at the plasma membrane and preventing nuclear signaling needed for IGF-1 transcription","D":"Absence of functional GH receptor prevents somatostatin release from hypothalamic periventricular neurons, creating continuous unopposed GHRH stimulation that paradoxically suppresses IGF-1 synthesis through a cAMP-mediated mechanism","E":"The mutation disrupts the signal peptide required for membrane insertion, causing the receptor to misfold in the endoplasmic reticulum and preventing surface expression entirely so that no GH ligand binding occurs at all"},"explanation":"This is Laron syndrome (primary GH insensitivity). Normal GH signaling requires: (1) GH binds one receptor monomer, (2) receptor dimerizes to bring two JAK2 molecules together, (3) JAK2 trans-phosphorylation, (4) STAT5b phosphorylation and nuclear translocation, (5) IGF-1 gene transcription in liver. A loss-of-function GH receptor mutation disrupts dimerization or JAK2 coupling, blocking the entire downstream cascade. High GH accumulates because IGF-1 fails to exert negative feedback on the hypothalamic-pituitary axis. Treatment is recombinant IGF-1, not GH. A signal-peptide defect is excluded because the receptor is expressed and binds GH normally. GH does not block the IGF-1 receptor, GH receptor signaling does not require clathrin-mediated internalization, and somatostatin tone does not suppress hepatic IGF-1 transcription.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Laron syndrome = GH receptor mutation → high GH, low IGF-1, short stature. Contrast with GH deficiency (low GH AND low IGF-1). Treatment with recombinant IGF-1 (mecasermin). COMLEX: Distinguish from hypothyroidism-related growth failure; Laron-specific clue is the markedly elevated GH despite short stature.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — IGF-1 mediating growth hormone effects and comparing duration of GH versus IGF-1 action","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"GH does not block the IGF-1 re","C":"GH does not block the IGF-1 re","D":"GH does not block the IGF-1 re","E":"A signal-peptide defect is exc"}},{"id":"sdl3_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe negative and positive feedback regulation of hormone secretion and provide clinical examples of each mechanism.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman presents with a 6-month history of fatigue, cold intolerance, constipation, and 8-pound weight gain. Examination shows periorbital puffiness, dry skin, and delayed relaxation phase of deep tendon reflexes. Laboratory studies: TSH 52 mIU/L, free T4 0.28 ng/dL. Thyroid peroxidase antibodies are markedly elevated. Which of the following best explains the markedly elevated TSH in this patient?","choices":{"C":"Loss of functional thyroid parenchyma removes T4-mediated negative feedback on hypothalamic TRH secretion and on thyrotroph sensitivity, so TRH drives continuous TSH release in an attempt to restore thyroid hormone levels","A":"Autoimmune destruction of thyroid follicular cells releases a burst of preformed T4 into circulation, triggering an acute negative feedback surge at the pituitary that transiently suppresses TSH before the chronic elevation begins","B":"Low serum T4 increases peripheral deiodination toward the inactive reverse T3 isoform, which directly binds and activates TSH receptors on anterior pituitary thyrotrophs in a compensatory positive feedback loop","D":"The thyroid peroxidase antibodies cross-react with TSH receptors on pituitary thyrotrophs, mimicking TSH receptor activation and driving autonomous TSH production independent of hypothalamic TRH stimulation","E":"Hypothyroid states reduce hepatic conjugation and biliary excretion of TSH glycoproteins, extending the serum half-life of already-secreted TSH and elevating measured serum concentrations without increasing thyrotroph secretory rate"},"explanation":"This is Hashimoto thyroiditis with resultant primary hypothyroidism. T3 and T4 normally exert negative feedback at both the hypothalamus (suppressing TRH) and the anterior pituitary (reducing thyrotroph sensitivity to TRH and suppressing TSH transcription). When thyroid hormone falls, this inhibitory brake is removed and TRH drives continuously elevated TSH production. TSH elevation is the most sensitive early marker of primary hypothyroidism. Reverse T3 does not activate TSH receptors — it is biologically inactive. Thyroid peroxidase antibodies do not act on pituitary TSH receptors, TSH is not cleared by biliary conjugation, and a burst of preformed T4 from follicle destruction would suppress, not raise, TSH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary hypothyroidism = high TSH, low T4. Central (2° or 3°) hypothyroidism = low/normal TSH with low T4. The TSH level distinguishes primary from central. COMLEX: Hashimoto is an autoimmune thyroiditis; anti-TPO and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies are the hallmark. Thyroid viscerosomatic reflex levels are C4–C6 and T2, with sympathetic supply from T1–T4 (OMM atlas Ch 15), vs the head-and-neck range T1–T5 (OMM course map); posterior Chapman point T2 (course Week 5 chart).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis, TRH/TSH roles, and negative feedback regulation by T3/T4","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"B":"Reverse T3 does not activate T","D":"Thyroid peroxidase antibodies ","E":"Thyroid peroxidase antibodies "}},{"id":"sdl3_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe negative and positive feedback regulation of hormone secretion and provide clinical examples of each mechanism.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman undergoing hormonal monitoring for infertility evaluation has serial serum measurements performed. On day 10 her estradiol is 120 pg/mL with normal LH of 8 mIU/mL. On day 12 her estradiol peaks at 480 pg/mL. On day 13, her LH surges to 95 mIU/mL and she ovulates on day 14. Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which sustained high estradiol triggered the LH surge?","choices":{"A":"Estradiol concentrations sustained above a threshold for 2 to 3 days switch anterior pituitary gonadotrophs from inhibitory to stimulatory sensitivity, such that GnRH pulses now elicit amplified LH secretion rather than the tonic suppression seen at lower estradiol levels","B":"Rapidly declining estradiol in the 24 hours after the follicular peak removes tonic negative feedback from pituitary gonadotrophs, triggering a compensatory rebound burst in GnRH secretion that drives the LH surge and leads to follicle rupture","C":"Peak estradiol directly binds GnRH receptors on hypothalamic neurons and functions as a competitive partial agonist, mimicking GnRH-receptor signaling and independently generating an LH secretory surge without requiring elevated GnRH pulse frequency","D":"The follicular estradiol peak sharply suppresses inhibin B secretion from granulosa cells, fully disinhibiting pituitary FSH release, and the resulting FSH rise cross-reacts with LH receptors on the preovulatory follicle to substitute for the LH surge","E":"Midcycle estradiol activates adenylyl cyclase through a Gs-alpha-coupled estrogen receptor isoform on pituitary thyrotrophs, generating cAMP that cross-activates LH synthesis and secretion through a PKA-CREB mechanism"},"explanation":"The LH surge is the paradigmatic positive feedback event in reproductive endocrinology. The key mechanistic principle is that estradiol must be both above a threshold level (at least 200 pg/mL per Costanzo Ch 10) and sustained, about 2 to 3 days above a critical rate in Guyton Ch 82, to switch pituitary gonadotrophs from their baseline negative-feedback state to positive-feedback amplification of GnRH-driven LH secretion. The surge does not follow a fall in estradiol — it begins while estradiol is still at or near peak. Estradiol does not directly bind GnRH receptors, inhibin B suppression and FSH cross-reactivity do not drive the LH surge, and a Gs-coupled estrogen receptor on thyrotrophs involves the wrong cell type and the wrong mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LH secretion rises for 1 to 2 days beginning 24 to 48 hours before ovulation and peaks about 16 hours before it (Guyton Ch 82). Sustained high estradiol = positive feedback. Know this timing for ovulation predictor kits and infertility board questions. COMLEX: Oral contraceptives suppress the LH surge (and FSH) by maintaining stable estrogen/progestin levels that prevent the threshold estradiol pattern required for positive feedback.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — hypothalamic releasing hormones controlling anterior pituitary secretion of hormones that regulate target endocrine glands","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"The surge does not follow a fa","C":"Estradiol does not directly bi","D":"Estradiol does not directly bi"}},{"id":"sdl3_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe negative and positive feedback regulation of hormone secretion and provide clinical examples of each mechanism.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman presents with 18 months of progressive central weight gain, purple abdominal striae, proximal muscle weakness, easy bruising, and hypertension. 24-hour urinary free cortisol is markedly elevated at 380 mcg/day. A 1-mg overnight dexamethasone suppression test fails to suppress morning cortisol below 2 mcg/dL. MRI reveals a 7-mm pituitary adenoma. Which of the following best explains why cortisol remains continuously elevated despite cortisol levels that would normally suppress ACTH secretion?","choices":{"D":"The adenoma cells carry somatic mutations that impair glucocorticoid receptor-mediated suppression of POMC transcription, making the adenoma relatively resistant to cortisol negative feedback while the surrounding normal corticotrophs remain suppressed","A":"The adenoma cells overexpress CRH receptors on their surface, allowing hypothalamic CRH to perpetually override the inhibitory cortisol signal and drive continuous ACTH synthesis despite markedly elevated circulating cortisol concentrations","B":"Elevated cortisol from the adenoma promotes autonomous hyperplasia of the zona fasciculata, which then secretes ACTH independently of the pituitary to self-perpetuate adrenal cortisol output and complete a circular positive feedback loop","C":"The adenoma cells constitutively activate adenylyl cyclase through a mutant Gs-alpha subunit, generating cAMP that drives PKA-mediated phosphorylation of CREB and continuous POMC transcription independent of CRH receptor occupancy","E":"High cortisol concentrations saturate cortisol-binding globulin in plasma, dramatically increasing biologically active free cortisol, which then paradoxically up-regulates glucocorticoid receptor expression on adenoma cells and increases their sensitivity"},"explanation":"Cushing disease (pituitary corticotroph adenoma) is caused by a corticotroph adenoma that secretes ACTH autonomously because the normal glucocorticoid-mediated suppression of POMC gene transcription is impaired by somatic mutations in the adenoma cells (commonly USP8, CABLES1, or NR3C1 mutations). This explains why high-dose dexamethasone (but not low-dose) may eventually suppress cortisol — the feedback threshold is shifted upward, not eliminated. Adrenal cells do not produce ACTH. McCune-Albright syndrome involves Gs-alpha mutations but that is not the mechanism of Cushing disease. After 1 mg of dexamethasone, normal morning cortisol is usually <2 mcg/dL and in Cushing syndrome usually >5 mcg/dL (Katzung Ch 39).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cushing disease (pituitary) vs Cushing syndrome (ectopic or adrenal) vs exogenous — distinguish by ACTH level and suppression tests. High-dose dexamethasone suppresses pituitary adenoma (partially) but not ectopic or adrenal sources. COMLEX: Classic Cushingoid features — moon facies, buffalo hump, striae, hypertension, hyperglycemia, and proximal myopathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis including CRH, ACTH, cortisol secretion, negative feedback, and circadian rhythm","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"B":"Adrenal cells do not produce A","C":"McCune-Albright syndrome invol"}},{"id":"sdl3_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe enzyme-linked receptor signaling that occurs through associated intracellular kinases, using the leptin receptor (JAK-STAT pathway) as a model, including receptor dimerization, kinase activation, and gene transcription.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with class III obesity and a BMI of 43 kg/m2 undergoes metabolic evaluation. Despite a serum leptin level of 62 ng/mL (markedly elevated), she reports persistent hyperphagia and has gained 18 pounds over the past year. Hypothalamic biopsy studies in similar patients show markedly increased expression of suppressor of cytokine signaling 3 protein. Which of the following best explains how elevated SOCS3 expression causes leptin resistance in this patient?","choices":{"A":"SOCS3 promotes clathrin-mediated endocytosis of unoccupied leptin receptors by recruiting beta-arrestin to the unliganded receptor, reducing surface receptor density before leptin can bind and initiate dimerization","B":"SOCS3 binds the extracellular domain of the leptin receptor and competitively inhibits leptin from occupying its binding site, reducing receptor occupancy and preventing the conformational change required to initiate JAK2 association and receptor dimerization","C":"SOCS3 transfers a phosphate group from its own ATP-binding domain to the JAK2 activation loop tyrosine residues, locking JAK2 in a stably phosphorylated but catalytically inactive conformation that cannot phosphorylate STAT3","D":"SOCS3 binds to phosphotyrosine residues on the activated leptin receptor-JAK2 complex and sterically blocks STAT3 recruitment and phosphorylation, preventing the nuclear translocation required for satiety-promoting gene transcription in hypothalamic neurons","E":"SOCS3 functions as a transcription repressor in the nucleus and directly occupies the promoter region of the leptin receptor gene in hypothalamic neurons, suppressing new receptor protein synthesis and reducing available surface receptors"},"explanation":"SOCS3 is an inducible negative regulator of JAK-STAT signaling. It is up-regulated by the very STAT3 activation it suppresses, creating an auto-regulatory brake. Mechanistically, SOCS3 binds phosphotyrosine residues on the leptin receptor intracellular tail and on JAK2, and can also directly inhibit JAK2 catalytic activity through its kinase inhibitory region. By blocking STAT3 docking, it prevents STAT3 phosphorylation and nuclear translocation. In obesity, chronically elevated SOCS3 (induced by inflammatory cytokines, free fatty acids, and hyperinsulinemia) sustains this block. SOCS3 is intracellular and does not reach the extracellular domain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SOCS proteins are the negative regulators of JAK-STAT — they are induced by the same STATs they inhibit (negative feedback within the pathway). SOCS3 is the primary mediator of leptin resistance. COMLEX: Leptin resistance (obesity, leptin-receptor mutation) has high serum leptin; congenital leptin deficiency has low or undetectable leptin and responds to recombinant leptin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — major stimulators and inhibitors of growth hormone secretion and regulation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl3_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe enzyme-linked receptor signaling that occurs through associated intracellular kinases, using the leptin receptor (JAK-STAT pathway) as a model, including receptor dimerization, kinase activation, and gene transcription.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man presents with headache, facial plethora, and pruritus that worsens after a warm shower. CBC shows hemoglobin 20.4 g/dL, hematocrit 62%, and platelets 840,000/mcL. Serum erythropoietin is undetectable. Bone marrow biopsy confirms trilineage hypercellularity with a megakaryocyte surplus. Molecular testing identifies a JAK2 V617F mutation. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of erythroid overproduction in this patient?","choices":{"B":"The V617F substitution in the JAK2 pseudokinase domain disrupts autoinhibition, producing constitutive JAK2 kinase activity and continuous STAT5 phosphorylation in hematopoietic progenitor cells without erythropoietin receptor activation","A":"The V617F mutation creates a novel high-affinity erythropoietin binding site on the extracellular domain of the EPO receptor, dramatically lowering the threshold EPO concentration required to initiate erythroid progenitor proliferation","C":"The mutation blocks SOCS3-mediated ubiquitination and proteasomal targeting of phosphorylated JAK2, prolonging the duration of STAT5 activation after each individual erythropoietin-receptor binding event without altering baseline signaling","D":"The mutation destabilizes the JAK2-receptor interaction interface, releasing free soluble JAK2 into the cytoplasm where it phosphorylates STAT5 in a diffuse, receptor-independent manner that scales with total cellular JAK2 protein levels","E":"The V617F substitution enhances JAK2 selectivity for STAT3 at the expense of STAT5, redirecting erythroid progenitor signaling from differentiation toward survival and proliferation pathways that increase steady-state red cell output"},"explanation":"Polycythemia vera is caused by a gain-of-function JAK2 V617F point mutation (valine to phenylalanine at position 617) in the JAK2 pseudokinase domain (JH2). The JH2 domain normally autoinhibits the JH1 (kinase) domain; V617F disrupts this autoinhibition, producing constitutively active JAK2 that signals continuously through STAT5 to drive erythroid, megakaryocyte, and myeloid proliferation without requiring EPO or other cytokines. This is why EPO is suppressed — erythropoiesis is autonomous. The mutation is in JAK2 itself, not in the EPO receptor extracellular domain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PV = JAK2 V617F mutation → constitutive JAK-STAT → trilineage hyperplasia. Low EPO distinguishes from secondary polycythemia (EPO elevated from chronic hypoxia or EPO-secreting tumor). Treatment: phlebotomy plus JAK2 inhibitor ruxolitinib for high-risk disease. COMLEX: Aquagenic pruritus after bathing is the classic symptom; elevated basophil count is also a clue.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — matching endocrine hormones to receptor type including non-receptor tyrosine kinase family","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl3_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe enzyme-linked receptor signaling that occurs through associated intracellular kinases, using the leptin receptor (JAK-STAT pathway) as a model, including receptor dimerization, kinase activation, and gene transcription.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man with biopsy-proven acromegaly and markedly elevated IGF-1 (820 ng/mL) has failed maximal somatostatin analog therapy. He is started on pegvisomant, a modified GH molecule. Over 6 months his IGF-1 normalizes to 180 ng/mL and his ring size decreases, though his serum GH rises from 18 to 42 ng/mL. Which of the following best explains pegvisomant's mechanism of action and the paradoxical rise in serum GH?","choices":{"D":"Pegvisomant binds the GH receptor and allows receptor dimerization but blocks the conformational change needed for signaling, so JAK2-STAT5b-driven IGF-1 transcription falls; lower IGF-1 removes negative feedback on somatotrophs, so GH secretion rises","A":"Pegvisomant inhibits somatostatin receptors on pituitary somatotrophs, releasing the inhibitory brake on GH secretion while simultaneously blocking peripheral GH receptor tyrosine kinase autophosphorylation to prevent hepatic IGF-1 gene transcription","B":"Pegvisomant binds and sequesters free circulating IGF-1 molecules, preventing IGF-1 from acting on peripheral tissues, while paradoxically stimulating hypothalamic GHRH secretion through an uncharacterized IGF-1 receptor feedback circuit","C":"Pegvisomant competitively inhibits the IGF-1 receptor on hepatocytes, preventing IGF-1 from initiating its own downstream signaling, while also triggering GH receptor internalization and lysosomal degradation to reduce available receptor surface density","E":"Pegvisomant acts as a partial GH receptor agonist, activating just enough JAK2-STAT5b signaling to reduce IGF-1 transcription through a paradoxical STAT5b-mediated auto-suppression pathway that does not require full receptor dimerization"},"explanation":"Pegvisomant is a GH receptor antagonist — it binds the GH receptor (and does so with higher affinity than native GH) but the modification (pegylation plus point mutations) gives it one high-affinity and one low-affinity receptor binding site, so it allows the initial step (receptor dimerization) but prevents the conformational change required for signal transduction (Katzung Ch 37). Without that change, JAK2 cannot be activated, STAT5b is not phosphorylated, and the liver cannot transcribe IGF-1. Because IGF-1 falls and no longer exerts negative feedback on pituitary somatotrophs (and on hypothalamic GHRH/somatostatin balance), GH secretion rises — this is expected and not a sign of treatment failure. IGF-1 (not GH) is used to monitor treatment efficacy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pegvisomant is a GH receptor antagonist — monitor with IGF-1, not GH (GH rises appropriately). Contrast with somatostatin analogs (octreotide, lanreotide) which lower GH and IGF-1 by suppressing somatotroph secretion. COMLEX: Acromegaly features — coarse facies, enlarged hands/feet, prognathism, hypertension, diabetes, carpal tunnel syndrome, colonic polyps.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — details of growth hormone receptor signaling through JAK2-STAT5b and the role of IGF-1 feedback","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"}],"4":[{"id":"sdl4_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the embryology and anatomical relationship between the hypothalamus and pituitary gland and identify the two distinct lobes of the pituitary.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the anatomical location of the pituitary gland and its connection to the hypothalamus?","choices":{"D":"The pituitary gland lies in the sella turcica, a bony cavity at the base of the skull formed by the sphenoid bone, and is connected to the overlying hypothalamus by the hypophysial stalk","A":"The pituitary gland lies in the optic canal of the sphenoid bone and is connected to the hypothalamus by the optic tract, which serves a dual role in transmitting both visual signals and hormonal messages to the gland","B":"The pituitary gland is suspended in the subarachnoid space adjacent to the cerebellopontine angle and receives hypothalamic hormone signals via the perivascular space running along the basilar artery","C":"The pituitary gland lies within the cavernous sinus bilaterally and is attached to the hypothalamus through a vascular plexus that runs through the floor of the third ventricle above the optic chiasm","E":"The pituitary gland is located in the petrous portion of the temporal bone adjacent to the carotid canal and receives hypothalamic hormone signals by diffusion through the cerebrospinal fluid of the fourth ventricle"},"explanation":"The pituitary gland (hypophysis) is approximately 1 cm in diameter and weighs 0.5 to 1 gram. It is seated in the sella turcica, a saddle-shaped bony cavity in the sphenoid bone at the base of the skull, and is connected to the hypothalamus above by the hypophysial stalk. The optic chiasm lies just anterior and superior to the pituitary, explaining why pituitary masses cause bitemporal visual field defects. The gland is not located in the cavernous sinus, optic canal, petrous temporal bone, or subarachnoid space remote from the sella.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sella turcica is the key anatomical landmark — pituitary adenomas expand within it and can compress the optic chiasm causing bitemporal hemianopia, and can invade the adjacent cavernous sinus causing CN III, IV, V1/V2, VI palsies. COMLEX: Sella turcica is a sphenoid bone structure; sinus headaches from sphenoid sinusitis can mimic pituitary pathology on clinical exam.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — pituitary anatomy and embryology: the pituitary gland lies in the sella turcica and connects to the hypothalamus via the hypophysial stalk; anterior pituitary arises from Rathke's pouch (oral ectoderm, epithelioid cells); posterior pituitary arises from neural tissue outgrowth of the hypothalamus (glial-type cells); pars intermedia lies between the two lobes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"The gland is not located in th","B":"The gland is not located in th","C":"The gland is not located in th"}},{"id":"sdl4_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the embryology and anatomical relationship between the hypothalamus and pituitary gland and identify the two distinct lobes of the pituitary.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly pairs each lobe of the pituitary gland with its embryological origin?","choices":{"B":"The anterior pituitary originates as a downward neural tissue outgrowth from the floor of the diencephalon, while the posterior pituitary originates as an upward invagination of the pharyngeal epithelium that fuses with it from below","A":"The anterior pituitary originates from neural crest cells that migrate from the mesencephalon during neurulation, while the posterior pituitary originates from mesoderm of the second pharyngeal arch that ascends toward the developing diencephalon","C":"Both the anterior and posterior pituitary originate from the same Rathke's pouch invagination, with differentiation into the two lobes determined entirely by local inductive signals from the adjacent neural tissue after the pouch closes","D":"The anterior pituitary originates from Rathke's pouch, an upward invagination of the pharyngeal epithelium (oral ectoderm), while the posterior pituitary originates as a downward neural tissue outgrowth from the floor of the hypothalamus","E":"The anterior pituitary originates from the neural plate of the diencephalon along with the thalamus and hypothalamus, while the posterior pituitary originates from the roof plate of the stomodeum during the sixth week of embryological development"},"explanation":"The two lobes of the pituitary have completely different embryological origins. The anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) arises from Rathke's pouch, which is an upward evagination of the ectoderm of the roof of the pharynx (oral ectoderm); this explains why its cells have an epithelioid character. The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) arises from a downward neural tissue outgrowth from the hypothalamus; this explains why it contains large numbers of glial-type cells. This distinction is directly clinically relevant: craniopharyngiomas arise from persistent Rathke's pouch remnants.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior pituitary = oral ectoderm (Rathke pouch); posterior pituitary = neuroectoderm (neural tissue). FA mnemonic: \"Rathke's pouch = Rathke rises from the Roof of the mouth.\" Craniopharyngioma arises from Rathke's pouch remnants — suprasellar calcification + bitemporal hemianopia in a child or adolescent. COMLEX: Rathke's pouch cysts are benign incidentalomas from the same remnant tissue.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — pituitary anatomy and embryology: the pituitary gland lies in the sella turcica and connects to the hypothalamus via the hypophysial stalk; anterior pituitary arises from Rathke's pouch (oral ectoderm, epithelioid cells); posterior pituitary arises from neural tissue outgrowth of the hypothalamus (glial-type cells); pars intermedia lies between the two lobes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl4_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the embryology and anatomical relationship between the hypothalamus and pituitary gland and identify the two distinct lobes of the pituitary.","batch":1,"stem":"A histology section of the pituitary shows a thin collar of glandular cells wrapped around the infundibulum, continuous below with the main mass of the anterior lobe. Which statement best describes the identity and embryologic origin of this collar?","choices":{"A":"Pars tuberalis, derived from the thickened lateral walls of Rathke's pouch, which forms a sheath around the infundibulum in the anterior lobe","B":"Pars intermedia, derived from the thin posterior wall of Rathke's pouch, which encircles the stalk and belongs to the posterior lobe of the gland","C":"Pars nervosa, derived from a downgrowth of diencephalic neuroectoderm, which wraps the stalk and holds the axon endings of magnocellular neurons","D":"Pars distalis, derived from the thickened anterior wall of Rathke's pouch, which spreads up along the stalk and makes up most of the stalk tissue","E":"Median eminence, derived from the floor of the third ventricle, which forms a glandular cuff that secretes releasing hormones"},"explanation":"The anterior lobe (adenohypophysis) has three parts, all derived from Rathke's pouch, an evagination of oropharyngeal ectoderm: the pars distalis (the bulk of the lobe, from the thickened anterior wall), the pars intermedia (a thin remnant of the posterior wall), and the pars tuberalis (from the thickened lateral walls), which forms a collar or sheath around the infundibulum (Pawlina Ch 21). The posterior lobe (neurohypophysis) consists of the pars nervosa and the infundibulum, both from a downgrowth of neuroectoderm from the floor of the third ventricle. The pars intermedia is part of the anterior lobe and lies between the pars distalis and pars nervosa; it does not encircle the stalk. The pars nervosa is neural tissue holding axon endings, not a glandular collar. The pars distalis is the main body of the anterior lobe, not the stalk sheath. The median eminence is hypothalamic tissue where releasing hormones enter the primary capillary plexus; it is not a glandular cuff of pituitary cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior lobe = pars distalis + pars intermedia + pars tuberalis, all from Rathke's pouch (oral ectoderm); posterior lobe = pars nervosa + infundibulum, from neuroectoderm of the floor of the third ventricle (Pawlina Ch 21). COMLEX: The hypophyseal portal veins run along the pars tuberalis to reach the pars distalis (Pawlina Ch 21), so a stalk lesion can interrupt both the portal route and the hypothalamohypophyseal tract.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — hypophyseal portal veins run along the pars tuberalis to the sinusoids of the pars distalis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"D":"The pars distalis is the main ","E":"The median eminence is hypotha"}},{"id":"sdl4_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal system and describe how it delivers hypothalamic hormones to the anterior pituitary.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the primary functional role of the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal system?","choices":{"B":"The portal system delivers hypothalamic releasing and inhibitory hormones secreted at the median eminence directly to the anterior pituitary sinuses via portal blood vessels, bypassing systemic dilution and enabling high local hormone concentrations that efficiently control anterior pituitary glandular cell secretion","A":"The portal system allows anterior pituitary hormones to travel retrograde from the pituitary sinuses back to the hypothalamus via portal vessels, where they directly modulate the activity of releasing hormone neurons through a short-loop negative feedback mechanism","C":"The portal system delivers oxygenated arterial blood from the internal carotid artery through a primary capillary plexus in the sella turcica to supply the metabolic and oxygen needs of all anterior pituitary cell types and their secretory apparatus","D":"The portal system transmits electrical action potentials from hypothalamic peptidergic neurons to anterior pituitary secretory cells via gap junctions in the perivascular space that runs along the outer surface of the hypophysial stalk","E":"The portal system transports ADH and oxytocin synthesized in the posterior pituitary directly to the anterior pituitary, where they function as paracrine modulators that enhance trophic hormone secretion in adjacent gonadotrope and corticotrope cells"},"explanation":"The anterior pituitary is a highly vascular gland. Blood entering its sinuses first passes through a capillary plexus in the lower hypothalamus at the median eminence, then flows through the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal vessels down the pituitary stalk into the anterior pituitary sinuses. Hypothalamic neurons secrete releasing and inhibitory hormones directly into this primary capillary plexus, so the anterior pituitary receives these hormones at concentrations far higher than systemic levels, enabling precise control of secretion. The anterior pituitary's blood supply is portal, not direct arterial; posterior pituitary hormones travel axonally, not via the portal system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Portal system is the key anatomical link between hypothalamus and anterior pituitary. Stalk compression (by tumor, trauma) interrupts portal delivery of releasing hormones, causing hypopituitarism — except for prolactin, which paradoxically rises because its inhibitory signal (dopamine) is also blocked. COMLEX: Disruption of the stalk is a classic \"stalk section effect\" scenario tested on COMLEX physiology questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — hypothalamic-hypophysial portal system: hypothalamic neurons secrete releasing and inhibitory hormones into the primary capillary plexus at the median eminence; portal vessels carry these hormones down the pituitary stalk to the anterior pituitary sinuses; this bypasses systemic dilution; prolactin is uniquely under tonic dopamine inhibition via the portal system","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"The anterior pituitary's blood","E":"The anterior pituitary's blood"}},{"id":"sdl4_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal system and describe how it delivers hypothalamic hormones to the anterior pituitary.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following most accurately describes the median eminence and its role in controlling anterior pituitary secretion?","choices":{"C":"The median eminence is the lowermost portion of the hypothalamus, joining the pituitary stalk; hypothalamic neurons terminate here and secrete releasing and inhibitory hormones into the primary capillary plexus, which drains into portal vessels to the anterior pituitary","A":"The median eminence is a paired hypothalamic nucleus located posteriorly near the mammillary bodies; it synthesizes ADH and oxytocin and packages them into secretory granules that are released into the bloodstream in response to osmotic stimuli from adjacent osmoreceptors","B":"The median eminence is the anatomical name for the pituitary stalk itself, which is composed entirely of myelinated axons of magnocellular neurons carrying ADH and oxytocin bound to neurophysin carrier proteins down to the posterior pituitary for storage and release","D":"The median eminence lies behind a tight blood-brain barrier that prevents peripheral hormones from reaching hypothalamic neurons, so feedback from target-gland hormones acts only at the level of the anterior pituitary","E":"The median eminence is the secondary capillary plexus located within the anterior pituitary sinuses that receives portal blood and delivers hypothalamic hormones to glandular cells; it serves as the terminal interface between portal blood and anterior pituitary secretory cells at the level of the sella turcica"},"explanation":"The median eminence is the lowermost part of the hypothalamus and serves as the functional link between the hypothalamus and the anterior pituitary. Hypothalamic neurons originating from various areas of the hypothalamus send axons to the median eminence and the adjacent tuber cinereum. These nerve terminals release hypothalamic releasing and inhibitory hormones into the surrounding tissue fluid, where they are immediately absorbed into the primary capillary plexus. These vessels coalesce into the portal blood vessels that run down the pituitary stalk. The median eminence is not the stalk itself, nor is it in the posterior hypothalamus or within the pituitary. It is also not shielded by a tight blood-brain barrier: as a circumventricular organ it lets circulating hormones reach hypothalamic neurons for feedback.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypothalamic hormones are secreted at the median eminence into the primary capillary plexus — this is the first \"gate\" of the portal system. Know that the median eminence is not in the pituitary gland but rather is the hypothalamic end of the portal axis. COMLEX: The median eminence is a circumventricular organ (no blood-brain barrier), which allows systemic signals to reach hypothalamic neurons — important for feedback from peripheral hormones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — hypothalamic-hypophysial portal system: hypothalamic neurons secrete releasing and inhibitory hormones into the primary capillary plexus at the median eminence; portal vessels carry these hormones down the pituitary stalk to the anterior pituitary sinuses; this bypasses systemic dilution; prolactin is uniquely under tonic dopamine inhibition via the portal system","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"The median eminence is not the","B":"The median eminence is not the","E":"The median eminence is not the"}},{"id":"sdl4_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal system and describe how it delivers hypothalamic hormones to the anterior pituitary.","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher surgically removes the pituitary gland from an animal and transplants it to a well-vascularized site in the neck, completely severing all connections to the hypothalamus. Which pattern of anterior pituitary hormone secretion would be expected after the gland has re-established its blood supply at the new location?","choices":{"D":"Secretion of all anterior pituitary hormones increases dramatically because the gland is freed from tonic hypothalamic inhibitory control; all tropic hormones rise to supraphysiologic levels within days of transplantation to the ectopic vascular site","A":"Secretion of most anterior pituitary hormones falls to very low levels because hypothalamic releasing hormones can no longer reach the transplanted gland, but prolactin secretion rises above baseline because tonic inhibition by hypothalamic dopamine is also lost","B":"Secretion of all anterior pituitary hormones continues at near-normal levels because the fully differentiated pituitary gland has intrinsic autonomous secretory capacity and does not require continuous hypothalamic releasing hormone stimulation for basal hormone production","C":"Secretion of all anterior pituitary hormones falls to very low levels and remains permanently suppressed at the ectopic site; no single hormone is spared because every anterior pituitary cell type requires continuous releasing hormone stimulation and none have intrinsic tonic secretory activity","E":"Secretion of TSH, ACTH, and GH falls to very low levels while FSH, LH, and prolactin continue at normal or elevated levels because gonadotrope and lactotrope cells possess an intrinsic autonomous secretory rhythm that does not depend on hypothalamic input"},"explanation":"This transplantation experiment is a classic demonstration of the portal system's essential role. When removed from hypothalamic portal control: most anterior pituitary hormones (GH, TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH) fall to very low levels because their releasing hormones (GHRH, TRH, CRH, GnRH) can no longer reach them. Prolactin, however, is the exception — it is primarily under tonic inhibitory control by dopamine (prolactin inhibitory hormone, PIH). When the portal connection is lost, dopamine cannot reach the lactotropes, so prolactin secretion actually increases. This unique prolactin physiology explains why stalk section and dopamine antagonist drugs both cause hyperprolactinemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prolactin is the only anterior pituitary hormone under predominant tonic inhibition (by dopamine). Every other anterior pituitary hormone is under net stimulatory hypothalamic control. This is why dopamine agonists (bromocriptine, cabergoline) treat prolactinomas by restoring inhibition, and why antipsychotics (dopamine antagonists) cause galactorrhea. COMLEX: Prolactin excess from any cause of stalk disruption is the \"stalk effect\" — always check for this in suprasellar mass cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — hypothalamic-hypophysial portal system: hypothalamic neurons secrete releasing and inhibitory hormones into the primary capillary plexus at the median eminence; portal vessels carry these hormones down the pituitary stalk to the anterior pituitary sinuses; this bypasses systemic dilution; prolactin is uniquely under tonic dopamine inhibition via the portal system","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"C":"Prolactin, however, is the exc","E":"When removed from hypothalamic"}},{"id":"sdl4_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the synthesis of posterior pituitary hormones in hypothalamic nuclei and their transport to the posterior pituitary.","batch":1,"stem":"How does the mechanism by which the hypothalamus controls posterior pituitary secretion fundamentally differ from its control of anterior pituitary secretion?","choices":{"A":"Posterior pituitary secretion is controlled by direct nerve signals from magnocellular hypothalamic neurons whose axons project through the hypophysial stalk and terminate in the posterior pituitary, releasing ADH and oxytocin directly into adjacent capillaries; anterior pituitary secretion is controlled by hypothalamic hormones transported via the portal blood vessels","B":"Posterior pituitary secretion is controlled by hypothalamic releasing hormones that travel via the portal system and bind to pituicyte receptors, whereas anterior pituitary secretion is regulated by direct electrical stimulation from preganglionic sympathetic neurons originating in the lateral hypothalamus","C":"Posterior pituitary secretion is regulated exclusively by peripheral hormone feedback acting directly on the gland itself without requiring any hypothalamic input, whereas anterior pituitary secretion requires continuous portal delivery of hypothalamic hormones combined with direct neural innervation from the suprachiasmatic nucleus","D":"Both lobes are regulated through the same hypothalamic-hypophysial portal system, but the posterior pituitary receives hormones at lower concentrations due to its location distal to the primary capillary plexus, resulting in a slower and less sensitive secretory response compared to the anterior pituitary","E":"Posterior pituitary secretion is controlled by releasing hormones secreted into the cerebrospinal fluid of the third ventricle that enter the gland via the choroid plexus, whereas anterior pituitary secretion is controlled by releasing hormones carried in the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal blood vessels to the adenohypophysis"},"explanation":"The two lobes of the pituitary are controlled through fundamentally different mechanisms. The posterior pituitary contains the axon terminals of large magnocellular neurons whose cell bodies lie in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus. When stimulated, these neurons fire action potentials that travel down axons through the stalk and release ADH or oxytocin directly into the capillaries of the posterior pituitary — this is direct neural control. In contrast, the anterior pituitary has no direct neural innervation from the hypothalamus; it is controlled entirely by chemical signals (releasing and inhibitory hormones) that travel via the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal blood vessels from the median eminence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior pituitary = neural control (axon terminals release hormone directly); anterior pituitary = hormonal/portal control (no direct nerve input). This distinction explains why posterior pituitary function can survive stalk lesions as long as the supraoptic/paraventricular neurons are intact and their axons can secrete from the median eminence portal capillaries. COMLEX: In central DI, the lesion is in the hypothalamic nuclei or the tract — not in the posterior pituitary gland itself.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — posterior pituitary hormone synthesis and transport: ADH and oxytocin are synthesized in magnocellular neurons of the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei; they are packaged with carrier proteins called neurophysins and transported by axonal flow down the hypophysial stalk to nerve terminals in the posterior pituitary for storage and stimulus-triggered release","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl4_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the synthesis of posterior pituitary hormones in hypothalamic nuclei and their transport to the posterior pituitary.","batch":1,"stem":"In which hypothalamic structures are ADH and oxytocin synthesized before being transported to the posterior pituitary gland?","choices":{"B":"Both ADH and oxytocin are synthesized in the cell bodies of large magnocellular neurons located in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus, then transported down axons to the posterior pituitary for storage and release","A":"ADH is synthesized by pituicytes within the posterior pituitary gland itself, while oxytocin is synthesized in the paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus and transported down axons to the posterior pituitary, where both are co-stored in Herring bodies prior to release","C":"ADH is synthesized in the arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus in response to circadian osmotic signals, while oxytocin is synthesized in the ventromedial nucleus of the hypothalamus in response to suckling or uterine stretch, and both are transported via portal vessels to the posterior pituitary","D":"Both ADH and oxytocin are synthesized in chromaffin cells located within the posterior pituitary gland itself and are secreted directly into the adjacent capillaries in response to nerve signals arriving via the hypothalamo-neurohypophysial tract","E":"ADH is synthesized in the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus, which coordinates circadian rhythms of water balance, while oxytocin is synthesized in the dorsomedial nucleus of the hypothalamus, and both are packaged with neurophysins for transport to the posterior pituitary"},"explanation":"ADH (vasopressin) and oxytocin are both synthesized in the cell bodies of magnocellular neurons in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus. Both nuclei produce both hormones, with ADH predominating in the supraoptic nucleus and oxytocin predominating in the paraventricular nucleus. After synthesis, the hormones are packaged with carrier proteins (neurophysins) into secretory vesicles and transported by axonal flow down the hypothalamo-neurohypophysial tract through the hypophysial stalk to axon terminals in the posterior pituitary. The posterior pituitary itself contains no secretory cell bodies — it is purely a storage and release site.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Supraoptic nucleus is more closely associated with ADH (osmotic regulation); paraventricular nucleus contributes heavily to both ADH and oxytocin (also projects to brainstem and spinal cord for autonomic control). Both nuclei produce both hormones. COMLEX: Damage to the supraoptic nucleus or the hypothalamo-neurohypophysial tract causes central DI; damage to the posterior pituitary itself often does not because the axons can release ADH from the median eminence portal capillaries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — posterior pituitary hormone synthesis and transport: ADH and oxytocin are synthesized in magnocellular neurons of the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei; they are packaged with carrier proteins called neurophysins and transported by axonal flow down the hypophysial stalk to nerve terminals in the posterior pituitary for storage and stimulus-triggered release","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"The posterior pituitary itself"}},{"id":"sdl4_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the synthesis of posterior pituitary hormones in hypothalamic nuclei and their transport to the posterior pituitary.","batch":1,"stem":"After ADH and oxytocin are synthesized in hypothalamic nuclei, by which mechanism are they transported to the posterior pituitary for storage and eventual stimulus-triggered release?","choices":{"E":"ADH and oxytocin are packaged with carrier proteins called neurophysins and transported within secretory vesicles down the axons of magnocellular neurons through the hypophysial stalk to nerve terminals in the posterior pituitary, where they are stored until action potentials trigger exocytotic release","A":"ADH and oxytocin are secreted into the primary capillary plexus at the median eminence and carried to the posterior pituitary through the same hypothalamic-hypophysial portal vessels used by anterior pituitary releasing hormones, then re-absorbed and stored in pituicyte secretory granules","B":"ADH and oxytocin are synthesized as inactive prohormones in the hypothalamic nuclei and secreted into the CSF of the third ventricle, where proteolytic enzymes cleave them to their active forms before they diffuse down a concentration gradient into the posterior pituitary sinuses","C":"ADH and oxytocin are released from hypothalamic neuron cell bodies into the hypothalamic extracellular fluid and diffuse passively down a concentration gradient through fenestrations in the blood-brain barrier of the pituitary stalk until they reach the posterior pituitary capillary bed","D":"ADH and oxytocin are bound to albumin and other plasma carrier proteins in hypothalamic capillaries and are transported to the posterior pituitary through fenestrated capillaries in the neurohypophysis, where they dissociate from their carriers and are sequestered by pituicytes for regulated storage and release"},"explanation":"ADH and oxytocin are synthesized as part of large prohormone precursors in the cell bodies of magnocellular neurons in the hypothalamus. Each hormone is co-synthesized with a specific neurophysin carrier protein. The hormone-neurophysin complexes are packaged into secretory vesicles and transported by slow axonal transport (axoplasmic flow) down the long axons of the hypothalamo-neurohypophysial tract through the hypophysial stalk. The vesicles accumulate in axon terminals (Herring bodies) in the posterior pituitary. When action potentials arrive, calcium-dependent exocytosis releases the hormones and their neurophysins into adjacent capillaries for systemic distribution. The neurophysins are co-released but have no independent hormonal activity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ADH and oxytocin are synthesized in hypothalamus and stored in posterior pituitary via neurophysins and axonal transport. \"Made in hypothalamus, stored in posterior pituitary\" is the classic board-yield fact. COMLEX: Neurophysin I is the carrier for oxytocin; neurophysin II carries ADH (vasopressin). This is a higher-order detail tested in biochemistry context.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — posterior pituitary hormone synthesis and transport: ADH and oxytocin are synthesized in magnocellular neurons of the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei; they are packaged with carrier proteins called neurophysins and transported by axonal flow down the hypophysial stalk to nerve terminals in the posterior pituitary for storage and stimulus-triggered release","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl4_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the embryology and anatomical relationship between the hypothalamus and pituitary gland and identify the two distinct lobes of the pituitary.","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-year-old boy is brought to the pediatrician for evaluation of short stature and delayed puberty. He has been experiencing progressive headaches and bilateral visual field deficits over the past 6 months. MRI reveals a 3.2-cm suprasellar mass with calcification and a cystic component compressing the optic chiasm. Serum IGF-1, testosterone, TSH, and cortisol are all low. Which of the following best explains the embryological origin of this tumor and why it causes anterior pituitary dysfunction?","choices":{"C":"Craniopharyngiomas arise from remnants of Rathke's pouch squamous epithelium in the suprasellar region; as they expand they compress the pituitary stalk and adenohypophysis, interrupting portal delivery of hypothalamic releasing hormones and causing panhypopituitarism","A":"Craniopharyngiomas arise from arachnoid cap cells lining the sellar diaphragm and expand inferiorly into the sella turcica, where direct cytotoxic compression of individual anterior pituitary cell types causes selective hormone deficiencies in a predictable order based on cell type vulnerability","B":"Craniopharyngiomas arise from ectopic thyroid tissue that failed to descend from the foramen cecum and instead lodged in the suprasellar space, where the mass secretes thyroid hormones that suppress the HPT axis and secondarily suppress the remaining anterior pituitary hormone axes through cross-reactivity","D":"Craniopharyngiomas arise from the posterior pituitary's neural progenitor cells and expand anteriorly into the adenohypophysis, causing panhypopituitarism by directly replacing secretory glandular cells with non-functional neural tumor tissue while the posterior pituitary function is paradoxically preserved","E":"Craniopharyngiomas arise from remnants of the craniopharyngeal canal, a bony channel through which the pituitary stalk normally descends; when this canal persists it traps the stalk and gradually compresses the pituitary gland against the floor of the sella without involving the suprasellar structures"},"explanation":"Craniopharyngiomas are benign epithelial tumors arising from squamous cell remnants of Rathke's pouch — the same oral ectoderm that gives rise to the anterior pituitary. They are the most common suprasellar tumor in children. Their characteristic features include calcification (visible on CT, often described as \"tooth-like\" or \"eggshell\"), a cystic component containing dark machine-oil fluid, and bitemporal hemianopia from optic chiasm compression. They cause anterior pituitary dysfunction primarily by compressing the pituitary stalk, which interrupts portal delivery of hypothalamic releasing hormones. The most commonly lost pituitary functions are GH (first), followed by gonadotropins, TSH, and finally ACTH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Craniopharyngioma = suprasellar calcified cystic tumor in children; from Rathke's pouch remnants. Presents with bitemporal hemianopia, growth failure, diabetes insipidus, and hypopituitarism. Cholesterol crystals (\"motor oil\" fluid) in the cyst is the classic pathology finding. COMLEX: Can also occur in adults (bimodal distribution: 5 to 15 years and 65 years or older per Robbins Ch 24; Bootcamp gives 5–14 for the childhood peak). Treat with surgery plus radiation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — clinicopathologic features of pituitary and suprasellar tumors including craniopharyngioma and their management","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl4_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the embryology and anatomical relationship between the hypothalamus and pituitary gland and identify the two distinct lobes of the pituitary.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man presents with progressive coarsening of facial features, enlarging hands and feet, new-onset hypertension, and impaired fasting glucose over 3 years. IGF-1 is markedly elevated at 890 ng/mL and GH fails to suppress below 1 ng/mL after a 75-gram oral glucose load. MRI reveals a 2.4-cm pituitary macroadenoma. A pathologist examines a biopsy specimen. Which of the following correctly describes the expected staining characteristics of the cells that produce this patient's primary pathology?","choices":{"E":"The adenoma cells would stain strongly with acid dyes because somatotropes, which produce growth hormone, are acidophilic; GH-secreting adenomas are therefore acidophilic tumors, the same staining class as prolactin-secreting lactotrope adenomas","A":"The adenoma cells would stain strongly with basic dyes because corticotropes are basophilic and this clinical presentation, including hypertension, insulin resistance, and facial changes, is consistent with a corticotrope adenoma causing excess ACTH and secondary adrenal hyperfunction","B":"The adenoma cells would stain strongly with basic dyes because TSH-secreting thyrotropes are basophilic, and excess TSH stimulates thyroid hyperfunction that produces secondary cardiovascular and metabolic effects including hypertension and glucose intolerance similar to this presentation","C":"The adenoma cells would show a mixed acidophilic and basophilic pattern because this presentation is consistent with a plurihormonal adenoma co-secreting both growth hormone and prolactin from mammosomatotrope cells that express markers of both acidophilic and basophilic lineages","D":"The adenoma cells would be chromophobic on routine hematoxylin and eosin staining because somatotropes lose their secretory granule content as they undergo neoplastic transformation, producing a chromophobe morphology despite continued growth hormone production at supraphysiologic levels"},"explanation":"This is a classic presentation of acromegaly (GH excess after epiphyseal fusion). Somatotropes are the cells that produce growth hormone and they stain strongly with acid dyes — they are acidophilic. Therefore, GH-secreting pituitary adenomas are called acidophilic tumors on histopathology. Similarly, lactotropes (prolactin-secreting cells) are also acidophilic. Corticotropes, thyrotropes, and gonadotropes are basophilic. Chromophobes are typically non-secretory or sparsely granulated. The FA mnemonic \"Acid PiG\" (Acidophils: Prolactin, GH) directly applies here. The glucose suppression test is the gold-standard confirmation test for acromegaly — GH should suppress below 1 ng/mL in normal subjects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FA mnemonics — Acid PiG: Acidophils secrete Prolactin and GH; B-FLAT: Basophils secrete FSH, LH, ACTH, TSH. Acromegaly features: coarse facies, enlarged hands/feet, prognathism, carpal tunnel, hypertension, diabetes, colonic polyps, increased cancer risk. GH fails to suppress on OGTT — gold standard diagnostic test. COMLEX: Sympathetic supply to the head and neck arises from T1–T4 (Foundations Ch 6); the OMM course map and OMM atlas Ch 15 list head and neck as T1–T5.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — the six major anterior pituitary hormones, their secreting cell types, and primary target organs","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Corticotropes, thyrotropes, an","B":"Corticotropes, thyrotropes, an","D":"Chromophobes are typically non"}},{"id":"sdl4_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the embryology and anatomical relationship between the hypothalamus and pituitary gland and identify the two distinct lobes of the pituitary.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman is admitted for management of severe postpartum hemorrhage following a prolonged vaginal delivery complicated by uterine atony. Three months later, she returns reporting that she has been unable to breastfeed since discharge, has not resumed menstrual cycles, and has developed progressive fatigue, cold intolerance, and decreased libido. Serum prolactin, FSH, LH, TSH, ACTH, and cortisol are all markedly low. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology underlying her presentation?","choices":{"A":"During labor, venous stasis in the cavernous sinus adjacent to the pituitary compresses the gland chronically over the course of pregnancy; the acute systemic hypotension of postpartum hemorrhage causes acute cavernous sinus thrombosis that permanently occludes the entire venous drainage of the pituitary and produces infarction of both lobes","B":"During pregnancy the pituitary enlarges significantly due to lactotroph hyperplasia, increasing the gland's metabolic demands within the fixed volume of the sella turcica; this enlarged gland becomes vulnerable to ischemic necrosis when severe hypotension during postpartum hemorrhage compromises the low-pressure hypothalamic-hypophysial portal blood supply to the adenohypophysis","C":"Postpartum hemorrhage triggers a systemic coagulopathy with disseminated intravascular coagulation that causes widespread microthrombi in both the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal sinuses and the posterior pituitary arterial supply, simultaneously infarcting the entire pituitary gland including both the adenohypophysis and the neurohypophysis equally","D":"Postpartum hemorrhage triggers prostaglandin-mediated vasospasm of the hypophysial stalk arteries, acutely cutting off blood flow to the entire pituitary gland and producing symmetric ischemic necrosis; the preserved posterior pituitary function noted in this patient reflects rapid revascularization of the neurohypophysis by anastomotic vessels from the inferior hypophysial artery","E":"Postpartum hemorrhage produces an autoimmune lymphocytic hypophysitis triggered by fetal trophoblast antigens released into the maternal circulation during delivery; the autoimmune process selectively destroys all anterior pituitary cell types over several months while sparing the posterior pituitary because it lacks the trophoblast antigen epitopes"},"explanation":"This is Sheehan syndrome — ischemic necrosis of the anterior pituitary following postpartum hemorrhage. The key anatomical-physiological basis: during pregnancy, the anterior pituitary nearly doubles in size due to massive lactotroph hyperplasia in preparation for lactation. This enlarged gland within the fixed bony sella turcica has increased metabolic demand and is supplied primarily by the low-pressure hypothalamic-hypophysial portal blood vessels (not high-pressure arteries). Severe hypotension from postpartum hemorrhage reduces portal blood flow to critically low levels, causing ischemic necrosis of the oxygen-deprived anterior pituitary. The posterior pituitary is usually spared because it receives its blood supply from the inferior hypophysial artery (a direct arterial supply). The first symptom is failure to lactate (prolactin loss), followed by hypogonadism, then hypothyroidism, and finally adrenal insufficiency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sheehan syndrome = postpartum pituitary infarction after severe hemorrhage. Key clues: inability to breastfeed after delivery, followed by amenorrhea, cold intolerance, fatigue. Posterior pituitary is usually spared (high-pressure arterial supply vs low-pressure portal supply to anterior). COMLEX: Distinguish from autoimmune lymphocytic hypophysitis (more common in pregnant/postpartum women, tends to spare posterior pituitary similarly). MRI shows an empty or small sella in Sheehan.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — pathogenesis, pathology, and clinical presentation of hypopituitarism including Sheehan syndrome","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"D":"The posterior pituitary is usu"}},{"id":"sdl4_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal system and describe how it delivers hypothalamic hormones to the anterior pituitary.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with schizophrenia who was recently started on haloperidol, a dopamine D2 receptor blocker, presents to her gynecologist 3 months later with galactorrhea, amenorrhea, and bilateral breast tenderness. Serum prolactin is markedly elevated at 148 ng/mL. Thyroid function tests and pregnancy test are normal. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which haloperidol caused hyperprolactinemia?","choices":{"B":"Dopamine from the hypothalamus travels via the portal blood vessels to the anterior pituitary and tonically inhibits prolactin secretion by binding D2 receptors on lactotropes; haloperidol blocks these D2 receptors on lactotropes, preventing dopaminergic inhibition and disinhibiting prolactin secretion","A":"Haloperidol crosses the blood-brain barrier and directly damages lactotrope cell membranes in the anterior pituitary, causing constitutive exocytosis of prolactin secretory granules by disrupting the calcium-dependent membrane sealing mechanism required for regulated hormone secretion","C":"Haloperidol stimulates hypothalamic neurons in the paraventricular nucleus to increase release of a prolactin-releasing factor into the portal system that tonically overrides the normal dopaminergic inhibitory signal and drives prolactin synthesis above baseline levels","D":"Haloperidol inhibits hepatic prolactin degradation by blocking cytochrome P450 enzymes responsible for prolactin catabolism, leading to passive accumulation of otherwise normally secreted prolactin in the circulation without any change in pituitary secretory rate","E":"Haloperidol increases estrogen receptor sensitivity on lactotrope cell membranes, which amplifies prolactin gene transcription in response to circulating estrogen, and the resulting hyperprolactinemia then suppresses GnRH pulse frequency causing secondary amenorrhea through a separate neuroendocrine mechanism"},"explanation":"Prolactin is the one anterior pituitary hormone under predominant tonic inhibitory control. Dopamine (prolactin inhibitory hormone, PIH) is secreted by tuberoinfundibular dopaminergic neurons into the median eminence primary capillary plexus, enters the portal circulation, and is delivered to lactotropes in the anterior pituitary, where it binds D2 receptors and tonically suppresses prolactin synthesis and secretion. Haloperidol blocks D2 receptors on lactotropes, removing this inhibitory brake and causing hyperprolactinemia. This is a drug-induced (iatrogenic) cause of hyperprolactinemia. Any drug that depletes dopamine or blocks D2 receptors (antipsychotics, metoclopramide, domperidone, methyldopa, reserpine, verapamil) can cause this.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dopamine = prolactin inhibitory hormone (PIH). Dopamine antagonists (haloperidol, risperidone, metoclopramide) and dopamine depleters (reserpine, methyldopa) all cause hyperprolactinemia. Dopamine agonists (bromocriptine, cabergoline) treat prolactinomas. Hyperprolactinemia causes: galactorrhea, amenorrhea (inhibits GnRH pulsatility), infertility, and decreased libido. COMLEX: Prolactinoma is the most common functioning pituitary adenoma; women present with galactorrhea/amenorrhea, men often present late with hypogonadism or mass effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — matching each anterior pituitary hormone with its corresponding hypothalamic releasing or inhibitory hormone","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl4_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal system and describe how it delivers hypothalamic hormones to the anterior pituitary.","batch":2,"stem":"A 51-year-old woman undergoes MRI for evaluation of headaches. Imaging reveals a meningioma compressing the pituitary stalk at the level of the median eminence. She also reports several months of fatigue, cold intolerance, and low libido. Which of the following laboratory patterns would most likely be found on pituitary function testing?","choices":{"C":"Elevated prolactin with low GH, low TSH, low ACTH, low FSH, and low LH; stalk compression prevents dopamine from reaching the anterior pituitary (disinhibiting prolactin) and also prevents the other hypothalamic releasing hormones from reaching their respective target cells, causing their deficiency","A":"Low prolactin with low GH, low TSH, low ACTH, low FSH, and low LH; all anterior pituitary hormone levels fall equally because the stalk compression impairs both the portal blood flow carrying releasing hormones and the direct arterial supply to the anterior pituitary, causing ischemic injury to all cell types uniformly","B":"Normal prolactin with low GH, low TSH, low ACTH, low FSH, and low LH; the portal system continues to deliver dopamine effectively despite stalk compression because dopamine has a lower molecular weight than peptide releasing hormones and can diffuse across the compressed stalk more readily","D":"Elevated prolactin, elevated GH, elevated TSH, elevated ACTH, elevated FSH, and elevated LH; disruption of the stalk prevents peripheral hormone negative feedback from reaching the hypothalamus, causing loss of all inhibitory signals and unregulated release of all anterior pituitary hormones simultaneously","E":"Elevated prolactin with normal GH, normal TSH, normal ACTH, normal FSH, and normal LH; stalk compression selectively reduces flow through the small dopaminergic portal vessels while the larger portal channels carrying peptide releasing hormones remain fully patent due to their higher intrinsic pressure"},"explanation":"Pituitary stalk compression produces a predictable dual hormonal pattern. First, it blocks portal delivery of dopamine from the hypothalamus to the anterior pituitary, removing the tonic inhibitory brake on lactotropes and causing hyperprolactinemia. Second, it simultaneously blocks delivery of all other hypothalamic releasing hormones (GHRH, TRH, CRH, GnRH) to their target cells, causing deficiency of GH, TSH, ACTH, FSH, and LH. This combination — elevated prolactin with deficiency of all other anterior pituitary hormones — is the hallmark \"stalk section effect.\" It can be caused by any suprasellar or intrasellar mass that compresses the stalk, including meningioma, craniopharyngioma, large non-functioning pituitary adenomas, and metastases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stalk section effect = high prolactin + low everything else. This is pathognomonic for stalk compression or interruption. Contrast with a prolactinoma, which causes high prolactin alone with normal other hormones (unless very large). COMLEX: Any mass lesion from the hypothalamus to the pituitary that compresses the stalk will produce this pattern — craniopharyngioma in children, meningioma or germinoma in adults. The pattern helps localize the level of pathology.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — how hypothalamic releasing hormones control anterior pituitary secretion of hormones that regulate target endocrine glands","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl4_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal system and describe how it delivers hypothalamic hormones to the anterior pituitary.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old obese woman presents with intermittent headaches over several months. She has no visual disturbances. An MRI obtained for headache evaluation incidentally reveals an enlarged sella turcica that appears to contain only CSF, with the pituitary gland compressed and flattened against the floor of the sella. A comprehensive pituitary function panel shows all hormone levels are within normal limits. Which of the following best explains the finding on MRI?","choices":{"D":"Herniation of the arachnoid membrane through a deficient sellar diaphragm lets CSF fill the sella turcica under subarachnoid pressure, progressively compressing and flattening the pituitary against the sellar floor while enough functional tissue is preserved along the periphery to maintain normal hormone secretion","A":"A non-functioning pituitary macroadenoma underwent spontaneous apoptotic regression after the tumor outgrew its blood supply, leaving behind an enlarged empty sella cavity; the remaining viable anterior pituitary cells migrated from the infundibulum to repopulate the inferior sella and restore full endocrine function over several months","B":"Repeated micro-hemorrhages within a previously undiagnosed non-functioning pituitary adenoma caused progressive cystic degeneration and fluid accumulation within the sella; the remaining viable pituitary parenchyma detached from the stalk and migrated superiorly toward the median eminence where it re-established portal blood supply","C":"Congenital agenesis of the sellar diaphragm caused CSF to slowly erode into the sella turcica from birth; the pituitary gland never properly descended into the sella during embryological development and instead formed a flat plate of tissue along the floor of the third ventricle beneath the hypothalamus","E":"Elevated intracranial pressure from idiopathic intracranial hypertension caused the pituitary gland to undergo pressure-induced apoptosis over several years; the empty sella represents an involuted gland shell and the apparently normal hormone levels reflect compensatory secretory upregulation in remaining viable cells"},"explanation":"Primary empty sella syndrome occurs when the sellar diaphragm (the dural fold that normally covers the sella turcica and protects the pituitary from CSF pressure) is absent or has a large opening. This allows the arachnoid to herniate downward, and CSF fills the sella turcica under subarachnoid pressure. The pituitary gland is compressed against the floor but retains sufficient functional glandular tissue to maintain normal hormone production in most patients. This condition is most common in middle-aged obese women and is often asymptomatic or associated with headaches. Idiopathic intracranial hypertension in obese women contributes by raising CSF pressure that drives the arachnoid herniation. Pituitary function is usually preserved.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Empty sella = arachnoid herniation through deficient sellar diaphragm. Associated with obesity, female sex, and idiopathic intracranial hypertension. Usually incidental and benign — pituitary function is normal. Distinguish from secondary empty sella (after pituitary tumor surgery, radiation, or infarction) where function may be impaired. COMLEX: Idiopathic intracranial hypertension is associated with obesity and papilledema; empty sella is a frequent associated finding on MRI.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — clinicopathologic features of pituitary and suprasellar tumors including management principles for structural sella abnormalities","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl4_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the synthesis of posterior pituitary hormones in hypothalamic nuclei and their transport to the posterior pituitary.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man is admitted to the ICU following a high-speed motor vehicle collision with traumatic brain injury. On hospital day 2 he develops massive polyuria at 9 liters per day with extreme thirst. Serum sodium is 158 mEq/L, serum osmolality is 318 mOsm/kg, and urine osmolality is 68 mOsm/kg. Plasma ADH is undetectable. Administration of intranasal desmopressin within 30 minutes reduces urine output and increases urine osmolality to 490 mOsm/kg. Which of the following best explains the anatomical basis of his condition?","choices":{"C":"Traumatic injury to the supraoptic nucleus or the hypothalamo-neurohypophysial tract disrupted ADH synthesis and axonal transport from the hypothalamus to the posterior pituitary, causing central diabetes insipidus; the prompt response to desmopressin confirms that the renal V2 receptors and aquaporin-2 channels are functionally intact","A":"Traumatic injury to the pituicytes of the posterior pituitary gland itself destroyed the storage and release compartment for ADH, while synthesis in the hypothalamic supraoptic nucleus is preserved; desmopressin bypasses the posterior pituitary storage defect by providing exogenous V2 receptor agonist directly to the renal collecting duct","B":"Traumatic injury to the anterior hypothalamus disrupted hypothalamic osmoreceptors that regulate thirst sensation, causing compulsive polydipsia despite normal ADH levels; the resulting dilutional suppression of ADH lowered urine osmolality, and desmopressin raised it by providing supraphysiologic V2 stimulation independent of the osmoreceptor defect","D":"Traumatic injury to the pituitary stalk disrupted the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal vessels that normally carry an anterior-pituitary-derived ADH co-secretory factor to the posterior pituitary; the posterior pituitary cannot release ADH without this paracrine signal, and desmopressin bypasses the absent co-secretory factor","E":"Traumatic injury triggered systemic inflammatory cytokine release that down-regulated aquaporin-2 channel expression in renal collecting duct principal cells, producing a transient form of nephrogenic diabetes insipidus; the apparent response to desmopressin reflects spontaneous osmotic equilibration coinciding with the drug administration"},"explanation":"This is central (neurogenic) diabetes insipidus following TBI. The condition arises from damage to the supraoptic and/or paraventricular nuclei or to the hypothalamo-neurohypophysial tract, disrupting ADH synthesis or axonal transport to the posterior pituitary. Without ADH, the renal collecting duct cannot insert aquaporin-2 water channels and massive water loss ensues. The diagnostic hallmark is dilute urine despite concentrated serum (hypernatremic, hyperosmolar state). The desmopressin response test distinguishes central DI from nephrogenic DI: in central DI, exogenous V2 agonist (desmopressin) restores urine concentration because the kidneys are intact; in nephrogenic DI, desmopressin fails to concentrate urine. Post-traumatic DI often involves the upper neurohypophysial tract; lesions below the median eminence may spare function because the proximal axon terminals can still release ADH into the portal capillary plexus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Central DI = ADH deficiency from hypothalamic or stalk damage. Triad: hypernatremia + polyuria + very dilute urine. Desmopressin corrects central DI but not nephrogenic. Nephrogenic DI (ADH present but ineffective kidneys) can be caused by lithium toxicity, hypercalcemia, hypokalemia, or V2 receptor mutations. COMLEX: Traumatic DI can be transient (stalk edema) or permanent. Distinguish from primary polydipsia (low serum Na, dilute urine) and SIADH (low Na, concentrated urine) using the serum and urine osmolality together.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — ADH mechanism of action via aquaporins and its regulation by osmolarity and blood volume","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"E":"The desmopressin response test"}},{"id":"sdl4_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the synthesis of posterior pituitary hormones in hypothalamic nuclei and their transport to the posterior pituitary.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman is in active labor at 39 weeks gestation. After each uterine contraction, the obstetrics team notes a transient rise in her serum oxytocin level, which is followed by a stronger subsequent contraction, and then another oxytocin surge. This pattern progressively intensifies throughout labor until delivery. Which of the following best explains the neuroendocrine mechanism responsible for this escalating oxytocin release pattern?","choices":{"A":"Uterine and cervical distension activates afferent stretch receptor signals that travel to the hypothalamic paraventricular nucleus and stimulate magnocellular neurons to release more oxytocin from posterior pituitary nerve terminals, creating a positive feedback loop in which each contraction drives further oxytocin secretion that amplifies subsequent contractions until the fetus is delivered","B":"Rising cortisol concentrations from maternal physiological stress during each contraction activate oxytocin gene transcription in the supraoptic nucleus; the surge in cortisol that accompanies each contraction directly stimulates a proportional burst of new oxytocin synthesis and immediate axonal release from posterior pituitary terminals","C":"Each uterine contraction mechanically compresses the pituitary stalk, transiently increasing intravascular pressure in the posterior pituitary capillaries; this elevated hydrostatic pressure forces oxytocin molecules stored in Herring body vesicles out of their nerve terminals into the capillary lumen through a pressure-driven non-exocytotic release mechanism","D":"Oxytocin released during each contraction enters the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal circulation via retrograde portal flow and binds oxytocin receptors on paraventricular nucleus neurons directly within the median eminence capillary plexus, creating an autocrine amplification loop within the portal vasculature itself","E":"Estrogen produced by the placenta progressively upregulates oxytocin receptors on posterior pituitary pituicytes during the third trimester; each contraction stimulates the pituicytes to release their stored oxytocin through an estrogen-primed autocrine mechanism that does not require any afferent neural input from uterine stretch receptors"},"explanation":"The Ferguson reflex is the positive feedback loop driving labor. As the fetus descends and distends the cervix and lower uterine segment, mechanoreceptors generate afferent nerve impulses that ascend to the hypothalamus (particularly the paraventricular nucleus), which activates magnocellular neurons to release oxytocin from posterior pituitary nerve terminals into the circulation. Oxytocin binds Gq-coupled OXTR receptors on myometrial cells, increasing intracellular calcium and driving contraction. Stronger contractions produce more cervical/uterine distension, generating stronger afferent signals, causing more oxytocin release — a true positive feedback loop. This loop only terminates with delivery of the fetus (removal of the stretch stimulus). This is one of the two canonical positive feedback loops in endocrinology (the other being the LH surge).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Oxytocin = positive feedback during labor (Ferguson reflex) and milk ejection (let-down reflex). Both are positive feedback loops: in labor, cervical stretch → more oxytocin → more contraction; in lactation, suckling → more oxytocin → milk ejection → more suckling. COMLEX: Oxytocin is used clinically for labor induction/augmentation and to prevent postpartum hemorrhage (causes uterine contraction). Contraindicated in abnormal fetal presentation or uterine scar.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — oxytocin mechanism of action and roles in labor and lactation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl4_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the synthesis of posterior pituitary hormones in hypothalamic nuclei and their transport to the posterior pituitary.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man with a 60-pack-year smoking history presents with progressive fatigue, confusion, and anorexia. Laboratory evaluation reveals serum sodium of 116 mEq/L, serum osmolality of 244 mOsm/kg, and urine osmolality of 680 mOsm/kg. Serum ADH is markedly elevated. Chest CT reveals a 3.8-cm central lung mass. Immunohistochemistry of the tumor biopsy shows ADH production by the tumor cells rather than by the hypothalamus. Which of the following best explains how ectopic ADH production by tumor cells differs from normal posterior pituitary ADH physiology?","choices":{"E":"In normal physiology, ADH is synthesized in magnocellular neurons of the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei, and hypothalamic osmoreceptors suppress its release when serum osmolality falls; ectopic ADH from the tumor cells is secreted constitutively without this osmotic suppression, explaining the persistently elevated ADH despite low serum osmolality","A":"In normal physiology, ADH is synthesized by corticotropes in the anterior pituitary and co-secreted with ACTH in response to combined osmotic and stress stimuli; tumor-derived ADH bypasses the portal system entirely and enters the systemic circulation at much higher concentrations than normally secreted ADH because it is not subject to portal dilution","B":"In normal physiology, ADH is synthesized as a permanently inactive prohormone in the posterior pituitary pituicytes and is activated by osmoreceptor-triggered enzymatic cleavage at the luminal surface of collecting duct principal cells; ectopic ADH from the tumor is constitutively pre-cleaved and therefore far more potent per molecule at the V2 receptor than physiological ADH","C":"In normal physiology, ADH is co-synthesized with oxytocin in a single large prohormone that is differentially cleaved in the supraoptic versus paraventricular nucleus; ectopic ADH production in lung cancer occurs because the tumor expresses only the hypothalamic ADH gene fragment and cannot produce oxytocin due to absence of the paraventricular nucleus-specific cleavage enzyme","D":"In normal physiology, ADH is released from the posterior pituitary in pulsatile bursts synchronized with anterior pituitary corticotropin secretion; tumor-derived ADH is secreted in a steady non-pulsatile pattern that produces continuous aquaporin-2 channel insertion in the collecting duct and therefore more efficient water reabsorption per unit of serum ADH concentration"},"explanation":"This is SIADH from ectopic ADH production by a small cell lung carcinoma. In normal posterior pituitary physiology, ADH secretion is tightly regulated by hypothalamic osmoreceptors: as serum osmolality falls, ADH secretion is suppressed to allow free water excretion and osmolality correction. In SIADH from ectopic production, the tumor cells produce ADH constitutively and autonomously, completely independent of osmotic regulation. The result is inappropriately high urine osmolality (concentrated urine) and inappropriately low serum osmolality (dilutional hyponatremia) — urine osmolality is higher than serum osmolality, which is the diagnostic hallmark. ADH is normally synthesized in the hypothalamic magnocellular nuclei (not the pituitary itself) and transported axonally; ectopic production bypasses this entire regulatory system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SIADH diagnosis = euvolemic hyponatremia + urine osmolality above serum osmolality (FA) + urine sodium greater than 30 mEq/L (Bootcamp) + low serum osmolality + clinically euvolemic. Causes: SCLC (most common ectopic ADH), CNS disorders, pulmonary disease, drugs (carbamazepine, SSRIs, cyclophosphamide). Treatment: fluid restriction first-line; hypertonic saline for severe symptoms; demeclocycline or tolvaptan for chronic SIADH. COMLEX: SIADH is a named CP3 condition. Do not correct sodium too rapidly (risk of osmotic demyelination syndrome if corrected more than 10-12 mEq/L per 24 hours).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — pathogenesis, pathology, clinical presentation, and management of diabetes insipidus and SIADH","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"ADH is normally synthesized in","B":"ADH is normally synthesized in"}}],"5":[{"id":"sdl5_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the six major anterior pituitary hormones, their cell types, and their primary target organs.","batch":1,"stem":"Which anterior pituitary cell type comprises approximately 30–40% of all anterior pituitary cells and is responsible for secreting growth hormone?","choices":{"E":"Somatotropes, which are acidophilic cells and represent the most abundant secretory cell type in the anterior pituitary","A":"Corticotropes, which are basophilic cells that constitute roughly 20% of anterior pituitary cells and secrete ACTH","B":"Thyrotropes, which are basophilic cells that account for only 3–5% of anterior pituitary cells and secrete TSH","C":"Gonadotropes, which are basophilic cells that make up 3–5% of anterior pituitary cells and secrete FSH and LH","D":"Lactotropes, also called mammotropes, which are acidophilic cells that account for roughly 3–5% of cells and secrete prolactin"},"explanation":"Somatotropes are the dominant cell type in the anterior pituitary, comprising 30–40% of its secretory cells. They stain acidophilically and secrete growth hormone, a 191 amino acid single-chain polypeptide. Corticotropes (~20%) and gonadotropes, thyrotropes, and lactotropes (each 3–5%) make up the remaining population (Guyton Ch 76). Pawlina's histology table gives different shares: somatotropes about 50%, lactotropes and corticotropes 15–20% each, gonadotropes 10%, thyrotropes about 5%; both rank somatotropes first. Remembering \"Acid PiG\" from First Aid helps: Acidophils secrete Prolactin and GH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FA mnemonic \"B-FLAT\" = Basophils secrete FSH, LH, ACTH, TSH; \"Acid PiG\" = Acidophils secrete Prolactin and GH. Somatotropes are the most numerous anterior pituitary cell type — a classic proportion question. COMLEX: Somatotrope hyperplasia from ectopic GHRH is a board-relevant neuroendocrine tumor scenario.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — Pituitary anatomy and dual-lobe embryologic origins","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Corticotropes (~20%) and gonad","B":"Corticotropes (~20%) and gonad","D":"Corticotropes (~20%) and gonad"}},{"id":"sdl5_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the six major anterior pituitary hormones, their cell types, and their primary target organs.","batch":1,"stem":"A histology instructor asks students to match anterior pituitary staining characteristics to their corresponding secretory products. Which pairing correctly identifies the staining class and its associated hormones?","choices":{"B":"Acidophilic cells produce growth hormone and prolactin, while basophilic cells produce FSH, LH, ACTH, and TSH","A":"Acidophilic cells produce FSH and LH, while basophilic cells produce growth hormone, prolactin, and ACTH","C":"Basophilic cells produce growth hormone and prolactin, while acidophilic cells produce FSH, LH, ACTH, and TSH","D":"Acidophilic cells produce ACTH and TSH, while basophilic cells produce growth hormone, prolactin, FSH, and LH","E":"Acidophilic cells produce TSH alone, while basophilic cells produce all remaining anterior pituitary hormones"},"explanation":"Anterior pituitary cells are classified by their hematoxylin-eosin staining. Acidophils (somatotropes and lactotropes) secrete growth hormone and prolactin. Basophils (corticotropes, thyrotropes, and gonadotropes) secrete ACTH, TSH, FSH, and LH. The First Aid mnemonics \"B-FLAT\" and \"Acid PiG\" encode this for board exams. Chromophobes are a third class with sparse granules and minimal secretory activity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"B-FLAT\" = Basophils: FSH, LH, ACTH, TSH. \"Acid PiG\" = Acidophils: Prolactin, GH. These mnemonics appear in virtually every Step 1 resource. COMLEX: Staining patterns are relevant for identifying the cell of origin in pituitary adenomas on biopsy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — Pituitary anatomy and dual-lobe embryologic origins","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Basophils (corticotropes, thyr","C":"Acidophils (somatotropes and l","D":"Basophils (corticotropes, thyr","E":"Basophils (corticotropes, thyr"}},{"id":"sdl5_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the six major anterior pituitary hormones, their cell types, and their primary target organs.","batch":1,"stem":"Several anterior pituitary and placental hormones share a common structural subunit. Which of the following groups of hormones all share an identical alpha subunit?","choices":{"D":"TSH, LH, FSH, and hCG all share a common alpha subunit, with hormone specificity determined by their unique beta subunits","A":"GH and prolactin share a common alpha subunit, conferring similar lactogenic and growth-promoting effects across both hormones","B":"ACTH and MSH share a common alpha subunit derived from the same precursor molecule produced in corticotropes","C":"TSH and ACTH share a common alpha subunit, which is why elevated TRH can sometimes stimulate ACTH secretion in stress","E":"GH and TSH share a common alpha subunit, explaining why GH excess can sometimes produce subtle changes in thyroid function"},"explanation":"TSH, LH, FSH, and hCG are all glycoprotein hormones that share an identical alpha subunit. Each hormone is distinguished by its unique beta subunit, which confers receptor specificity. This shared alpha subunit is clinically relevant because elevated hCG in early pregnancy can weakly activate TSH receptors, causing a transient suppression of TSH. GH and prolactin are structurally similar single-chain polypeptides but do not share a common subunit with the glycoprotein hormones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic board trap — hCG shares alpha subunit with TSH → explains why hyperthyroidism can occur in molar pregnancy or hyperemesis gravidarum. The alpha subunit is common to TSH, LH, FSH, and hCG; beta subunit determines specificity. COMLEX: hCG-mediated TSH suppression in early pregnancy is a clinical reasoning scenario.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — Pituitary anatomy and dual-lobe embryologic origins","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"E":"GH and prolactin are structura"}},{"id":"sdl5_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Match each anterior pituitary hormone with its corresponding hypothalamic releasing or inhibitory hormone.","batch":1,"stem":"Which hypothalamic hormone is primarily responsible for stimulating the release of ACTH from basophilic corticotropes of the anterior pituitary?","choices":{"A":"Thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH), a tripeptide secreted by the hypothalamus that also stimulates prolactin release from lactotropes","B":"Corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH), a 41 amino acid peptide secreted by the paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus","C":"Growth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH), a 44 amino acid peptide that acts on somatotropes to stimulate growth hormone secretion","D":"Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH), a 10 amino acid decapeptide that controls secretion of both FSH and LH from gonadotropes","E":"Dopamine, a catecholamine released from the hypothalamus that tonically inhibits prolactin secretion from lactotropes"},"explanation":"CRH is the principal hypothalamic stimulator of ACTH release from anterior pituitary corticotropes. CRH is a 41 amino acid peptide secreted by the paraventricular nucleus and acts through a Gs-coupled receptor to increase cAMP and promote ACTH exocytosis. ACTH then acts on the adrenal cortex to stimulate cortisol synthesis. TRH is a tripeptide that stimulates TSH (and prolactin); GHRH stimulates GH; GnRH stimulates FSH and LH; dopamine tonically inhibits prolactin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CRH → ACTH → cortisol is the HPA axis. Know that ADH (vasopressin) also stimulates ACTH release and acts synergistically with CRH. COMLEX: Adrenal viscerosomatic reflex levels (T8–T10 with sympathetic T7–T11, OMM atlas Ch 15; sympathetic T10–L1 per Moore Ch 5) connect to HPA stress response — relevant for OMM management of adrenal dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — Hypothalamic-hypophysial portal system delivers releasing hormones to anterior pituitary","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"TRH is a tripeptide that stimu"}},{"id":"sdl5_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Match each anterior pituitary hormone with its corresponding hypothalamic releasing or inhibitory hormone.","batch":1,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with no pituitary lesion on MRI presents with amenorrhea and a milky nipple discharge. Her TSH is markedly elevated and free T4 is low. Which mechanism best explains her galactorrhea?","choices":{"B":"Elevated TRH from primary hypothyroidism stimulates prolactin secretion from lactotropes in addition to its primary role in stimulating TSH","A":"Decreased dopamine from hypothyroidism removes tonic inhibition of lactotropes, allowing unrestricted prolactin secretion independent of TRH","C":"Elevated TSH cross-reacts with prolactin receptors on lactotropes, directly stimulating milk production via shared receptor signaling","D":"Low T4 directly activates prolactin gene transcription in lactotropes by binding to nuclear receptors in the anterior pituitary","E":"Elevated CRH from hypothyroid stress co-stimulates both ACTH and prolactin secretion from the same basophilic cell population"},"explanation":"TRH is a tripeptide that stimulates both TSH and prolactin secretion. In primary hypothyroidism, reduced T4 removes negative feedback on the hypothalamus, causing compensatory TRH elevation. Elevated TRH then stimulates both TSH (the primary target) and prolactin (a secondary effect), resulting in hyperprolactinemia, galactorrhea, and amenorrhea. This is a classic board scenario distinguishing secondary hyperprolactinemia from a true prolactinoma, because treating the hypothyroidism normalizes prolactin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic board trap — hypothyroidism causes hyperprolactinemia via elevated TRH, NOT decreased dopamine. Always check TSH before attributing galactorrhea to a prolactinoma. COMLEX: A patient with hypothyroid features plus galactorrhea and amenorrhea is a high-yield COMLEX clinical presentation linking thyroid and pituitary physiology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — Hypothalamic-hypophysial portal system delivers releasing hormones to anterior pituitary","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl5_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Match each anterior pituitary hormone with its corresponding hypothalamic releasing or inhibitory hormone.","batch":1,"stem":"Unlike other anterior pituitary hormones, prolactin secretion is under dominant inhibitory control rather than stimulatory control from the hypothalamus. Which hypothalamic substance mediates this tonic inhibition?","choices":{"D":"Dopamine, a catecholamine released by tuberoinfundibular neurons into the portal blood that tonically suppresses prolactin release","A":"Somatostatin, a 14 amino acid peptide that inhibits both growth hormone and TSH and can suppress prolactin in pharmacologic doses","B":"GABA, an inhibitory neurotransmitter released by hypothalamic interneurons that reduces the firing of TRH-secreting neurons near lactotropes","C":"Neuropeptide Y, a peptide co-released with CRH that provides tonic inhibition of the lactotrope population during stress responses","E":"Endorphins, opioid peptides released from POMC-processing neurons that suppress prolactin via mu-receptor activation in the median eminence"},"explanation":"Dopamine (also called prolactin-inhibiting hormone, PIH) is released from tuberoinfundibular dopaminergic neurons into the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal blood. It binds D2 receptors on lactotropes, activating a Gi-coupled pathway that decreases cAMP and inhibits prolactin secretion. This tonic inhibition means that any disruption of the portal system (pituitary stalk compression) causes hyperprolactinemia by eliminating dopamine delivery. Drugs that block D2 receptors (antipsychotics, metoclopramide) also elevate prolactin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dopamine = prolactin-inhibiting hormone (PIH). D2 agonists (bromocriptine, cabergoline) lower prolactin; D2 antagonists (antipsychotics, metoclopramide, domperidone) raise prolactin. Pituitary stalk compression causes hyperprolactinemia via dopamine blockade. COMLEX: Drug-induced galactorrhea from antipsychotics is a high-yield COMLEX clinical presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — Hypothalamic-hypophysial portal system delivers releasing hormones to anterior pituitary","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl5_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how hypothalamic releasing hormones control anterior pituitary secretion of hormones that regulate target endocrine glands.","batch":1,"stem":"Hypothalamic releasing hormones must travel from their site of synthesis to the anterior pituitary. Which pathway is responsible for delivering these peptides to their target cells in the adenohypophysis?","choices":{"B":"The hypothalamic-hypophysial portal system, in which fenestrated capillaries at the median eminence collect releasing hormones and deliver them via portal veins to anterior pituitary sinusoids","A":"Direct neural projections from hypothalamic nuclei into the anterior pituitary parenchyma, releasing hormones at axon terminals adjacent to secretory cells","C":"Systemic arterial circulation carrying releasing hormones diluted throughout the body, with anterior pituitary cells detecting concentration gradients","D":"Lymphatic channels within the pituitary stalk that transport large peptide molecules unable to cross the blood-brain barrier directly","E":"Retrograde transport from posterior pituitary axon terminals through fenestrated capillaries, allowing hypothalamic factors to diffuse anteriorly"},"explanation":"Hypothalamic releasing and inhibiting hormones are secreted into fenestrated capillaries at the median eminence, then travel through the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal veins down the pituitary stalk into the anterior pituitary sinusoids. This portal circulation creates a high local concentration of hypothalamic hormones reaching anterior pituitary cells before dilution in systemic blood. The anterior pituitary has no direct neural input from the hypothalamus — unlike the posterior pituitary, which receives true axonal projections from supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior pituitary control = hormonal (portal blood). Posterior pituitary control = neural (axon terminals). Any lesion compressing the pituitary stalk disrupts portal flow, paradoxically causing hyperprolactinemia because dopamine delivery is lost. COMLEX: The portal system anatomy is relevant to understanding why pituitary adenomas cause hormone changes and visual field deficits.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — Portal vessels as conduit for hypothalamic control of anterior pituitary secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl5_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how hypothalamic releasing hormones control anterior pituitary secretion of hormones that regulate target endocrine glands.","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher studying GnRH physiology compares pulsatile versus continuous GnRH infusion in normal volunteers. Pulsatile GnRH maintains normal FSH and LH levels. What is the expected effect of switching to continuous GnRH infusion?","choices":{"C":"Selective suppression of FSH only, while LH secretion is maintained because each gonadotropin has a separate receptor population","A":"Increased FSH and LH secretion due to sustained receptor stimulation, resulting in elevated testosterone and estrogen levels","B":"No significant change in FSH or LH because gonadotropes can compensate for continuous stimulation via receptor sensitization","D":"Downregulation of GnRH receptors on gonadotropes, leading to decreased FSH and LH secretion and suppressed sex hormone production","E":"Increased prolactin release because continuous GnRH stimulation cross-activates TRH receptors on lactotropes at high concentrations"},"explanation":"Pulsatile GnRH is required to maintain GnRH receptor expression on gonadotropes and support normal FSH and LH secretion. Continuous GnRH exposure causes receptor downregulation and uncoupling, dramatically reducing gonadotropin secretion. This principle is exploited clinically: GnRH analogs (leuprolide, goserelin) given continuously cause medical castration and are used for prostate cancer, endometriosis, and central precocious puberty. An initial testosterone flare occurs during the first 7–10 days before receptor downregulation is complete (Katzung Ch 37).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pulsatile GnRH = fertility. Continuous GnRH analog = castrate testosterone levels. Leuprolide is used for prostate cancer, endometriosis, and precocious puberty. Initial testosterone flare during the first 7–10 days is a classic board point. COMLEX: GnRH analog therapy is a high-yield pharmacology topic linking HPG axis physiology to clinical oncology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — Portal vessels as conduit for hypothalamic control of anterior pituitary secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Continuous GnRH exposure cause","B":"Continuous GnRH exposure cause","C":"Continuous GnRH exposure cause"}},{"id":"sdl5_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how hypothalamic releasing hormones control anterior pituitary secretion of hormones that regulate target endocrine glands.","batch":1,"stem":"GHRH binds to its receptor on anterior pituitary somatotropes and triggers both acute GH vesicle exocytosis and long-term increases in GH gene transcription. Which intracellular second messenger mediates these effects?","choices":{"D":"Cyclic AMP, generated when GHRH activates adenylyl cyclase via a Gs protein, with PKA activation promoting both GH secretion and transcription","A":"Inositol trisphosphate and diacylglycerol, generated when GHRH activates phospholipase C via Gq, triggering intracellular calcium release and PKC activation","B":"Cyclic GMP (cGMP), generated when GHRH activates guanylyl cyclase, leading to PKG activation and GH vesicle fusion with the plasma membrane","C":"The JAK-STAT pathway activated when GHRH binds to an enzyme-linked receptor, causing receptor dimerization and STAT5 phosphorylation in somatotropes","E":"Decreased cAMP via Gi protein coupling, which paradoxically disinhibits voltage-gated calcium channels to trigger GH exocytosis acutely"},"explanation":"GHRH binds a Gs protein-coupled receptor on somatotropes, activating adenylyl cyclase and increasing intracellular cAMP. cAMP activates protein kinase A (PKA), which phosphorylates targets that promote GH vesicle exocytosis acutely and increases GH gene transcription chronically. This is why McCune-Albright syndrome (activating Gs mutation) can cause excess GH. Somatostatin acts on somatotropes via Gi to oppose GHRH: it inhibits adenylyl cyclase, reducing cAMP and suppressing GH release.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GHRH → Gs → cAMP → PKA → GH release. Somatostatin → Gi → decreased cAMP → GH suppression. McCune-Albright syndrome involves an activating Gs alpha mutation causing autonomous hormone secretion. COMLEX: cAMP-mediated hormone secretion is a core signaling pathway tested across multiple endocrine SDLs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — Portal vessels as conduit for hypothalamic control of anterior pituitary secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"E":"Somatostatin acts on somatotro"}},{"id":"sdl5_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Predict the consequences of over or underproduction of the pituitary hormones.","batch":1,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy with a GH-secreting pituitary adenoma is noted to be growing at twice the expected rate. His epiphyseal growth plates remain open on X-ray. If untreated, which condition is this child most likely to develop?","choices":{"A":"Gigantism, characterized by proportionate overgrowth of long bones due to sustained GH excess while epiphyseal plates remain open","B":"Acromegaly, characterized by enlargement of hands, feet, and facial bones due to GH excess acting on already-fused epiphyseal plates","C":"Cushing syndrome, caused by ACTH hypersecretion from a corticotrope co-secreting tumor in the setting of excess GH production","D":"Constitutional tall stature, a normal variant unrelated to hormonal excess that resolves spontaneously with epiphyseal plate closure","E":"Laron syndrome, caused by GH receptor insensitivity leading to paradoxical growth despite elevated circulating GH levels"},"explanation":"GH excess before epiphyseal plate closure causes gigantism, a condition of proportionate overgrowth of long bones that can result in heights of up to 8 feet (Guyton Ch 76). The open growth plates respond to GH-stimulated IGF-1 by continuing longitudinal growth. In contrast, GH excess after epiphyseal fusion causes acromegaly, characterized by membranous bone enlargement and soft tissue changes. The key distinguishing factor is epiphyseal plate status at the time of GH excess onset.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH excess before fusion = gigantism (tall stature). GH excess after fusion = acromegaly (big hands, jaw, prognathism). Gigantism can transition to acromegaly after puberty if the tumor persists past epiphyseal closure. COMLEX: Gigantism is explicitly named in CP3 physical exam findings — recognizing the combination of tall stature and coarse features in a child is high yield.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — Clinical features of GH excess and deficiency including gigantism and acromegaly","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl5_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Predict the consequences of over or underproduction of the pituitary hormones.","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old man presents with progressive enlargement of his hands and feet, coarsening facial features, prognathism, increased spacing between teeth, and new-onset type 2 diabetes mellitus. He reports his hat and glove sizes have increased over the past decade. Which pituitary hormone excess is responsible for this clinical picture?","choices":{"D":"Prolactin excess from a lactotrope adenoma causing peripheral insulin resistance, soft tissue hypertrophy, and skeletal remodeling","A":"Growth hormone excess after epiphyseal closure, causing acromegaly with membranous bone and soft tissue overgrowth but no height gain","B":"TSH excess from a thyrotrope adenoma causing hypermetabolism with peripheral bone resorption and soft tissue hypertrophy","C":"ACTH excess causing Cushing syndrome with characteristic central adiposity, osteoporosis, and glucose intolerance","E":"IGF-1 excess from a hepatocellular source without pituitary GH elevation, mimicking acromegaly through peripheral growth factor action"},"explanation":"Acromegaly results from GH excess occurring after epiphyseal plate closure, so longitudinal height growth has ceased. GH-stimulated IGF-1 acts on membranous bones and soft tissues, causing enlarged hands, feet, and facial bones (prognathism, macroglossia, frontal bossing), organomegaly, and carpal tunnel syndrome. GH causes insulin resistance, leading to diabetes mellitus in about 10–15% of patients and impaired glucose tolerance in about 50% (Marks Ch 41). Diagnosis requires failure of GH suppression with oral glucose challenge plus elevated IGF-1.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acromegaly diagnosis = IGF-1 elevated + GH not suppressed by oral glucose tolerance test. Classic features: ring/shoe size increasing, prognathism, carpal tunnel, diabetes, hypertension, colon polyps. COMLEX: Acromegaly and gigantism are named CP3 physical exam findings — recognizing coarse features plus organ enlargement plus diabetes is a classic board scenario.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — Clinical features of GH excess and deficiency including gigantism and acromegaly","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl5_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Predict the consequences of over or underproduction of the pituitary hormones.","batch":1,"stem":"A large pituitary macroadenoma destroys the vast majority of anterior pituitary secretory cells. Which combination of hormone deficiencies would be expected in a patient with panhypopituitarism?","choices":{"C":"Deficiency of GH, ACTH, TSH, FSH, LH, and prolactin, resulting in adrenal insufficiency, hypothyroidism, hypogonadism, and growth failure","A":"Deficiency of ACTH and TSH only, since GH and gonadotropins are made redundant by peripheral organs that can produce similar trophic signals","B":"Deficiency of GH and prolactin only, as the acidophilic cells are most susceptible to mass effect while basophilic cells are more resistant","D":"Deficiency of FSH and LH only, because gonadotropes are uniquely sensitive to compression while other cell types maintain residual function","E":"Deficiency of TSH and prolactin only, since thyroid and lactation pathways are regulated exclusively by anterior pituitary trophic hormones"},"explanation":"Panhypopituitarism refers to the deficiency of all anterior pituitary hormones. This presents as secondary adrenal insufficiency (ACTH loss → hypocortisol), secondary hypothyroidism (TSH loss → low T4), hypogonadism (FSH/LH loss → low sex hormones), growth failure in children (GH loss), and loss of lactation potential (prolactin loss). Life-threatening emergencies stem from adrenal and thyroid deficiency; sexual dysfunction and infertility are common. GH loss typically occurs first with progressive pituitary destruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Order of GH deficiency in panhypopituitarism — GH lost first, then gonadotropins, then TSH, then ACTH, then prolactin last. Sheehan syndrome (postpartum pituitary necrosis) is a classic cause. COMLEX: Panhypopituitarism connects to OMM because chronic stress (adrenal and thyroid deficiency) has viscerosomatic consequences at T1–T5 for the heart (OMM course map and OMM atlas Ch 15); for the thyroid at C4–C6 and T2 with sympathetic T1–T4 (OMM atlas) vs head and neck T1–T5 (OMM course map); and for the adrenal at T8–T10 (OMM atlas) vs sympathetic T10–L1 per Moore Ch 5.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — Clinical features of GH excess and deficiency including gigantism and acromegaly","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl5_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the six major anterior pituitary hormones, their cell types, and their primary target organs.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has panhypopituitarism after surgery for a pituitary macroadenoma. He takes hydrocortisone, levothyroxine, and testosterone, and his cortisol, free T4, and testosterone levels are now normal. Over the next year he still loses lean mass and gains fat mass. His endocrinologist explains that one of his missing anterior pituitary hormones differs from the others because it acts on almost all body tissues rather than mainly by stimulating a target endocrine gland. Which hormone is this?","choices":{"E":"Growth hormone","A":"Thyroid-stimulating hormone","B":"Luteinizing hormone","C":"Adrenocorticotropic hormone","D":"Follicle-stimulating hormone"},"explanation":"Guyton (Ch 76) states that all the major anterior pituitary hormones except growth hormone exert their principal effects mainly by stimulating target glands: the thyroid gland, adrenal cortex, ovaries, testes, and mammary glands. GH instead promotes growth and metabolic effects in almost all tissues, partly directly and partly through IGF-1 made mainly in the liver. Robbins notes that GH deficiency in adults lowers lean mass and raises fat mass, which fits this patient. Thyroid-stimulating hormone acts on the thyroid, and its deficit is covered by levothyroxine. Adrenocorticotropic hormone acts on the adrenal cortex, and hydrocortisone replaces its main product. Luteinizing hormone acts on the testis to drive testosterone, which he now receives. Follicle-stimulating hormone acts on the testis to regulate spermatogenesis, so it too works through a target gland.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH is the anterior pituitary hormone that is not a tropic hormone for a target endocrine gland; its growth effects are carried largely by IGF-1 from the liver (Guyton Ch 76). Adult GH deficiency lowers lean mass and raises fat mass (Robbins). COMLEX: Replacing target-gland hormones does not substitute for GH, because no downstream gland hormone carries GH's actions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — IGF-1 made in the liver mediates many of growth hormone's effects","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl5_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the six major anterior pituitary hormones, their cell types, and their primary target organs.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman at 10 weeks gestation presents for routine prenatal labs. Her serum hCG is 85,000 mIU/mL. Her TSH is reported at 0.1 mIU/L (reference: 0.45–4.12), free T4 is 1.3 ng/dL (reference: 0.8–1.4), and she has mild nausea but no palpitations, tremor, or heat intolerance. Which structural feature of hCG best explains the suppressed TSH?","choices":{"D":"hCG shares an identical alpha subunit with TSH, so it weakly activates thyroid TSH receptors and suppresses pituitary TSH via negative feedback","A":"hCG shares an identical beta subunit with TSH, conferring nearly identical receptor binding affinity and causing sustained hyperthyroidism throughout gestation","B":"hCG activates TRH receptors on hypothalamic neurons, suppressing TRH secretion and reducing the hypothalamic drive for anterior pituitary TSH production","C":"hCG cross-activates GH receptors on anterior pituitary somatotropes, which then produce a TSH-suppressing paracrine signal within the adenohypophysis","E":"hCG directly inhibits TSH gene transcription in thyrotropes by binding to nuclear thyroid hormone receptors in the anterior pituitary"},"explanation":"TSH, LH, FSH, and hCG all share a common alpha subunit. Hormone specificity is determined by the unique beta subunit. Because hCG shares the alpha subunit with TSH, it can weakly activate the TSH receptor (TSHR) on thyroid follicular cells, stimulating thyroid hormone synthesis. The resulting mild T4 elevation suppresses TSH via negative feedback on the HPT axis. This physiologic TSH suppression in the first trimester is normal, particularly in the context of hyperemesis gravidarum. True gestational hyperthyroidism occurs when hCG levels are extremely elevated (molar pregnancy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hCG cross-reacts with TSH receptors via shared alpha subunit → physiologic TSH suppression in first trimester. Molar pregnancy has extremely high hCG → overt hyperthyroidism. Beta subunit determines hormone specificity (not alpha). COMLEX: First-trimester TSH suppression without symptoms requires no treatment — distinguishing physiologic from pathologic is a high-yield COMLEX clinical reasoning task.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — Thyroid hormone transport and HPT axis feedback establishes how T4 elevations suppress TSH via central negative feedback","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Hormone specificity is determi"}},{"id":"sdl5_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the six major anterior pituitary hormones, their cell types, and their primary target organs.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman who underwent bilateral adrenalectomy for refractory Cushing syndrome two years ago now presents with progressive skin hyperpigmentation and a newly identified pituitary mass on MRI. Biopsy of the mass reveals basophilic cells staining positive for both ACTH and MSH. Which precursor molecule accounts for the production of both ACTH and MSH by the same corticotrope cell?","choices":{"C":"Pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC), a large precursor protein cleaved by tissue-specific prohormone convertases to yield ACTH, MSH, beta-endorphin, and related peptides","A":"Pre-pro-TRH, a precursor protein processed by thyrotropes that generates both TSH regulatory peptides and melanocyte-stimulating fragments during hyperthyroid states","B":"Pro-enkephalin, an opioid precursor processed by corticotropes under chronic stress that generates ACTH-like fragments with peripheral melanocyte-stimulating activity","D":"Pre-pro-somatostatin, a 116 amino acid precursor cleaved by hypothalamic convertases to produce both somatostatin-14 and cortistatin with ACTH-like fragments","E":"Proglucagon, a pancreatic precursor protein processed differently in corticotropes under adrenal insufficiency to generate ACTH and MSH alongside GLP-1 fragments"},"explanation":"POMC is a large precursor processed by tissue-specific prohormone convertases. In the anterior pituitary, PC1 cleaves POMC into ACTH and beta-lipotropin (beta-LPH). In the hypothalamus and skin, PC2 further cleaves ACTH into alpha-MSH. This patient has Nelson syndrome: after bilateral adrenalectomy removes cortisol feedback, the corticotrope adenoma grows unrestrained, secreting massive ACTH and co-producing MSH from POMC cleavage, causing hyperpigmentation. POMC also yields beta-endorphin from beta-LPH cleavage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: POMC → ACTH + beta-LPH → alpha-MSH + beta-endorphin. Nelson syndrome = post-adrenalectomy ACTH elevation + pituitary expansion + hyperpigmentation. Addison disease also causes hyperpigmentation via POMC-derived MSH when ACTH is elevated. COMLEX: POMC processing is high-yield for explaining hyperpigmentation in adrenal insufficiency and Nelson syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — Congenital adrenal hyperplasia and adrenal pathology connects ACTH excess to pigmentation changes through POMC-derived MSH","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl5_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Match each anterior pituitary hormone with its corresponding hypothalamic releasing or inhibitory hormone.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman with acromegaly is started on a long-acting somatostatin analog. Before discussing side effects, her endocrinologist reviews the normal actions of hypothalamic somatostatin, also called growth hormone-inhibiting hormone, on the anterior pituitary. Besides growth hormone from somatotropes, which anterior pituitary hormone does hypothalamic somatostatin also inhibit?","choices":{"E":"Thyroid-stimulating hormone from thyrotropes","A":"Adrenocorticotropic hormone from corticotropes","B":"Luteinizing hormone from gonadotropes","C":"Melanocyte-stimulating hormone from corticotropes","D":"Follicle-stimulating hormone from gonadotropes"},"explanation":"First Aid 2025 (p 332) lists somatostatin, also called growth hormone-inhibiting hormone, as decreasing both GH and TSH. Katzung (Ch 37) likewise states that somatostatin inhibits release of GH and TSH, as well as glucagon, insulin, and gastrin. Guyton's table of hypothalamic hormones (Ch 76) lists inhibition of GH secretion by somatotropes as its primary action. Adrenocorticotropic hormone is driven by CRH from the paraventricular nucleus, not restrained by somatostatin. Melanocyte-stimulating hormone comes from the same POMC precursor as ACTH and rises with CRH (First Aid p 332). Luteinizing hormone and follicle-stimulating hormone are both controlled by GnRH from the hypothalamus and are suppressed by hyperprolactinemia, not by somatostatin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Somatostatin (GHIH) ↓ GH and ↓ TSH; dopamine (prolactin-inhibiting factor) ↓ prolactin and ↓ TSH; TRH ↑ TSH and ↑ prolactin (First Aid 2025 p 332). COMLEX: Somatostatin is also made in the pancreas and GI tract; octreotide adverse effects include nausea, steatorrhea, gallstones, and bradycardia (Katzung Ch 37).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — somatostatin from periventricular neurons inhibits growth hormone secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl5_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Match each anterior pituitary hormone with its corresponding hypothalamic releasing or inhibitory hormone.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man with HIV infection on long-term antiretroviral therapy has a growing waist and increased visceral abdominal fat on imaging. He is started on daily subcutaneous tesamorelin, a synthetic analog of a hypothalamic hormone. Which pairing correctly identifies the hormone that tesamorelin mimics and its action on the anterior pituitary?","choices":{"A":"Growth hormone-releasing hormone, which acts on somatotropes to increase release of growth hormone and IGF-1","B":"Somatostatin, which acts on somatotropes to decrease release of growth hormone and lower circulating IGF-1","C":"Corticotropin-releasing hormone, which acts on corticotropes to increase release of ACTH and adrenal cortisol","D":"Gonadotropin-releasing hormone, which acts on gonadotropes to increase release of LH and FSH","E":"Thyrotropin-releasing hormone, which acts on thyrotropes to increase release of TSH and thyroid hormone"},"explanation":"First Aid 2025 (p 332) lists tesamorelin as an analog of growth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH) used to treat HIV-associated lipodystrophy. GHRH, a 44-amino-acid peptide, binds receptors on somatotropes and raises cAMP, increasing GH release and GH synthesis (Guyton Ch 76); GH in turn raises IGF-1 and mobilizes fatty acids from adipose tissue for energy. A somatostatin analog such as octreotide would lower GH and IGF-1, which is the treatment for acromegaly rather than for fat accumulation. CRH analogs would raise ACTH and cortisol, which promotes central fat rather than reducing it. GnRH analogs act on gonadotropes; given continuously, as with leuprolide, they suppress the gonadal axis. TRH stimulates TSH and prolactin and has no role in lipodystrophy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypothalamic hormone analogs: tesamorelin = GHRH (HIV-associated lipodystrophy); leuprolide = GnRH (continuous dosing suppresses the gonadal axis); octreotide = somatostatin (lowers GH) (First Aid 2025 p 332; Katzung Ch 37). COMLEX: GHRH acts through Gs and cAMP in somatotropes, with short-term release and long-term synthesis of GH (Guyton Ch 76).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — growth hormone increases mobilization and use of fatty acids from adipose tissue","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"A somatostatin analog such as ","C":"CRH analogs would raise ACTH a","E":"TRH stimulates TSH and prolact"}},{"id":"sdl5_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Match each anterior pituitary hormone with its corresponding hypothalamic releasing or inhibitory hormone.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with metastatic castration-sensitive prostate cancer is started on leuprolide injections every 3 months. His oncologist pre-treats him with bicalutamide (an androgen receptor antagonist) for 2 weeks before the first leuprolide dose. One week after beginning leuprolide, his PSA rises from 14 to 22 ng/mL before declining to 0.8 ng/mL at 6 weeks. Which mechanism best explains the initial PSA rise following leuprolide initiation?","choices":{"B":"Leuprolide directly stimulates prostatic PSA gene transcription via GnRH receptor expression in prostate epithelial cells before systemic testosterone declines","A":"Bicalutamide competitively inhibits androgen receptors and temporarily blocks the normal negative feedback on LH, causing a rebound LH and testosterone elevation","C":"Leuprolide initially acts as a GnRH agonist, stimulating LH and FSH release before receptor downregulation occurs, so a transient testosterone surge drives PSA","D":"Continuous GnRH receptor stimulation immediately causes receptor sensitization rather than desensitization in the first week, uniquely elevating LH beyond baseline","E":"Leuprolide displaces endogenous GnRH from portal blood binding proteins, transiently increasing free hypothalamic GnRH available to pituitary gonadotropes"},"explanation":"Leuprolide is a GnRH receptor agonist. On initial administration, it acts as a stimulant on GnRH receptors, causing an acute LH and FSH surge (\"flare\") and a resulting testosterone spike. This testosterone flare can stimulate prostate cancer growth (PSA rise, bone pain flare). After the first 7–10 days of continuous receptor stimulation (Katzung Ch 37), GnRH receptors downregulate and uncouple, reducing LH/FSH and eventually dropping testosterone to castrate levels. Bicalutamide is pre-given to block androgen receptors during the flare window, protecting against tumor stimulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leuprolide flare = initial agonist effect before downregulation. Antiandrogen coverage (bicalutamide) during flare is standard of care for prostate cancer. GnRH agonists used in central precocious puberty, endometriosis, uterine fibroids, and prostate cancer. COMLEX: Leuprolide pharmacology connects HPG axis physiology to oncology and reproductive medicine.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — GHRH and somatostatin regulation parallels GnRH pulsatility as examples of hypothalamic control over anterior pituitary hormone secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Bicalutamide is pre-given to b"}},{"id":"sdl5_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how hypothalamic releasing hormones control anterior pituitary secretion of hormones that regulate target endocrine glands.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with acromegaly has a pituitary macroadenoma that could not be completely resected surgically. Post-operatively, his GH remains elevated at 8.4 ng/mL and IGF-1 is 640 ng/mL. He is started on octreotide, a somatostatin analog. At 3-month follow-up, GH falls to 1.1 ng/mL and IGF-1 normalizes. Which mechanism best explains octreotide's ability to suppress GH secretion from the adenoma?","choices":{"C":"Somatostatin analog binding to SSTR2 receptors on somatotropes activates Gi proteins, inhibiting adenylyl cyclase and reducing cAMP, thereby suppressing GH exocytosis","A":"Somatostatin analog binding blocks GHRH receptors on somatotropes competitively, preventing GHRH-mediated adenylyl cyclase activation without affecting basal cAMP levels","B":"Somatostatin analog binding to IGF-1 receptors on the somatotrope tumor reverses IGF-1-mediated positive autocrine feedback that sustains autonomous GH production","D":"Somatostatin analog stimulates dopamine release from tuberoinfundibular neurons, which then travels through the portal system to inhibit somatotrope GH secretion indirectly","E":"Somatostatin analog activates GH receptor-coupled negative feedback within the anterior pituitary, triggering Janus kinase inhibition and suppression of GH secretion"},"explanation":"Somatostatin (and its analog octreotide) acts on somatostatin receptor subtypes, primarily SSTR2, on somatotropes. SSTR2 is Gi-coupled: receptor activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase, reducing cAMP and PKA activity, which suppresses GH vesicle exocytosis. This opposes the Gs-coupled cAMP increase driven by GHRH. Additionally, somatostatin inhibits calcium influx into somatotropes, further suppressing exocytosis. Octreotide/lanreotide are first-line medical therapy for acromegaly when surgery is incomplete or contraindicated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Somatostatin → Gi → decreased cAMP → decreased GH (and TSH). Octreotide analog is used for acromegaly and carcinoid tumors. GHRH → Gs → increased cAMP → increased GH. The Gs/Gi opposition between GHRH and somatostatin is a high-yield signaling concept. COMLEX: Octreotide is tested for both acromegaly treatment and carcinoid syndrome management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Somatostatin and GHRH regulation of growth hormone secretion provides the physiologic basis for octreotide pharmacology in acromegaly","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl5_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how hypothalamic releasing hormones control anterior pituitary secretion of hormones that regulate target endocrine glands.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old elite distance runner presents with secondary amenorrhea for 8 months. Her BMI is 17.8 kg/m2. LH is 1.2 mIU/mL (low), FSH is 1.8 mIU/mL (low), estradiol is 20 pg/mL (low), and prolactin is 9 ng/mL. MRI shows no pituitary mass. Her GnRH secretion is identified as having suppressed and infrequent pulses. Restoration of normal pulsatile GnRH secretion would be expected to produce which effect on her anterior pituitary?","choices":{"B":"Restoration of FSH and LH secretion by preventing GnRH receptor downregulation, allowing normal follicular development and ovulatory cycles to resume","A":"Suppression of FSH and LH secretion because GnRH receptors on her gonadotropes have undergone prolonged desensitization during the period of low pulsatile frequency","C":"Selective restoration of FSH without LH because LH secretion requires higher GnRH pulse amplitudes that cannot be generated by stress-suppressed hypothalamic neurons","D":"Immediate restoration of normal estradiol and ovulation within one menstrual cycle because gonadotrope reserves are fully intact after the period of suppression","E":"Increased prolactin secretion alongside FSH and LH because normalization of GnRH pulsatility removes the negative feedback that GnRH previously exerted on lactotropes"},"explanation":"In hypothalamic amenorrhea, GnRH pulse frequency and amplitude are reduced by negative energy balance and stress signals, leading to low FSH and LH and functional hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. Unlike GnRH analog-induced downregulation (caused by excess continuous stimulation), this state involves insufficient receptor stimulation. Restoration of physiologic pulsatile GnRH — through weight restoration, reduced training, or pulsatile GnRH pump therapy — maintains GnRH receptor expression on gonadotropes and allows FSH/LH secretion to resume. Recovery of ovulatory cycles may take weeks to months.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Female athlete triad = low energy availability + low BMI + amenorrhea + low bone density. Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea has low LH, low FSH, low estradiol (hypogonadotropic pattern). Treatment is weight gain and energy balance, not hormonal replacement alone. COMLEX: Female athlete triad is a high-yield COMLEX clinical presentation linking exercise physiology to HPG axis suppression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — Portal system dynamics and hypothalamic hormone delivery establish how reduced GnRH pulse frequency translates to anterior pituitary gonadotropin deficiency","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Unlike GnRH analog-induced dow","D":"Recovery of ovulatory cycles m"}},{"id":"sdl5_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how hypothalamic releasing hormones control anterior pituitary secretion of hormones that regulate target endocrine glands.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man presents with fatigue, headaches, and progressive acromegalic features. Workup reveals an elevated IGF-1 and failure of GH suppression on oral glucose tolerance test. MRI shows a normal-sized pituitary gland without discrete adenoma. CT of the chest and abdomen identifies a 3.2 cm pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor. GHRH level is markedly elevated at 1,240 pg/mL. Which mechanism best explains his acromegaly in the absence of a pituitary adenoma?","choices":{"A":"Ectopic GHRH from the pancreatic tumor travels through systemic circulation to activate somatotrope GHRH receptors via Gs-cAMP signaling, causing diffuse somatotrope hyperplasia and excess GH secretion","B":"Ectopic IGF-1 secretion from the pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor bypasses the pituitary entirely and directly stimulates peripheral tissue growth independent of GH or GHRH","C":"Ectopic GH secretion from the pancreatic tumor at levels too low to detect as a discrete mass, but sufficient to drive systemic IGF-1 elevation and acromegalic changes","D":"Ectopic somatostatin suppression from the pancreatic tumor removes hypothalamic inhibition of the normal pituitary, allowing constitutive GH excess from normal somatotropes","E":"Ectopic CRH from the pancreatic tumor cross-activates GHRH receptors on somatotropes via shared Gs protein coupling, driving GH hypersecretion as an off-target effect"},"explanation":"Ectopic GHRH secretion from a pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor (or bronchial carcinoid) is a rare but high-yield cause of acromegaly. Unlike pituitary GH-secreting adenomas, ectopic GHRH causes diffuse somatotrope hyperplasia (not a focal adenoma) because all somatotropes are continuously stimulated. GHRH binds its Gs-coupled receptor, activating adenylyl cyclase, increasing cAMP, and driving GH secretion and somatotrope proliferation. Diagnosis requires measuring GHRH levels and imaging the chest and abdomen for neuroendocrine tumors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ectopic GHRH from carcinoid or pancreatic NET causes acromegaly with somatotrope hyperplasia, not a focal pituitary adenoma — the key distinguishing feature on MRI. GHRH-secreting tumors should be suspected when MRI is negative in a patient with classic acromegaly biochemistry. COMLEX: Neuroendocrine tumors and ectopic hormone syndromes are high-yield for both Step 1 and COMLEX boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — Major stimulators and inhibitors of GH secretion provide the framework for understanding ectopic GHRH as a non-physiologic stimulus of somatotrope function","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl5_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Predict the consequences of over or underproduction of the pituitary hormones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old male is referred for evaluation of extreme tall stature. He is 7 feet 1 inch tall and has been growing rapidly since age 11. His facial features have become increasingly coarse over the past two years, and he has developed enlarged hands and feet. He has recently been found to have a fasting glucose of 138 mg/dL. His epiphyseal plates are nearly closed on bilateral hand X-rays. Which single diagnosis best accounts for the full constellation of findings?","choices":{"E":"A GH-secreting pituitary adenoma causing gigantism that transitions to acromegaly as the epiphyses fuse, with secondary diabetes from GH excess","A":"Constitutional tall stature with concomitant early-onset type 2 diabetes mellitus from obesity-related insulin resistance unrelated to any hormonal excess","B":"Marfan syndrome with arachnodactyly causing tall stature and peripheral joint changes, plus incidental hyperglycemia from glucocorticoid use for musculoskeletal pain","C":"McCune-Albright syndrome with activating GNAS mutation causing autonomous GH secretion limited to gigantism without membranous bone involvement","D":"Acromegaly alone from a late-adolescent GH-secreting tumor that developed after epiphyseal closure, producing only membranous bone changes without prior longitudinal growth excess"},"explanation":"This patient has a GH-secreting pituitary adenoma causing gigantism since childhood (height excess from open plates) now transitioning to acromegaly features as plates close (coarse features, enlarged acral structures). GH causes insulin resistance by opposing insulin signaling, leading to secondary diabetes mellitus (occurring in about 10–15% of acromegaly patients per Marks Ch 41). The coexistence of extreme height from childhood plus acromegalic features in an adolescent strongly suggests a GH adenoma spanning epiphyseal fusion. IGF-1 and GH non-suppression on OGTT would confirm the diagnosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH-secreting tumor before epiphyseal fusion = gigantism. Same tumor persisting after fusion = combined gigantism and acromegaly. Diabetes from GH excess is a direct consequence of GH anti-insulin actions (increased gluconeogenesis, decreased glucose uptake). COMLEX: Gigantism with acromegalic features is a named CP3 physical exam finding — the height plus coarse features combination is a classic clinical presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — GH metabolic effects including decreased glucose utilization and insulin resistance explain the hyperglycemia and diabetes seen in this patient","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl5_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Predict the consequences of over or underproduction of the pituitary hormones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman who received external beam radiation to the pituitary for a non-secretory macroadenoma 10 years ago now presents with progressive fatigue, 15-pound weight gain, cold intolerance, decreased libido, and orthostatic hypotension. Lab results show: free T4 0.4 ng/dL, TSH 0.8 mIU/L, cortisol 3.2 mcg/dL, ACTH 8 pg/mL (reference 10–60), FSH 2.1 mIU/mL, LH 1.4 mIU/mL, estradiol 14 pg/mL, GH 0.2 ng/mL, IGF-1 46 ng/mL. Which pattern of hormone deficiencies does this represent?","choices":{"C":"Panhypopituitarism with secondary hypothyroidism, secondary adrenal insufficiency, hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, and GH deficiency from radiation-induced loss of anterior pituitary trophic hormones","A":"Primary hypothyroidism combined with Addison disease in a patient with autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 2, consistent with the combination of low cortisol and low T4","B":"Cushing disease in remission following radiation therapy, with the current low cortisol representing post-treatment suppression rather than true panhypopituitarism","D":"Isolated central adrenal insufficiency without pituitary involvement, caused by radiation-induced hypothalamic CRH deficiency with a structurally intact anterior pituitary","E":"Sheehan syndrome from postpartum pituitary necrosis at the time of delivery 10 years ago, incidentally discovered after radiation therapy for an unrelated incidental mass"},"explanation":"This patient has panhypopituitarism from radiation damage to anterior pituitary cells. The key findings are low free T4 with inappropriately low or normal TSH (secondary hypothyroidism), low cortisol with low ACTH (secondary adrenal insufficiency), low sex hormones with low gonadotropins (secondary hypogonadism), and low GH/IGF-1. In primary gland failure, the pituitary trophic hormone would be elevated. Here, all trophic hormones are low, confirming anterior pituitary origin. Radiation-induced hypopituitarism develops years after treatment and requires lifelong hormone replacement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secondary hypothyroidism = low T4 + low or normal TSH. Secondary adrenal insufficiency = low cortisol + low ACTH. In APS type 2, TSH would be elevated (primary hypothyroidism). Distinguishing primary vs. secondary gland failure by trophic hormone level is a cornerstone board skill. COMLEX: Panhypopituitarism after pituitary radiation is a high-yield scenario for integrating endocrine axes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — Hypopituitarism pathology and management directly addresses panhypopituitarism from pituitary mass, radiation, and apoplexy","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"In primary gland failure, the "}},{"id":"sdl5_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Predict the consequences of over or underproduction of the pituitary hormones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman has a serum prolactin of 380 ng/mL. She has had no menstrual periods for 18 months and bilateral galactorrhea. DEXA scan reveals lumbar spine bone density 2.4 SD below age-matched controls. MRI reveals a 1.4 cm pituitary macroadenoma. She is started on cabergoline. After 6 months, prolactin normalizes, menses resume, and BMD is improving. Which mechanism best explains both her amenorrhea and her accelerated bone loss prior to treatment?","choices":{"A":"Hyperprolactinemia suppresses hypothalamic GnRH pulsatility, reducing FSH and LH, leading to estrogen deficiency that causes both anovulatory amenorrhea and decreased bone mineral density","B":"Prolactin directly activates osteoclast prolactin receptors in trabecular bone, stimulating bone resorption independent of estrogen levels or HPG axis suppression","C":"Prolactin stimulates cortisol production from the adrenal glands via a cross-regulatory pathway, causing cortisol-mediated bone loss and hypothalamic amenorrhea","D":"The macroadenoma compresses the pituitary stalk, simultaneously blocking dopamine delivery and reducing FSH and LH from direct mass effect on gonadotropes","E":"Elevated prolactin directly inhibits endometrial estrogen receptors, preventing menstrual cycle initiation independent of circulating estrogen or gonadotropin levels"},"explanation":"Hyperprolactinemia suppresses hypothalamic GnRH pulsatility, reducing LH and FSH secretion and resulting in hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. The resulting estrogen deficiency causes anovulation and amenorrhea, as well as accelerated bone resorption (estrogen normally inhibits osteoclast activity). Both amenorrhea and bone loss improve when prolactin is normalized. Cabergoline, a D2 receptor agonist, is the preferred dopamine agonist for prolactinomas due to its superior efficacy and tolerability compared to bromocriptine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyperprolactinemia → suppresses GnRH → low FSH/LH → low estrogen → amenorrhea + osteoporosis. Cabergoline is preferred over bromocriptine for prolactinoma. Prolactinomas are the most common functional pituitary adenomas. COMLEX: Prolactinoma is a named CP3 pituitary condition — the triad of amenorrhea, galactorrhea, and bone loss should immediately suggest hyperprolactinemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — Hyperpituitarism pathology covers prolactinoma diagnosis, clinical features, and dopamine agonist management in detail","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"}],"6":[{"id":"sdl6_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain growth hormone's three primary metabolic effects: increased protein synthesis, increased fat utilization, and decreased glucose utilization.","batch":1,"stem":"Which set of three metabolic effects correctly summarizes the primary actions of growth hormone on body tissues?","choices":{"A":"Increased protein synthesis in most cells, increased mobilization and utilization of fatty acids from adipose tissue, and decreased glucose utilization throughout the body","B":"Increased glucose uptake by skeletal muscle and adipose tissue, decreased fatty acid mobilization to conserve fat stores, and increased protein catabolism to supply amino acids for gluconeogenesis","C":"Increased protein synthesis in most cells, decreased fatty acid mobilization by inhibiting hormone-sensitive lipase, and increased glucose uptake in skeletal muscle via GLUT4 translocation","D":"Increased lipolysis from adipose tissue, increased hepatic glycogenolysis to raise blood glucose, and decreased protein synthesis to redirect amino acids toward gluconeogenesis","E":"Increased glucose utilization by stimulating peripheral insulin receptors, increased protein catabolism to maintain nitrogen balance, and decreased fat mobilization from visceral adipose depots"},"explanation":"GH has three primary metabolic effects: it promotes protein synthesis by enhancing amino acid transport, ribosomal translation, and DNA transcription; it stimulates lipolysis to release fatty acids for energy, sparing carbohydrates and proteins; and it decreases glucose utilization by inducing insulin resistance. Together, GH enhances body protein, decreases fat stores, and conserves carbohydrates. Its effects are sometimes called \"diabetogenic\" because of the resulting hyperglycemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH = increased protein synthesis + increased fat utilization + decreased glucose use (diabetogenic). FA: GH increases insulin resistance. COMLEX: GH excess in acromegaly produces the same metabolic triad clinically — lean body habitus with hyperglycemia and increased muscle mass.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Consequences of GH overproduction include metabolic changes explored further here","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl6_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain growth hormone's three primary metabolic effects: increased protein synthesis, increased fat utilization, and decreased glucose utilization.","batch":1,"stem":"Growth hormone promotes protein deposition through three distinct cellular mechanisms. Which of the following is described as the most important long-term mechanism for sustained new protein synthesis under GH influence?","choices":{"D":"Increased transcription of DNA to form new RNA in the nucleus, which occurs over 24 to 48 hours and provides the sustained mRNA needed for ongoing protein production","A":"Enhanced transport of amino acids through cell membranes into the intracellular compartment, which raises intracellular amino acid concentrations for ribosomal use","B":"Enhanced translation of existing mRNA at ribosomes in the cytoplasm, which causes immediate increases in protein output without requiring new RNA synthesis","C":"Decreased catabolism of intracellular proteins by inhibiting the ubiquitin-proteasome pathway, which conserves existing proteins and creates a positive nitrogen balance","E":"Activation of insulin receptor substrate signaling in muscle cells, which drives ribosomal biogenesis through mTOR and provides immediate structural scaffolding for new protein synthesis"},"explanation":"GH promotes protein synthesis through three mechanisms: enhanced amino acid transport into cells, enhanced ribosomal translation of existing mRNA, and increased nuclear transcription of DNA to form new RNA. Guyton and Hall specifically identifies the nuclear transcription mechanism as potentially the most important long-term function because it provides a sustained supply of new mRNA over 24-48 hours. GH also reduces protein catabolism by mobilizing fatty acids for energy, sparing cellular proteins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH anabolic effects — short-term: amino acid uptake + ribosomal translation; long-term: DNA transcription. The \"protein sparer\" role of GH via fat mobilization is a classic board concept. COMLEX: GH deficiency leads to decreased lean body mass and increased fat deposition — the reverse of GH excess.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Consequences of GH overproduction include metabolic changes explored further here","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl6_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain growth hormone's three primary metabolic effects: increased protein synthesis, increased fat utilization, and decreased glucose utilization.","batch":1,"stem":"Growth hormone's effects on glucose metabolism are described as \"diabetogenic.\" Which mechanism is primarily responsible for GH-induced insulin resistance and hyperglycemia?","choices":{"E":"GH inhibits GLUT4 gene transcription in skeletal muscle, permanently reducing the total pool of insulin-responsive glucose transporter molecules available for membrane insertion after insulin stimulation","A":"GH directly inhibits pancreatic beta cell function by activating somatostatin-secreting delta cells in the islets of Langerhans, suppressing insulin secretion before insulin resistance develops","B":"GH activates glucagon gene transcription in pancreatic alpha cells, causing sustained glucagonemia that drives hepatic glucose production via glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis independently of insulin signaling","C":"GH depletes hepatic glycogen stores through activation of glycogen phosphorylase, reducing the liver's glucose buffering capacity and causing postprandial hyperglycemia from unregulated glycogenolysis","D":"GH attenuates insulin's ability to stimulate glucose uptake in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue while also inhibiting insulin's suppression of hepatic gluconeogenesis, leading to elevated blood glucose and compensatory insulin secretion"},"explanation":"GH induces insulin resistance, attenuating insulin's actions to stimulate glucose uptake and utilization in skeletal muscle and fat, and to inhibit hepatic gluconeogenesis. This leads to elevated blood glucose and compensatory increased insulin secretion. The mechanism involves GH-induced lipolysis: elevated plasma free fatty acids impair insulin signaling at the tissue level. Because of this, GH excess produces metabolic disturbances similar to type 2 diabetes; however, acromegaly patients are typically lean (unlike T2DM), because GH also mobilizes fat for energy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH = diabetogenic via insulin resistance. Elevated FFA from lipolysis → impairs insulin signaling in muscle and liver. Acromegaly patients lean but hyperglycemic, unlike T2DM patients. FA: GH increases insulin resistance. COMLEX: Chronic GH excess in acromegaly can produce secondary diabetes mellitus — a high-yield metabolic complication.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Consequences of GH overproduction include metabolic changes explored further here","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl6_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the role of insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1) in mediating growth hormone's effects and compare their duration of action.","batch":1,"stem":"Insulin-like growth factor-1 mediates many of the growth-promoting effects of growth hormone. Which organ is the primary source of circulating IGF-1 in the blood?","choices":{"C":"The liver, which produces and secretes IGF-1 into the bloodstream in direct response to GH receptor activation, with plasma IGF-1 levels closely following the rate of GH secretion","A":"The anterior pituitary, which co-secretes IGF-1 alongside GH from somatotrope secretory granules during each pulsatile GH release episode throughout the day","B":"Skeletal muscle, which is the dominant source of circulating IGF-1 because it is the largest mass of GH-responsive tissue in the body and produces the most IGF-1 per unit of GH","D":"The adrenal cortex, which synthesizes IGF-1 under combined stimulation by ACTH and GH to mediate the cortisol-related tissue growth that occurs during stress and anabolic states","E":"Epiphyseal chondrocytes, which produce and secrete sufficient IGF-1 into the systemic circulation during childhood to maintain plasma IGF-1 levels adequate for full-body growth promotion"},"explanation":"GH stimulates the liver — and to a much lesser extent other tissues — to produce insulin-like growth factors, the most important of which is IGF-1. Plasma IGF-1 concentration closely follows the rate of GH secretion, making it a reliable clinical marker of GH activity. Children with IGF-1 deficiency fail to grow even when GH is normal or elevated, as seen in African pygmies and Laron syndrome. The liver is thus the critical intermediary between GH secretion and systemic growth effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IGF-1 = somatomedin C, produced primarily by the liver in response to GH. Serum IGF-1 is the preferred screening test for acromegaly because GH levels fluctuate. COMLEX: Low IGF-1 with high GH = GH resistance (Laron syndrome) — distinguishable from GH deficiency by the high GH level.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — GH is the primary secretory product of somatotropes and the upstream driver of IGF-1 synthesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl6_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the role of insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1) in mediating growth hormone's effects and compare their duration of action.","batch":1,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy presents with proportionate short stature. His serum GH is markedly elevated at 32 ng/mL. IGF-1 is undetectable. Genetic testing reveals a loss-of-function mutation in the gene encoding the GH receptor. Which mechanism explains his growth failure?","choices":{"D":"The mutated GH receptor cannot undergo productive dimerization or activate JAK2 kinase, so downstream IGF-1 synthesis in the liver cannot occur despite elevated GH levels","A":"Elevated GH tonically suppresses GnRH secretion from the hypothalamus, causing gonadotropin deficiency that impairs the pubertal growth spurt without affecting hepatic IGF-1 synthesis","B":"The elevated GH desensitizes pituitary somatotropes through autofeedback, reducing GH pulse amplitude below the minimum threshold required to stimulate any epiphyseal chondrocyte growth","C":"The GH receptor mutation causes misfolding of IGFBP-3, which then degrades any IGF-1 that is synthesized before it can reach epiphyseal growth plate chondrocytes in the bone","E":"Elevated circulating GH competes with IGF-1 for binding to the shared IGF-1 receptor on chondrocytes, blocking the growth-promoting effects of any residual IGF-1 present in the blood"},"explanation":"This is Laron syndrome, caused by a GH receptor loss-of-function mutation. GH cannot activate JAK2 kinase or STAT5 because the receptor cannot dimerize or transduce signal; therefore, the liver cannot produce IGF-1. Patients have elevated GH from the absence of IGF-1 negative feedback, but low IGF-1 and proportionate dwarfism. Treatment is recombinant IGF-1. Laron syndrome is the classic example of GH resistance distinct from GH deficiency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Laron syndrome = GH receptor mutation → high GH + low IGF-1 + short stature. African pygmies have a similar phenotype from congenital inability to synthesize IGF-1. Treatment: recombinant IGF-1, not GH. FA: GH excess causes acromegaly; GH receptor loss causes GH resistance despite elevated GH. COMLEX: Distinguishing GH deficiency from GH resistance using GH and IGF-1 levels is high-yield.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — GH is the primary secretory product of somatotropes and the upstream driver of IGF-1 synthesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl6_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the role of insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1) in mediating growth hormone's effects and compare their duration of action.","batch":1,"stem":"Growth hormone is secreted in short pulses with a plasma half-life of less than 20 minutes, yet growth-promoting effects persist for many hours after each pulse. Which property of IGF-1 explains this prolonged downstream effect?","choices":{"B":"IGF-1 binds tightly to a specific carrier protein in the blood that is also produced in response to GH, slowing IGF-1 release to tissues and extending its half-life to approximately 20 hours","A":"IGF-1 is a smaller peptide than GH and therefore filtered more slowly by the kidney glomerulus, reducing renal clearance and explaining the much longer plasma half-life compared to GH","C":"IGF-1 binds nonspecifically to plasma albumin, which is present in vast excess and acts as a nonspecific reservoir that slowly releases the hormone over many hours into the interstitium","D":"GH is rapidly inactivated by a specific hepatic GH-binding protein that does not cross-react with IGF-1, creating an apparent discrepancy in clearance rates that is entirely attributable to GH instability","E":"IGF-1 is sequestered within platelet alpha granules during each GH pulse and is then released slowly over hours during routine platelet activation in peripheral capillary beds throughout the day"},"explanation":"GH attaches weakly to plasma proteins and has a half-life under 20 minutes, making it rapidly cleared. IGF-1, by contrast, binds strongly to a specific carrier protein (IGFBP-3 together with an acid-labile subunit) that is produced in response to GH. This ternary complex slows IGF-1 release to tissues, extending its half-life to approximately 20 hours. The prolonged action of IGF-1 ensures sustained growth-promoting effects between pulsatile GH bursts, which explains why serum IGF-1 is a much more stable and clinically useful marker than GH itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH half-life less than 20 minutes vs. IGF-1 half-life approximately 20 hours due to IGFBP-3 binding. Serum IGF-1 is the preferred diagnostic test for acromegaly because it reflects 24-hour mean GH levels, while GH fluctuates. COMLEX: The IGFBP-3 carrier system is the reason a single serum IGF-1 level reliably screens for GH excess or deficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — GH is the primary secretory product of somatotropes and the upstream driver of IGF-1 synthesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl6_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how growth hormone stimulates bone and cartilage growth.","batch":1,"stem":"Growth hormone stimulates bone and cartilage growth through three cellular mechanisms. Which mechanism is unique to GH and involves converting existing cartilage cells into bone-forming cells?","choices":{"D":"Conversion of chondrocytes into osteogenic cells at sites of endochondral ossification, enabling direct deposition of new bone in areas previously containing cartilage matrix","A":"Stimulation of periosteal osteoblasts to deposit new bone matrix on the external surfaces of existing cortical bone, increasing overall bone diameter and cortical thickness progressively","B":"Activation of osteoclasts to resorb older bone matrix while simultaneously stimulating osteoblasts to deposit more new bone in resorption lacunae, producing net bone expansion","C":"Stimulation of epiphyseal chondrocytes to undergo accelerated mitosis, increasing the total number of chondrocytes available for producing cartilage matrix in the growth plate","E":"Direct deposition of hydroxyapatite crystals into existing collagen scaffolds within cartilage by GH-activated osteoblast precursors recruited from the periosteal cambium layer"},"explanation":"GH stimulates bone and cartilage growth through three mechanisms: increased protein deposition by chondrocytic and osteogenic cells, increased reproduction of these cells, and conversion of chondrocytes into osteogenic cells to deposit new bone. The third mechanism is unique to GH's action at sites of endochondral ossification. Together these three mechanisms account for GH's skeletal effects, complemented by IGF-1's parallel anabolic effects on the same cell types. Periosteal apposition, remodeling by osteoclasts and osteoblasts, and chondrocyte mitosis are real growth processes but do not convert one cell type into another, and GH does not deposit hydroxyapatite itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH bone growth mechanisms — three processes: protein deposition, cell reproduction, and chondrocyte-to-osteocyte conversion. IGF-1 mediates similar effects on bone. COMLEX: These mechanisms explain why GH excess produces bone enlargement in acromegaly, while GH deficiency in childhood causes proportionate dwarfism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Gigantism and acromegaly arise from GH excess acting on the same bone growth mechanisms explored here","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Periosteal apposition, remodel","B":"Periosteal apposition, remodel","C":"Periosteal apposition, remodel","E":"Periosteal apposition, remodel"}},{"id":"sdl6_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how growth hormone stimulates bone and cartilage growth.","batch":1,"stem":"Long bones grow in length during childhood and adolescence but cease longitudinal growth by late adolescence even in the presence of adequate GH. Which process directly explains why longitudinal bone growth can no longer occur after late adolescence?","choices":{"C":"Exhaustion of epiphyseal cartilage during adolescence results in bony fusion between the shaft and the epiphyses, eliminating the growth plate as a template for new longitudinal bone formation","A":"GH receptor expression in epiphyseal chondrocytes is selectively silenced during puberty by rising sex hormone levels, blocking GH from stimulating further chondrocyte proliferation at the growth plate","B":"Increased somatostatin tone in late adolescence progressively suppresses GH pulses to below the minimum threshold needed to sustain chondrocyte proliferation at the epiphyseal cartilage","D":"Estrogen and testosterone from puberty reduce hepatic IGF-1 output through negative feedback on the GH receptor, creating a relative IGF-1 deficiency that halts epiphyseal chondrocyte mitosis","E":"Periosteal osteoblasts overgrow the periphery of the epiphyseal cartilage during puberty, physically displacing chondrocytes and replacing cartilage tissue with cortical bone from the outside inward"},"explanation":"Long bones grow at the epiphyseal cartilages, where new cartilage is deposited and then converted to bone, elongating the shaft and pushing epiphyses apart. By late adolescence, epiphyseal cartilage is progressively exhausted and bony fusion occurs between the shaft and epiphyses. At this point, no further longitudinal growth is possible. However, GH can still stimulate osteoblasts to thicken bones — hence why acromegaly causes bone enlargement but not height increase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epiphyseal fusion = end of longitudinal bone growth. Bone age X-ray assesses growth plate status. GH excess before fusion = gigantism; after fusion = acromegaly. COMLEX: Recognizing the distinction between open and fused growth plates is essential for understanding why the same pituitary tumor produces different presentations at different ages.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Gigantism and acromegaly arise from GH excess acting on the same bone growth mechanisms explored here","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl6_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how growth hormone stimulates bone and cartilage growth.","batch":1,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with acromegaly is noted to have progressive thickening of the skull vault and jaw even though his long bones show no further lengthening. Which mechanism accounts for continued bone growth in these sites after epiphyseal fusion?","choices":{"B":"GH stimulates osteoblasts in the bone periosteum to deposit new bone matrix on the surfaces of existing cortical bone, allowing membranous bones without growth plates to continue thickening","A":"GH stimulates chondrocyte proliferation in residual cartilaginous sutures between skull plates that remain unfused in adults, allowing compensatory calvarial expansion via endochondral ossification","C":"GH activates fibroblasts within the dura mater to transdifferentiate into osteogenic progenitors that deposit new bone on the endosteal surface of the calvarium from the inside outward","D":"GH upregulates IGF-1 receptor expression specifically on craniofacial osteoblasts but not on long bone periosteum, directing all GH-mediated bone growth preferentially to membranous bones in adults","E":"GH enhances renal calcium and phosphate reabsorption, raising serum mineral levels above the solubility threshold for spontaneous hydroxyapatite deposition preferentially in membranous bones"},"explanation":"Even after epiphyseal fusion prevents longitudinal growth, GH continues to stimulate osteoblasts in the bone periosteum to deposit new bone on existing surfaces. When this deposition exceeds osteoclast resorption, bones thicken. This is especially pronounced in membranous bones: the jaw can continue to protrude forward, the forehead can enlarge, and the nose can grow to twice normal size. GH-stimulated osteoblast activity accounts for all the skeletal changes of acromegaly in adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH excess after fusion = acromegaly — enlarged hands, feet, jaw, nose, frontal bossing from periosteal osteoblast stimulation. GH stimulates osteoblasts strongly. COMLEX: Acromegaly is a named CP3 physical exam finding — enlarged acral structures and coarse facial features from periosteal bone deposition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Gigantism and acromegaly arise from GH excess acting on the same bone growth mechanisms explored here","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl6_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the details of growth hormone receptor signaling.","batch":1,"stem":"Growth hormone exerts its effects on target tissues by binding to a cell surface receptor. Which receptor class correctly describes the GH receptor and its signaling mechanism?","choices":{"C":"A nonreceptor tyrosine kinase receptor belonging to the class I cytokine receptor superfamily, which lacks intrinsic enzymatic activity and signals through non-covalently associated JAK2 kinase molecules","A":"A G protein-coupled receptor linked to the Gs subunit that activates adenylyl cyclase in target tissues, increasing intracellular cAMP and activating PKA to phosphorylate downstream growth-related substrates","B":"A receptor tyrosine kinase with an intrinsic kinase domain in its intracellular tail, which autophosphorylates on ligand binding and directly recruits SH2-domain adapter proteins to drive IGF-1 synthesis","D":"A nuclear hormone receptor that requires GH to enter target cells by facilitated diffusion and bind DNA response elements to directly activate transcription of IGF-1 and anabolic protein synthesis genes","E":"A receptor serine-threonine kinase similar to the TGF-beta receptor family, which phosphorylates SMAD transcription factors after GH binding to activate IGF-1 and collagen synthesis gene programs"},"explanation":"The GH receptor belongs to the class I cytokine receptor superfamily — a group of nonreceptor tyrosine kinase receptors that have no intrinsic catalytic activity but non-covalently associate with JAK2 kinase. GH, prolactin, leptin, erythropoietin, and several cytokines all use this receptor class. The FA mnemonic \"GET a JAKed PIG\" helps recall this group. This is distinct from receptor tyrosine kinases (insulin, IGF-1 receptors), G protein-coupled receptors, and nuclear receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH receptor = nonreceptor tyrosine kinase, JAK-STAT pathway. FA mnemonic: \"GET a JAKed PIG\" = G-CSF, Erythropoietin, Thrombopoietin; Prolactin, Immunomodulators, GH all use JAK-STAT. \"Think acidophils and cytokines.\" COMLEX: JAK-STAT signaling is high-yield for understanding GH, prolactin, and cytokine receptor signaling in one unified framework.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — JAK-STAT signaling was introduced using the leptin receptor as a model and applies directly to GH receptor signaling","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"This is distinct from receptor","B":"This is distinct from receptor","D":"This is distinct from receptor"}},{"id":"sdl6_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the details of growth hormone receptor signaling.","batch":1,"stem":"When growth hormone binds to its cell surface receptor, what is the immediate downstream molecular event that initiates the JAK-STAT signaling cascade at the receptor?","choices":{"A":"GH receptor monomers directly phosphorylate STAT5 proteins on tyrosine residues through a cryptic kinase domain that is only exposed after ligand-induced conformational changes in the receptor","B":"A single GH molecule binds two GH receptor monomers, inducing receptor homodimerization that brings two JAK2 kinase molecules into proximity and triggers their transphosphorylation and activation","C":"GH receptor binding activates phospholipase C via a Gq protein, generating IP3 to release intracellular calcium and DAG to activate PKC, which then phosphorylates STAT5 on serine residues","D":"GH receptor binding recruits the adapter protein Grb2 and activates the SOS-Ras-Raf-MEK-ERK cascade, with ERK then phosphorylating STAT5 as the final step before nuclear translocation","E":"GH receptor monomers release sequestered cytoplasmic inhibitors of STAT5 called SOCS proteins, allowing constitutively active STAT5 dimers to translocate to the nucleus without further phosphorylation"},"explanation":"The GH receptor is a pre-dimerized or monomeric receptor that, upon GH binding, undergoes conformational change bringing two JAK2 molecules (non-covalently associated with each receptor chain) into proximity. This proximity activates JAK2 through transphosphorylation. Activated JAK2 then phosphorylates tyrosine residues on the receptor itself and on STAT5. One GH molecule binds two receptor monomers simultaneously — this structural mechanism is the basis of GH receptor signaling and why receptor dimerization is required for signal transduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH binds two receptor monomers → dimerization → JAK2 transphosphorylation → STAT5 activation → IGF-1 transcription. Pegvisomant is a GH receptor antagonist: it allows dimerization but blocks the conformational change needed for signaling (Katzung Ch 37). COMLEX: SDL 3 covered JAK-STAT with leptin — GH uses the same cascade with JAK2 and STAT5 as the key molecules.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — JAK-STAT signaling was introduced using the leptin receptor as a model and applies directly to GH receptor signaling","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl6_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the details of growth hormone receptor signaling.","batch":1,"stem":"Following JAK2 activation at the GH receptor, which transcription factor is phosphorylated, dimerizes, and then translocates to the nucleus to drive IGF-1 gene expression in hepatocytes?","choices":{"B":"STAT5, which is phosphorylated on tyrosine by activated JAK2, dimerizes with another phospho-STAT5, translocates to the nucleus, and binds IGF-1 gene promoter elements to activate transcription","A":"NF-kB, which is released from its inhibitor after JAK2 phosphorylation, translocates to the nucleus as a p50-p65 heterodimer, and drives both IGF-1 and inflammatory cytokine gene transcription together","C":"CREB, which is phosphorylated by JAK2 on serine-133, binds CRE sequences in the IGF-1 promoter to activate transcription in a manner similar to PKA-mediated CREB activation in the cAMP pathway","D":"SMAD2 and SMAD3, which form a heterotrimeric complex with SMAD4 after JAK2 activation, translocate to the nucleus, and activate IGF-1 along with collagen synthesis gene programs in hepatocytes","E":"AP-1 heterodimers of Fos and Jun subunits, which are assembled after JAK2 activates the MAPK cascade, then bind GH response elements in the IGF-1 gene promoter to enhance transcription"},"explanation":"After GH-induced JAK2 activation, JAK2 phosphorylates STAT5 (primarily STAT5b in the liver) on a specific tyrosine residue. Phospho-STAT5 undergoes homodimerization and translocates to the nucleus, where it binds gamma-activated sequence (GAS) elements in the IGF-1 gene promoter to activate transcription. This is the canonical JAK-STAT pathway shared by GH, prolactin, and cytokines. SOCS proteins provide negative feedback by inhibiting JAK2 and targeting the receptor for ubiquitin-mediated degradation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: JAK2 → STAT5 phosphorylation → STAT5 dimerization → nuclear translocation → IGF-1 gene activation. This is the same JAK-STAT pathway covered in SDL 3 using the leptin receptor. COMLEX: Understanding JAK-STAT mechanistically connects GH receptor signaling to cytokine receptor pharmacology, including JAK inhibitors used for rheumatoid arthritis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — JAK-STAT signaling was introduced using the leptin receptor as a model and applies directly to GH receptor signaling","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl6_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain growth hormone's three primary metabolic effects: increased protein synthesis, increased fat utilization, and decreased glucose utilization.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with acromegaly from a pituitary somatotrope adenoma presents for metabolic evaluation. His BMI is 23 kg/m2. Fasting glucose is 178 mg/dL, HbA1c is 7.6%, and fasting insulin is markedly elevated. Despite significant hyperglycemia, he has no central obesity and his adipose distribution appears reduced compared to age-matched controls. Which mechanism best explains the combination of glucose intolerance without obesity in this patient?","choices":{"E":"GH excess induces insulin resistance as rising plasma free fatty acids impair insulin signaling in muscle and liver, while also enhancing mobilization of adipose fat for use as a primary energy substrate, producing a lean hyperglycemic phenotype","A":"GH-secreting tumors directly destroy pancreatic beta cells through local inflammation, causing absolute insulin deficiency that produces hyperglycemia while the catecholamine release from adrenal involvement suppresses adipogenesis","B":"GH excess activates hepatic glucagon receptor signaling through cross-receptor activation, causing sustained hepatic glucose output that overwhelms peripheral glucose disposal without affecting adipose tissue lipolysis or mass","C":"The high insulin levels in this patient reflect primary hyperinsulinism from a co-secreting pancreatic tumor, which paradoxically causes insulin-mediated lipolysis instead of lipogenesis through a poorly understood receptor desensitization mechanism","D":"GH excess causes renal glucose wasting by inhibiting tubular SGLT2 transporters, reducing blood glucose buffering capacity and producing apparent hyperglycemia despite normal peripheral insulin sensitivity and preserved adipose tissue"},"explanation":"GH's diabetogenic effect results from insulin resistance, in which GH-induced lipolysis raises plasma free fatty acids that impair insulin receptor signaling in skeletal muscle and fat. At the same time, GH promotes fat mobilization for energy, reducing fat stores and producing a lean body habitus. This explains why acromegaly patients are typically lean with hyperglycemia, contrasting with type 2 diabetes patients who have central obesity. The compensatory hyperinsulinemia reflects intact beta cell response to insulin resistance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH = lean + hyperglycemic due to fat mobilization + insulin resistance. Classic board distinction from T2DM = obese + hyperglycemic. Acromegaly-associated diabetes resolves after tumor resection. FA: GH increases insulin resistance. COMLEX: Acromegaly metabolic complications include diabetes mellitus in about 10–15% of patients (Marks Ch 41) — a named complication tested on boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — Clinical features of acromegaly directly reflect GH's metabolic effects of insulin resistance and fat utilization described in this objective","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl6_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain growth hormone's three primary metabolic effects: increased protein synthesis, increased fat utilization, and decreased glucose utilization.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy from sub-Saharan Africa is evaluated for growth failure. He has severe protein malnutrition (kwashiorkor) with serum GH of 42 ng/mL and undetectable serum IGF-1. Despite adequate caloric intake from carbohydrates, he shows no improvement in growth after 3 days. Protein supplementation begun on day 4 gradually lowers GH and improves growth velocity over 25 days. Which mechanism most directly explains why the carbohydrate diet did not lower GH or restore growth?","choices":{"A":"Protein deficiency causes severe hypoaminoacidemia that prevents hepatic IGF-1 synthesis despite intact GH receptor activation, disconnecting GH levels from actual anabolic growth activity because substrate for IGF-1 production is absent","B":"Carbohydrate feeding elevates blood glucose, which suppresses GH secretion by activating somatostatin, but the hypoaminoacidemia from protein deficiency simultaneously stimulates GH via a competing pathway that overrides glucose inhibition","C":"The GH receptor is reversibly downregulated in protein-deficient states, so even normal hepatic amino acid availability from carbohydrate feeding cannot restore receptor expression quickly enough to stimulate IGF-1 within 3 days","D":"Carbohydrates provide caloric energy but cannot supply the essential amino acids needed for ribosomal translation of IGF-1 mRNA that was already synthesized in response to GH, halting IGF-1 production at the translational step","E":"Protein deficiency depletes plasma IGFBP-3, causing any IGF-1 that is synthesized to be rapidly cleared by the kidney before it can reach target tissues, making plasma IGF-1 undetectable even when hepatic synthesis is occurring normally"},"explanation":"In kwashiorkor, severe protein deficiency causes markedly elevated GH, likely from hypoglycemia, stress, and amino acid-driven hypothalamic signaling. Despite intact GH receptors, the liver cannot synthesize IGF-1 without adequate amino acid substrate. Carbohydrate addition provides energy but not amino acids, so GH remains elevated and growth remains stunted. Protein supplementation restores amino acid availability, normalizes IGF-1, and provides negative feedback to suppress GH. This demonstrates that hepatic IGF-1 synthesis requires both GH receptor activation and adequate amino acid substrate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kwashiorkor = high GH + low IGF-1 due to protein substrate deficiency; treatment is protein refeeding, not GH. Classic Step 1 scenario: high GH does not equal growth without IGF-1. COMLEX: Protein malnutrition disrupts the GH-IGF-1 axis — a clinically relevant pediatric endocrinology concept.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — Major stimulators of GH secretion include protein deficiency and hypoglycemia, explaining the elevated GH in this malnourished patient","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl6_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain growth hormone's three primary metabolic effects: increased protein synthesis, increased fat utilization, and decreased glucose utilization.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old competitive bodybuilder self-administers recombinant GH at supraphysiologic doses for 6 months along with adequate protein and calories. Which combination of metabolic effects is most consistent with chronic pharmacologic GH excess in this setting?","choices":{"B":"Decreased lean body mass from protein wasting, hypoglycemia from enhanced peripheral glucose uptake, and increased visceral fat accumulation from GH-stimulated lipogenesis in adipose depots","A":"Increased lean body mass and muscle protein nitrogen retention, increased plasma free fatty acid levels with reduced adipose tissue stores, and elevated fasting glucose from GH-induced insulin resistance","C":"Increased adipose tissue deposition from GH-stimulated lipogenesis in the fasting state, normal fasting glucose due to compensatory hyperinsulinemia fully correcting GH-induced resistance, and reduced muscle protein synthesis","D":"Uniform weight gain from combined fluid retention, visceral fat accumulation, and muscle hypertrophy, with normal insulin sensitivity because GH and insulin act through convergent signaling pathways in muscle","E":"Increased protein catabolism releasing amino acids for gluconeogenesis, ketoacidosis from excessive fat breakdown, and progressive muscle wasting despite adequate caloric intake due to GH-mediated protein mobilization"},"explanation":"Pharmacologic GH produces all three primary metabolic effects simultaneously: enhanced protein synthesis and muscle growth (increased lean mass), increased lipolysis with elevated FFA and reduced fat stores (fat sparing), and insulin resistance with hyperglycemia. The \"protein sparer\" effect of GH results from fat being used preferentially for energy, protecting muscle protein. This is the anabolic rationale for GH misuse in athletics. Adverse effects include insulin resistance and potential diabetes, carpal tunnel syndrome, and bone thickening.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH excess = lean mass increases + fat decreases + hyperglycemia. Performance-enhancing drug scenario. Adverse effects of GH excess: diabetes, carpal tunnel, organomegaly, acromegalic features with prolonged use. COMLEX: GH misuse connects metabolic physiology to sports medicine — a growing board topic.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — Clinical features of GH excess including metabolic changes are the clinical expression of the three primary GH metabolic effects described in this objective","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl6_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the role of insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1) in mediating growth hormone's effects and compare their duration of action.","batch":2,"stem":"An 8-year-old girl is evaluated for short stature. She was born at the 50th percentile for length but is now below the 3rd percentile. Her father and all paternal relatives have similar short stature. GH stimulation test shows an appropriate GH peak of 24 ng/mL. Serum IGF-1 is undetectable. Growth velocity is markedly reduced. Genetic testing reveals a loss-of-function mutation in the GHR gene encoding the GH receptor. Which diagnosis is most consistent?","choices":{"C":"Laron syndrome from GH receptor mutation causing GH resistance, with high GH from absent IGF-1 negative feedback and undetectable IGF-1 because JAK2-STAT5 signaling cannot proceed","A":"Constitutional growth delay with a delayed bone age that will result in normal adult height once puberty begins and rising sex hormones amplify the GH-IGF-1 axis response in epiphyseal chondrocytes","B":"Growth hormone deficiency from pituitary somatotrope insufficiency, with the low IGF-1 reflecting insufficient GH secretion and the family history representing an autosomal dominant GH gene deletion","D":"Hypothyroidism causing proportionate growth arrest through reduced bone maturation and impaired responsiveness to GH and IGF-1 signaling at the epiphyseal growth plate cartilage","E":"Primary IGF-1 deficiency from a deletion of the IGF1 gene itself, with the GH receptor intact but unable to drive IGF-1 production because the IGF-1 coding sequence is absent from the genome"},"explanation":"Laron syndrome is caused by GH receptor mutations that prevent JAK2 activation and downstream STAT5-driven IGF-1 synthesis. Patients have elevated GH from absent IGF-1 negative feedback, undetectable IGF-1, and proportionate short stature that does not respond to exogenous GH. The family history of short stature is consistent with an autosomal recessive or dominant GH receptor mutation. Treatment is recombinant IGF-1. This distinguishes Laron from GH deficiency (low GH, low IGF-1) and from IGF-1 gene deletion (normal GH receptor function, but absent IGF-1 gene).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Laron syndrome = GHR mutation → high GH + low IGF-1 + short stature + does not respond to GH therapy. Treatment: recombinant IGF-1. Contrast with GH deficiency = low GH + low IGF-1 + responds to GH therapy. COMLEX: Distinguishing GH deficiency from GH resistance using serum GH and IGF-1 levels is a high-yield clinical reasoning task.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — Clinical features of GH deficiency and dwarfism contrast with Laron syndrome where GH is elevated but cannot signal due to receptor mutation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"E":"This distinguishes Laron from "}},{"id":"sdl6_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the role of insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1) in mediating growth hormone's effects and compare their duration of action.","batch":2,"stem":"A clinical biochemist is explaining to students why serum IGF-1 is a more reliable diagnostic test for acromegaly than random serum GH. She notes that GH has a half-time in blood of less than 20 minutes, whereas IGF-1 has a half-time of about 20 hours. Which structural property of the IGF-1 transport system in blood accounts for this prolonged half-life?","choices":{"D":"IGFBP-3 is synthesized at a constant baseline rate independent of GH, so its binding capacity remains saturated at all times and acts as a constant reservoir that limits IGF-1 clearance regardless of acute GH fluctuations","A":"IGF-1 is stored in hepatic secretory granules and released by constitutive exocytosis at a constant slow rate that is independent of acute GH pulses, making IGF-1 levels stable regardless of pulsatile GH patterns","B":"IGF-1 undergoes renal tubular secretion followed by reabsorption in a cycle that recirculates the hormone repeatedly before net clearance occurs, extending its apparent plasma half-life far beyond simple first-order clearance kinetics","C":"IGF-1 binds to red blood cell surface IGF-1 receptors and is slowly released from erythrocytes during capillary transit, extending its apparent plasma half-life through this erythrocyte-mediated reservoir mechanism","E":"IGF-1 forms a ternary complex with IGFBP-3 and an acid-labile subunit, both of which are produced in response to GH, creating a high-molecular-weight carrier complex that slows IGF-1 release to tissues and clearance from plasma"},"explanation":"IGF-1 circulates in a ternary complex with IGFBP-3 and an acid-labile subunit (ALS). Critically, both IGFBP-3 and ALS are themselves produced in response to GH. This ternary complex binds IGF-1 strongly and releases it only slowly to the tissues. The result is an IGF-1 half-life of approximately 20 hours, compared to less than 20 minutes for GH. In acromegaly, sustained GH secretion keeps both IGFBP-3 and ALS elevated, maintaining persistently high IGF-1 levels that provide a stable, integrated marker of GH excess.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IGF-1 half-life approximately 20 hours due to IGFBP-3 ternary complex. GH half-life under 20 minutes. Serum IGF-1 is the screening test for acromegaly (not random GH). COMLEX: The IGFBP-3 ternary complex explains why single random GH measurements are unreliable in acromegaly diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — GHRH drives pulsatile GH secretion whose effects are prolonged and integrated by the IGF-1 half-life mechanism described in this objective","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl6_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the role of insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1) in mediating growth hormone's effects and compare their duration of action.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old girl with proportionate short stature below the 3rd percentile is found to have a homozygous deletion of the IGF1 gene confirmed by genetic testing. Her serum GH is 8 ng/mL and GH stimulation test is normal. She is started on mecasermin injections. Which mechanism best describes how mecasermin restores growth in this patient?","choices":{"E":"Mecasermin is recombinant IGF-1 that directly activates IGF-1 receptors on epiphyseal chondrocytes via the receptor tyrosine kinase pathway, stimulating chondrocyte proliferation and bone growth without requiring GH-mediated hepatic IGF-1 synthesis","A":"Mecasermin binds GH receptors on somatotropes and suppresses excess GH secretion, reducing the elevated GH-driven somatostatin feedback that was paradoxically inhibiting chondrocyte growth in this patient","B":"Mecasermin activates the JAK2-STAT5 pathway in hepatocytes to stimulate production of residual endogenous IGF-1 from any remaining intact IGF1 gene alleles in this compound heterozygous deletion","C":"Mecasermin binds to circulating IGFBP-3 and displaces endogenous IGF-1 fragments from the carrier protein, making otherwise sequestered IGF-1 bioavailable for epiphyseal growth plate stimulation","D":"Mecasermin activates GH receptors on epiphyseal chondrocytes to stimulate local autocrine IGF-1 production from the IGF1 gene, circumventing the need for hepatic IGF-1 by using the bone as an alternative synthesis site"},"explanation":"Mecasermin is recombinant human IGF-1 used to treat primary IGF-1 deficiency (including Laron syndrome and IGF-1 gene deletion). IGF-1 receptors are receptor tyrosine kinases — distinct from GH receptors (which are JAK-STAT). IGF-1 binds its receptor on epiphyseal chondrocytes, causing receptor dimerization, autophosphorylation, and activation of the MAP kinase and PI3K-Akt pathways to drive chondrocyte proliferation and bone growth. This bypasses the GH-liver-IGF-1 axis entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mecasermin = recombinant IGF-1 for Laron syndrome and primary IGF-1 deficiency. IGF-1 receptor = receptor tyrosine kinase, uses MAP kinase pathway. GH receptor = JAK-STAT pathway. COMLEX: Distinguishing the receptor types for GH vs IGF-1 is high-yield for understanding why GH therapy works in GH deficiency but not in Laron syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — Tyrosine kinase receptors including the IGF-1 receptor use dimerization and autophosphorylation, contrasting with the JAK2-associated GH receptor described in this objective","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl6_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how growth hormone stimulates bone and cartilage growth.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-month-old boy is evaluated for slow growth. He was born at term with normal length and weight, but over recent months his length has fallen well below his growth curve, and he has had several episodes of fasting hypoglycemia. Provocative testing shows a subnormal growth hormone response, and genetic testing confirms isolated congenital GH deficiency. Which best explains why his size at birth was normal?","choices":{"A":"Prenatal growth does not depend on GH; IGF-1 drives fetal growth, and IGF-1 production and growth become GH-dependent during the first year of life","B":"Maternal GH crosses the placenta in large amounts and substitutes for fetal GH until it is cleared from the infant's blood in the first months of life","C":"Fetal thyroid hormone controls prenatal linear growth by itself, so a lack of GH or IGF-1 cannot slow growth in any way before birth occurs","D":"GH secreted before birth is stored in the fetal liver bound to IGFBP-3, and these hepatic stores are used up over the first months after delivery","E":"Fetal growth plates lack GH receptors until puberty, so GH plays no part in linear growth until adolescence, when the deficit first becomes visible"},"explanation":"Katzung (Ch 37) states that neonates with isolated GH deficiency are typically of normal size at birth because prenatal growth is not GH-dependent. In contrast, IGF-1 is essential for normal prenatal and postnatal growth, and through poorly understood mechanisms IGF-1 expression and postnatal growth become GH-dependent during the first year of life. GH deficiency in childhood then presents as short stature, and hypoglycemia is an early sign because GH is part of the counter-regulatory response to low glucose. Maternal transfer of GH is not the explanation the source gives. The thyroid explanation is wrong because IGF-1 itself is required for fetal growth, so IGF-1 deficiency does slow prenatal growth. IGFBP-3 carries IGF-1, not GH, and there is no fetal GH store. Growth plates respond to GH and IGF-1 throughout childhood, as this infant's postnatal growth failure shows.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Isolated GH deficiency: normal birth size, then growth failure, often with mild adiposity and hypoglycemia; Laron syndrome (GH receptor defect) has high GH with low IGF-1 and is treated with recombinant IGF-1, mecasermin (Katzung Ch 37). COMLEX: Diagnosis requires a subnormal height velocity plus a subnormal GH response to at least two provocative stimuli (Katzung Ch 37).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — growth hormone deficiency in childhood causes proportionate short stature","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Maternal transfer of GH is not","D":"IGFBP-3 carries IGF-1, not GH,","E":"Growth plates respond to GH an"}},{"id":"sdl6_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how growth hormone stimulates bone and cartilage growth.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher studies GH's direct versus IGF-1-mediated effects on bone. After hypophysectomy in rats, systemic GH injections restore serum IGF-1 and normalize growth. However, direct injection of a very small dose of GH into one epiphyseal cartilage causes local bone growth at that site within hours, before systemic IGF-1 rises measurably. What does this finding demonstrate about GH's mechanism of action on bone?","choices":{"D":"GH can stimulate local IGF-1 synthesis within chondrocytes for autocrine and paracrine growth promotion, showing that GH has direct, IGF-1-mediated effects at the tissue level independent of hepatic IGF-1 production","A":"Local GH injection delivers sufficient GH to produce measurable systemic IGF-1 before the IGF-1 assay detects it centrally, because peripheral vascular delivery of hepatic IGF-1 reaches bone before central blood sampling","B":"Direct GH injection at the growth plate bypasses hepatic first-pass metabolism and delivers unaltered pharmacologically active GH to epiphyseal chondrocytes at concentrations far exceeding what systemic dosing achieves","C":"The local bone growth reflects a direct cAMP-mediated growth effect of GH acting through a Gs-coupled GH receptor isoform present only in epiphyseal chondrocytes but not in hepatocytes or muscle cells","E":"GH injected locally into the bone displaces existing bone-bound IGF-1 from IGFBP-3 carrier proteins that had accumulated at the growth plate from prior systemic GH pulses, transiently releasing bioavailable IGF-1"},"explanation":"While most GH growth effects are mediated through hepatic IGF-1, GH also has direct local effects. Local injection of minute GH doses into epiphyseal cartilage causes growth before systemic IGF-1 rises, explained by local IGF-1 production within the chondrocytes themselves. GH activates JAK2-STAT5 in chondrocytes, stimulating local IGF-1 synthesis for autocrine and paracrine effects. GH also has some IGF-1-independent direct effects on chondrocyte differentiation. This dual mechanism is why both GH and IGF-1 are required for normal bone growth.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH has direct effects on bone via local IGF-1 production in chondrocytes, not only via hepatic IGF-1. Both mechanisms are needed for normal bone growth. COMLEX: Understanding that GH has local tissue-level IGF-1-independent effects helps explain why IGF-1 replacement alone does not fully substitute for GH in all tissues.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — GHRH-driven GH secretion stimulates both hepatic IGF-1 and direct bone effects, explaining why both circulating IGF-1 and local GH action are needed for full growth","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl6_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how growth hormone stimulates bone and cartilage growth.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher performs GH injections in a pancreatectomized dog that lacks all insulin. Despite adequate calories from fat and protein, the dog shows markedly reduced growth velocity and near-undetectable serum IGF-1 despite normal serum GH and normal GH receptor expression. Carbohydrate supplementation also fails to restore growth. Which mechanism most directly explains why GH's growth-promoting actions fail without insulin?","choices":{"E":"Insulin is required for hepatic amino acid transport and protein synthesis, and without insulin, the liver cannot produce enough IGF-1 in response to GH receptor activation even when GH binding and JAK2 activation are intact","A":"Insulin is required to phosphorylate STAT5 through IRS-1 and PI3K before JAK2 can access STAT5, and without this priming phosphorylation event, GH receptor dimerization cannot trigger downstream STAT5 activation in hepatocytes","B":"Insulin deficiency causes rapid GH receptor ubiquitination and lysosomal degradation in hepatocytes, reducing GH receptor surface density below the minimum threshold needed to activate sufficient JAK2 for IGF-1 transcription","C":"Insulin is required for IGFBP-3 synthesis in the liver, and without IGFBP-3 to form a protective ternary complex, any IGF-1 that is produced is immediately cleared by the kidney before reaching epiphyseal growth plates","D":"Insulin normally phosphorylates the GH receptor on serine residues to prevent endocytosis, and without this protective phosphorylation, the GH receptor undergoes constitutive internalization and cannot accumulate at the cell surface"},"explanation":"GH requires both insulin and carbohydrates for its growth-promoting action. GH fails to cause growth in animals lacking a pancreas or fed a carbohydrate-free diet. The requirement for insulin is partly because insulin enhances amino acid transport into hepatocytes — the same substrate needed for IGF-1 protein synthesis. Without adequate amino acid delivery to hepatocytes, JAK2 and STAT5 may be activated normally, but IGF-1 mRNA cannot be translated into protein. Insulin also works synergistically with GH at the ribosomal level to support protein synthesis throughout the body.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH requires insulin and carbohydrates for full growth-promoting effects — fails without pancreas or carbohydrate diet. Clinical implication: poorly controlled T1DM patients can have growth failure despite elevated GH because insulin deficiency prevents IGF-1 synthesis. COMLEX: GH-insulin interaction is a high-yield integration of two major hormone systems.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — Hypothalamic releasing hormone control of anterior pituitary establishes how GH secretion is regulated, setting the stage for understanding what limits GH's growth effects at the target tissue level","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl6_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the details of growth hormone receptor signaling.","batch":2,"stem":"A research group maps growth hormone receptor expression across adult human tissues to predict which organs respond most strongly to circulating GH. The receptor is found in almost every tissue examined. Which set of tissues shows the highest level of GH receptor expression?","choices":{"C":"Liver, muscle, adipose tissue, and kidney","A":"Brain, retina, nerve, and spinal cord","B":"Thyroid, adrenal cortex, gonads, and pituitary","D":"Lung, spleen, lymph nodes, and bone marrow","E":"Skin, salivary glands, stomach, and pancreas"},"explanation":"Guyton (Ch 76) describes the GH receptor as a cytokine receptor expressed in almost all tissues, with the liver, muscle, fat, and kidneys showing the highest levels of expression. It has an extracellular GH-binding domain, a single membrane-spanning domain, and an intracellular domain associated with JAK2. The tissue pattern matches GH's main actions: hepatic IGF-1 production, protein deposition in muscle, and fat mobilization from adipose tissue. Neural tissues, the endocrine target glands of other pituitary hormones, lymphoid and hematopoietic organs, and skin and gut glands all express the receptor but are not the sites of highest expression in this source.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The GH receptor has no intrinsic kinase; GH binding activates the associated JAK2, which phosphorylates STAT proteins that enter the nucleus (Guyton Ch 76). Highest receptor expression: liver, muscle, fat, kidney. COMLEX: Much of the circulating IGF-1 is produced by the liver, while GH also stimulates local IGF-1 in bone, cartilage, muscle, and kidney (Katzung Ch 37).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — the liver is the main source of circulating IGF-1","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Neural tissues, the endocrine ","B":"Neural tissues, the endocrine ","D":"Neural tissues, the endocrine "}},{"id":"sdl6_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the details of growth hormone receptor signaling.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has been in the intensive care unit for 12 days with septic shock and multiorgan failure. Despite enteral feeding he has marked muscle wasting. Serum GH is elevated, while IGF-1 is low. GH receptor gene sequencing is normal and no anti-GH antibodies are detected. Which mechanism best explains his resistance to GH?","choices":{"A":"Inflammatory cytokines induce SOCS proteins, which disrupt JAK-STAT signaling downstream of the GH receptor and blunt IGF-1 production","B":"Circulating antibodies against the GH receptor block GH binding at the hepatocyte surface, so IGF-1 falls while GH rises from lost feedback","C":"Critical illness switches somatotropes to a GH isoform that binds but cannot activate the receptor, so IGF-1 falls despite high GH","D":"High circulating somatostatin during sepsis blocks hepatic GH receptors directly, so IGF-1 falls while pituitary GH output continues to rise","E":"Hepatic IGFBP-3 synthesis rises sharply in sepsis and binds GH itself in plasma, keeping GH away from its receptors despite high levels"},"explanation":"Guyton (Ch 76) explains that intracellular GH signaling is attenuated by suppressor of cytokine signaling (SOCS) proteins, which disrupt the JAK-STAT pathway and induce resistance to GH. In patients with kidney failure or critical illness, inflammatory cytokines induce high levels of SOCS proteins, which may impair GH signaling; the result is high GH with low IGF-1, because IGF-1 no longer feeds back on GH secretion. Receptor-blocking antibodies are not the mechanism this source gives for critical illness. An inactive GH isoform from somatotropes is not described. Somatostatin inhibits GH release from the pituitary rather than blocking hepatic receptors, and high somatostatin would lower GH, not raise it. IGFBP-3 binds IGF-1 in the blood, not GH (Katzung Ch 37).","boardPrep":"Step 1: SOCS proteins are inducible brakes on JAK-STAT; cytokine-induced SOCS causes acquired GH resistance in kidney failure and critical illness (Guyton Ch 76). Pattern: high GH, low IGF-1, like Laron syndrome but acquired. COMLEX: The same SOCS brake contributes to leptin resistance in obesity (SDL 3 Obj 3).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — JAK-STAT signaling through receptor-associated JAK2, modeled with the leptin receptor","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Receptor-blocking antibodies a","C":"An inactive GH isoform from so","E":"IGFBP-3 binds IGF-1 in the blo"}},{"id":"sdl6_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the details of growth hormone receptor signaling.","batch":2,"stem":"In a hepatocyte, GH binding activates JAK2 associated with the GH receptor, and JAK2 phosphorylates STAT proteins that move to the nucleus. Activated GH receptor and JAK2 also recruit other intracellular signaling molecules that let GH engage further pathways in the cell. Which molecules are recruited in this way?","choices":{"B":"Insulin receptor substrate proteins and Shc adapter proteins, which link GH receptor activation to additional kinase pathways","A":"Beta-arrestins and G protein receptor kinases, which link GH receptor activation to receptor desensitization and cAMP signaling","C":"Gs alpha subunits and adenylyl cyclase, which link GH receptor activation to cAMP production and protein kinase A activity","D":"SMAD2 and SMAD3 transcription factors, which link GH receptor activation to serine-threonine kinase signaling in the nucleus","E":"Phospholipase C beta and Gq alpha subunits, which link GH receptor activation to IP3 release and protein kinase C activity"},"explanation":"Guyton (Ch 76) states that activation of the GH receptor and JAK2 recruits additional intracellular signaling pathways, including insulin receptor substrate (IRS) proteins and Shc adapter proteins, which in turn activate other pathways that work together to produce the complex responses to GH in target cells. JAK2 itself is the primary signal transducer, phosphorylating STAT proteins that drive GH-specific gene expression. The GH receptor is a cytokine receptor, not a G protein-coupled receptor, so beta-arrestin and G protein receptor kinase desensitization, Gs-adenylyl cyclase, and Gq-phospholipase C pathways are not its recruited partners. SMAD proteins carry signals from serine-threonine kinase receptors of the TGF-beta family, not from the GH receptor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH receptor → JAK2 → STAT (main route to IGF-1 gene) plus recruitment of IRS and Shc adapters; SOCS proteins switch the pathway off (Guyton Ch 76). COMLEX: IRS proteins are also the substrates phosphorylated by the activated insulin receptor (SDL 27), so GH and insulin signaling share downstream adapters.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — IRS proteins are phosphorylated by the activated insulin receptor","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"}],"7":[{"id":"sdl7_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major stimulators and inhibitors of growth hormone secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following physiological states is a well-established stimulator of growth hormone (GH) secretion from anterior pituitary somatotropes?","choices":{"E":"Hypoglycemia produced by insulin-induced lowering of blood glucose, signaling nutritional deficit to the hypothalamus","A":"Postprandial hyperglycemia occurring after ingestion of a high-carbohydrate meal and representing adequate glucose substrate availability","B":"Chronically elevated plasma free fatty acid concentrations reflecting abundant lipid substrate in an overfed or obese state","C":"Increased circulating IGF-1 concentrations produced by GH-stimulated hepatic synthesis during periods of GH excess","D":"Exogenous recombinant GH administration via subcutaneous injection used for therapeutic replacement or performance enhancement"},"explanation":"Hypoglycemia is a potent physiological stimulator of GH secretion; decreased blood glucose signals nutritional deficit and activates hypothalamic GHRH release while suppressing somatostatin tone, collectively increasing GH output from somatotropes. Hyperglycemia inhibits GH secretion by signaling abundant glucose substrate. Elevated free fatty acids also inhibit GH, reflecting adequate fat substrate. Elevated IGF-1 and exogenous GH both exert negative feedback on GH secretion, suppressing GHRH or enhancing somatostatin at the hypothalamus and pituitary.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypoglycemia is the classic provocative test to stimulate GH release in workup of GH deficiency. Know the pattern: low substrates stimulate GH; high substrates inhibit. COMLEX: GH and insulin are metabolic antagonists; hypoglycemia is the key trigger linking nutritional status to GH secretion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — GH metabolic effects explain why nutritional status and energy substrate availability drive GH stimulation and inhibition","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Hyperglycemia inhibits GH secr","C":"Elevated IGF-1 and exogenous G","D":"Elevated IGF-1 and exogenous G"}},{"id":"sdl7_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major stimulators and inhibitors of growth hormone secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following is correctly classified as an inhibitor of growth hormone (GH) secretion?","choices":{"B":"Chronically elevated plasma free fatty acid concentrations signaling abundant lipid substrate availability to the hypothalamus","A":"Moderate-to-high intensity aerobic exercise increasing circulating catecholamines and creating a relative energy deficit at muscle","C":"Deep slow-wave sleep stages 3 and 4 associated with high-amplitude slow-wave electroencephalographic activity and circadian GH pulsatility","D":"Protein deficiency states in which markedly reduced plasma amino acid concentrations signal anabolic need to the hypothalamus","E":"Physical trauma and psychological stress activating hypothalamic stress-response systems and sympathetic nervous system output"},"explanation":"Elevated plasma free fatty acids inhibit GH secretion by signaling to the hypothalamus that fat substrate is abundantly available, reducing the perceived need for GH-driven lipolysis. Exercise, slow-wave sleep, protein deficiency, and stress are all established stimulators of GH secretion: exercise through catecholamine and metabolic signals, sleep through circadian GH pulsatility, protein deficiency through amino acid sensing of anabolic need, and stress through CRH-related and adrenergic pathways.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Remember both categories: stimulators include hypoglycemia, exercise, sleep, amino acids, ghrelin, stress, sex hormones; inhibitors include hyperglycemia, elevated FFA, obesity, aging, somatostatin, IGF-1, exogenous GH. COMLEX: Obesity blunts GH pulsatility due to chronically elevated FFA and leptin resistance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — GH metabolic effects explain why nutritional status and energy substrate availability drive GH stimulation and inhibition","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Exercise, slow-wave sleep, pro","E":"Exercise, slow-wave sleep, pro"}},{"id":"sdl7_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major stimulators and inhibitors of growth hormone secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly pairs a physiological stimulus with its mechanism for promoting growth hormone (GH) secretion?","choices":{"A":"Arginine infusion: rising plasma amino acids lower hypothalamic somatostatin release, lifting its brake on somatotropes so GH secretion rises","B":"Hyperglycemia: elevated blood glucose directly activates arcuate nucleus GHRH neurons to increase GH secretion in proportion to caloric intake","C":"Elevated plasma IGF-1: acts as a short-loop positive feedback signal on anterior pituitary somatotropes to amplify ongoing GH secretion","D":"Obesity: increased adipose tissue mass signals energy surplus through leptin receptor activation of arcuate GHRH neurons to elevate GH","E":"Exogenous somatostatin infusion: inhibits IGF-1 synthesis in hepatocytes and removes negative feedback from the axis to allow GH to rise"},"explanation":"Guyton Ch 76 (course reading, SDL 4–7) lists increased blood amino acids (arginine) among the stimuli of GH secretion, and Katzung Ch 37 gives the mechanism: arginine and insulin-induced hypoglycemia \"cause diminished SST, which increases GH release.\" Hyperglycemia inhibits GH rather than stimulating it. Somatomedins (IGF-1) inhibit GH secretion (Costanzo Ch 9), so they do not amplify it. Obesity and increased free fatty acids inhibit GH secretion (Guyton Table 76-3; Costanzo Table 9.4). Somatostatin inhibits GH secretion by blocking GHRH action on the somatotroph (Costanzo Ch 9), so an infusion lowers GH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Arginine and insulin-induced hypoglycemia both raise GH by lowering somatostatin, so both serve as provocative tests; GH deficiency is diagnosed by a subnormal GH response to at least two secretagogues (Katzung Ch 37). COMLEX: Amino acid availability and protein nutritional status are key regulators of anabolic hormone secretion, connecting nutrition to pituitary function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — GH metabolic effects explain why nutritional status and energy substrate availability drive GH stimulation and inhibition","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Hyperglycemia inhibits GH rath","C":"Somatomedins (IGF-1) inhibit G","D":"Obesity and increased free fat","E":"Somatostatin inhibits GH secre"}},{"id":"sdl7_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the roles of GHRH and somatostatin in regulating growth hormone secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Growth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH) is secreted from the hypothalamus and acts on anterior pituitary somatotropes. What is the immediate intracellular signaling event triggered by GHRH binding its receptor on somatotropes?","choices":{"E":"Inhibition of adenylyl cyclase through Gi protein coupling, decreasing intracellular cAMP and paradoxically promoting GH gene transcription","A":"Activation of phospholipase C through Gq protein coupling, generating IP3 and DAG to trigger ER calcium release and PKC activation","B":"Activation of adenylyl cyclase through Gs protein coupling, increasing intracellular cAMP concentrations in the somatotrope","C":"Direct activation of JAK2 kinase through nonreceptor tyrosine kinase coupling, phosphorylating STAT5 to stimulate GH gene synthesis in somatotropes","D":"Nuclear receptor binding after ligand internalization, with direct translocation to the nucleus to activate the GH gene promoter region"},"explanation":"GHRH binds a Gs-coupled GPCR on somatotropes, activating adenylyl cyclase and increasing intracellular cAMP. Elevated cAMP has two downstream effects: in the short term it promotes calcium influx through voltage-gated channels, driving GH vesicle exocytosis; in the long term it activates PKA, which stimulates DNA transcription of the GH gene to build secretory capacity. Somatostatin acts through Gi to inhibit adenylyl cyclase, the mechanistic opposite. JAK2-STAT5 is the signaling mechanism at GH receptor on target tissues, not GHRH receptor on somatotropes. GHRH is a peptide hormone that acts on membrane receptors, not nuclear receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GHRH receptor is Gs-coupled; somatostatin receptor is Gi-coupled. This contrast is a classic board test point. COMLEX: Octreotide is a somatostatin analog that mimics Gi signaling to suppress GH in acromegaly treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — GH receptor JAK2-STAT5 signaling at target tissues contrasts with GHRH-cAMP signaling at somatotropes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"D":"GHRH is a peptide hormone that"}},{"id":"sdl7_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the roles of GHRH and somatostatin in regulating growth hormone secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Somatostatin is a hypothalamic hormone that inhibits growth hormone (GH) secretion. What is the intracellular mechanism by which somatostatin suppresses GH release from anterior pituitary somatotropes?","choices":{"B":"Somatostatin binds a Gi-coupled receptor on somatotropes, inhibiting adenylyl cyclase and lowering cAMP to suppress GH exocytosis and GH gene transcription","A":"Somatostatin binds a Gs-coupled receptor on somatotropes, activating adenylyl cyclase and increasing intracellular cAMP in a GH-suppressing downstream cascade","C":"Somatostatin binds a tyrosine kinase receptor on somatotropes, activating downstream MAPK signaling that phosphorylates GH promoter repressor elements","D":"Somatostatin binds a Gq-coupled receptor on somatotropes, activating phospholipase C and generating IP3 to suppress GH-containing vesicle exocytosis","E":"Somatostatin directly enters somatotropes as a lipophilic molecule and acts as a nuclear receptor ligand to block GHRH-stimulated GH gene expression"},"explanation":"Somatostatin binds a Gi-coupled GPCR on somatotropes, inhibiting adenylyl cyclase and decreasing intracellular cAMP. Because cAMP drives both short-term GH vesicle exocytosis via calcium influx and long-term GH gene transcription via PKA, reducing cAMP suppresses both routes of GH output. This is the mechanistic opposite of GHRH, which acts through Gs to increase cAMP. Somatostatin receptors are not Gs-coupled, not tyrosine kinase receptors, not Gq-coupled, and not nuclear receptors — somatostatin is a peptide that acts on membrane surface receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Somatostatin inhibits both GH and TSH secretion from the anterior pituitary. Somatostatin analogs such as octreotide are first-line medical therapy for acromegaly after failed surgery. COMLEX: Somatostatin is also released from pancreatic delta cells to inhibit insulin, glucagon, and GI secretion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — GH receptor JAK2-STAT5 signaling at target tissues contrasts with GHRH-cAMP signaling at somatotropes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Somatostatin receptors are not","D":"Somatostatin receptors are not","E":"Somatostatin receptors are not"}},{"id":"sdl7_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the roles of GHRH and somatostatin in regulating growth hormone secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes how both IGF-1 and exogenous growth hormone (GH) suppress endogenous GH secretion through negative feedback?","choices":{"A":"Both IGF-1 and exogenous GH act at the hypothalamus and pituitary to suppress GHRH output, enhance somatostatin tone, or both, reducing endogenous GH secretion","B":"Both IGF-1 and exogenous GH bind directly to somatotrope GH receptors and activate Gi protein signaling, mimicking somatostatin inhibition at the adenylyl cyclase level","C":"IGF-1 acts exclusively at the anterior pituitary to downregulate GHRH receptors on somatotropes, while exogenous GH acts exclusively at the hypothalamus to stimulate somatostatin neurons","D":"Both IGF-1 and exogenous GH suppress GH secretion by directly blocking voltage-gated calcium channel opening in somatotropes to prevent GH vesicle exocytosis","E":"IGF-1 provides positive feedback on the GH axis to maintain homeostasis, while exogenous GH suppresses GH secretion by pituitary-level receptor downregulation alone"},"explanation":"Both IGF-1 and exogenous GH exert negative feedback on the GH axis at the level of the hypothalamus and pituitary, reducing GHRH secretion and enhancing somatostatin tone; the precise relative contributions at each site are not fully characterized. A related principle — hyperglycemia as a GH inhibitor — is the basis for GH suppression testing in acromegaly: administering oral glucose raises blood glucose, reduces GHRH drive, and GH falls below 1 ng/mL in normal subjects. Failure to suppress confirms autonomous GH secretion from a somatotroph adenoma. IGF-1 does not stimulate positive feedback on GH; it is purely inhibitory in this context.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Failure of GH to suppress below 1 ng/mL after oral glucose tolerance test is the diagnostic criterion for acromegaly. Elevated IGF-1 is the best single screening test. COMLEX: Understand the negative feedback axis for any board question about hormone suppression testing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — GH receptor JAK2-STAT5 signaling at target tissues contrasts with GHRH-cAMP signaling at somatotropes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"E":"IGF-1 does not stimulate posit"}},{"id":"sdl7_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the clinical features of growth hormone deficiency (dwarfism), gigantism, and acromegaly.","batch":1,"stem":"A 15-year-old male is evaluated for exceptional height of 7 feet 1 inch with continued rapid growth. His father is 5 feet 10 inches. Serum GH and IGF-1 are markedly elevated; GH fails to suppress on oral glucose tolerance testing. MRI reveals a pituitary macroadenoma. Fasting glucose is 152 mg/dL. Which of the following best characterizes this condition?","choices":{"D":"Gigantism from GH excess before epiphyseal fusion, allowing continued proportionate longitudinal growth via IGF-1-stimulated chondrocyte proliferation","A":"Acromegaly resulting from GH excess after epiphyseal plate fusion, causing selective enlargement of membranous bones in the jaw, skull, and acral skeleton without height increase","B":"Constitutional tall stature from familial height variant with normal GH and IGF-1, in which bone age is appropriate and growth velocity is within normal limits","C":"Marfan syndrome causing tall stature through FBN1 fibrillin-1 gene mutation, associated with lens dislocation and aortic root dilatation rather than GH elevation","E":"Precocious puberty with sex hormone-driven early growth acceleration followed by premature epiphyseal plate closure and normal final adult height"},"explanation":"This patient has gigantism, which results when a GH-secreting pituitary adenoma develops before epiphyseal plate fusion. With open growth plates, elevated IGF-1 drives ongoing chondrocyte proliferation at the epiphysis, enabling continued proportionate longitudinal bone growth throughout the skeleton; heights up to 8 feet have been reported. The elevated fasting glucose reflects GH's diabetogenic effect — approximately 10% of untreated gigantism cases develop frank diabetes mellitus. Acromegaly produces skeletal changes only in bones that retain lifelong membranous growth capacity because the growth plates are already fused. Constitutional tall stature, Marfan syndrome, and precocious puberty have distinct features and do not involve elevated GH or IGF-1.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gigantism occurs before epiphyseal fusion; acromegaly occurs after. Both are caused by GH-secreting somatotroph adenoma. Treatment is transsphenoidal surgery. COMLEX: Acromegaly and gigantism are explicitly named in the COMLEX CP3 blueprint as physical exam findings requiring recognition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — GH metabolic effects including diabetogenic action and protein anabolism underlie the clinical features of GH excess and deficiency","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Acromegaly produces skeletal c","E":"Constitutional tall stature, M"}},{"id":"sdl7_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the clinical features of growth hormone deficiency (dwarfism), gigantism, and acromegaly.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following physical examination findings is most characteristic of acromegaly due to a GH-secreting pituitary adenoma in an adult who has already reached full adult height?","choices":{"E":"Progressive enlargement of the jaw, skull, nose, and supraorbital ridges with coarsening of facial features due to ongoing membranous bone growth","A":"Proportionate increase in height by 3 to 4 inches over the past year due to renewed longitudinal bone growth at reactivated epiphyseal growth plates","B":"Short stature with delayed bone age and absent secondary sexual characteristics resulting from panhypopituitarism from mass effect of the adenoma","C":"Bitemporal hemianopia with normal serum GH and IGF-1 resulting from optic chiasm compression by a nonfunctioning pituitary macroadenoma","D":"Central obesity with wide purple striae, proximal muscle weakness, and hypertension resulting from cortisol excess in Cushing syndrome"},"explanation":"In acromegaly, GH excess develops after epiphyseal plate fusion, so longitudinal bone growth is no longer possible. Instead, GH stimulates membranous bones — those that retain growth capacity throughout life — producing progressive enlargement of the jaw with prognathism, frontal bossing, enlarged supraorbital ridges, broadened nose, and coarse facial features. Acral growth causes shoe size to increase and ring size to enlarge as hands and feet widen. Height does not increase because growth plates are fused. Short stature and hypogonadism can arise from mass effect in untreated large tumors, but coarsened facial features from membranous bone overgrowth are the defining skeletal finding of GH excess after fusion. Purple striae and central obesity characterize Cushing syndrome, not acromegaly. Bitemporal hemianopia with normal GH and IGF-1 is the mass effect of a nonfunctioning adenoma, which makes no GH and so cannot cause bony overgrowth.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acromegaly classic features include enlarged jaw, hands, and feet; coarsened facial features; kyphosis; organomegaly; and carpal tunnel syndrome. COMLEX: Both acromegaly and gigantism are named in COMLEX CP3 as physical exam findings to recognize clinically.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — GH metabolic effects including diabetogenic action and protein anabolism underlie the clinical features of GH excess and deficiency","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Height does not increase becau"}},{"id":"sdl7_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the clinical features of growth hormone deficiency (dwarfism), gigantism, and acromegaly.","batch":1,"stem":"A 9-year-old child presents with proportionate short stature at the 1st percentile for age and sex. Growth velocity has been consistently low over the past 3 years. Bone age is delayed by 2 years. Laboratory evaluation reveals very low serum GH with poor response to both arginine infusion and insulin-induced hypoglycemia stimulation testing. IGF-1 is markedly reduced. Which of the following correctly identifies the diagnosis and expected clinical features?","choices":{"C":"Isolated GHRH deficiency, in which GHRH is absent from the hypothalamus but GH reserves remain normal as demonstrated by preserved somatotrope response to direct GH stimulation","A":"Laron syndrome from GH receptor loss-of-function mutation, in which serum GH is markedly elevated and exogenous GH administration fails to increase IGF-1 concentrations","B":"African pygmy growth pattern, in which GH levels and GH receptor function are normal but IGF-1 production is constitutively reduced through a separate hepatic regulatory mechanism","D":"Constitutional growth delay with normal GH axis function and stimulated GH response, in which final adult height is predicted to be within the normal range without GH treatment","E":"Pituitary GH deficiency, causing proportionate dwarfism and delayed bone age; about two-thirds of such children have panhypopituitarism and fail to enter puberty"},"explanation":"Very low GH with failure to respond to two independent provocative stimuli confirms true pituitary GH deficiency; in about two-thirds of such children it is part of panhypopituitarism, while one-third have isolated GH deficiency and mature sexually. Clinical features include proportionate short stature, delayed bone age, and in two-thirds of affected patients, absent or incomplete puberty due to coexistent gonadotropin deficiency. Treatment is recombinant human GH produced from Escherichia coli. Laron syndrome is characterized by elevated GH with low IGF-1 due to GH receptor mutation; exogenous GH has no effect. African pygmies have normal GH and GH receptor. Constitutional growth delay produces normal stimulated GH levels. Isolated GHRH deficiency would produce low GH by indirect mechanism, but the distinction requires GHRH stimulation testing rather than GH stimulation testing alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH deficiency in children requires two failed GH stimulation tests for diagnosis. Low IGF-1 is the screening test. Treatment is recombinant human GH. Laron syndrome has elevated GH, low IGF-1, and no response to exogenous GH. COMLEX: Distinguish pituitary dwarfism from GH resistance syndromes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — GH metabolic effects including diabetogenic action and protein anabolism underlie the clinical features of GH excess and deficiency","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"B":"African pygmies have normal GH","D":"Constitutional growth delay pr"}},{"id":"sdl7_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major stimulators and inhibitors of growth hormone secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with a BMI of 39 kg/m2 and type 2 diabetes mellitus presents for an annual visit. His fasting glucose is 218 mg/dL and his HbA1c is 8.7%. His physician reviews his GH axis and notes that his serum GH levels are chronically suppressed compared to age-matched lean controls, with blunted pulsatility on 24-hour sampling. Which combination of factors most directly explains the suppressed GH secretion in this patient?","choices":{"B":"Chronically elevated plasma free fatty acids and persistent hyperglycemia, each of which inhibits GH secretion by reducing GHRH drive and raising somatostatin tone","A":"Reduced circulating amino acid concentrations from inadequate dietary protein intake suppress GHRH release and decrease GH secretion from somatotropes in obese patients","C":"Elevated plasma IGF-1 from GH-independent hepatic stimulation exerts excessive negative feedback on the hypothalamic-pituitary GH axis in obese adults","D":"Severely disrupted sleep architecture from obesity hypoventilation syndrome abolishes the nocturnal GH pulse while concurrent hyperglycemia independently suppresses daytime GH secretion","E":"Excess cortisol from obesity-associated HPA axis dysregulation directly activates periventricular somatostatin neurons and independently abolishes somatotrope responsiveness to GHRH"},"explanation":"In obesity with type 2 diabetes, two major inhibitory signals converge to chronically suppress GH secretion: elevated plasma free fatty acids from expanded adipose stores, and persistent hyperglycemia from insulin resistance. Both signal to the hypothalamus that metabolic substrate is abundant, reducing GHRH output and enhancing somatostatin tone. The net effect is suppressed GH pulsatility. While sleep-disordered breathing may impair nocturnal GH pulsatility as a secondary contributor, the primary drivers in metabolic syndrome are FFA elevation and hyperglycemia. Plasma IGF-1 is not GH-independent; it is reduced in obesity despite adequate caloric intake. Dietary amino acid intake is not typically limiting in obese adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Obesity and hyperglycemia are classic GH inhibitors. GH declines with aging, and elevated FFA from obesity accelerates this decline further. COMLEX: Blunted GH pulsatility in obesity contributes to the anabolic deficiency seen in metabolic syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — GH diabetogenic effects explain why hyperglycemia and elevated FFA from GH excess feedback to suppress further GH secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"Plasma IGF-1 is not GH-indepen","D":"While sleep-disordered breathi"}},{"id":"sdl7_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major stimulators and inhibitors of growth hormone secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A clinical pharmacologist designs a study to assess peak GH secretory reserve in healthy adult volunteers. She uses three provocative agents: insulin (to induce hypoglycemia), arginine infusion, and ghrelin administration. All three reliably produce GH peaks in her volunteers. A fourth colleague proposes adding glucose-stimulated glucagon as a test agent, arguing that glucagon signals nutritional deficit. The pharmacologist reviews the literature on ghrelin's mechanism. Which of the following correctly describes ghrelin's mechanism of action in stimulating GH secretion?","choices":{"C":"Ghrelin is a stomach-derived peptide that acts as a potent GH secretagogue by binding GHS receptors on hypothalamic GHRH neurons and directly on anterior pituitary somatotropes to stimulate GH release","A":"Ghrelin is a hypothalamic peptide released from arcuate nucleus neurons that inhibits GH secretion by mimicking somatostatin Gi-coupled receptor signaling on somatotropes","B":"Ghrelin is a liver-derived peptide secreted after fasting that directly suppresses somatostatin release from periventricular neurons, allowing GH to rise passively without directly stimulating somatotropes","D":"Ghrelin is an adipokine released from adipocytes in caloric excess states that stimulates GH secretion through leptin receptor signaling on hypothalamic GHRH neurons","E":"Ghrelin is a pancreatic peptide that mimics glucagon receptor signaling on somatotropes and activates Gs-coupled adenylyl cyclase through a mechanism identical to that of GHRH"},"explanation":"Ghrelin is produced primarily by oxyntic cells in the gastric fundus, especially during fasting and before meals. It is a powerful GH secretagogue that acts through GHS-R1a receptors on both hypothalamic GHRH neurons, amplifying GHRH output, and directly on anterior pituitary somatotropes, stimulating GH release independent of GHRH. Rising ghrelin levels during fasting connect nutritional state to GH axis activation. Ghrelin is not hypothalamic in origin, is not from the liver, is not an adipokine, and is not a pancreatic product. It does not inhibit GH secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ghrelin from the stomach stimulates GH secretion and appetite. High-yield contrast: ghrelin rises with fasting and stimulates appetite and GH; leptin rises with feeding and inhibits appetite. COMLEX: Ghrelin connects gastrointestinal endocrine function with pituitary regulation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — HPA axis negative feedback logic parallels GH axis feedback through IGF-1 and somatostatin on GHRH neurons","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Ghrelin is not hypothalamic in","B":"Ghrelin is not hypothalamic in","D":"Ghrelin is not hypothalamic in","E":"Ghrelin is not hypothalamic in"}},{"id":"sdl7_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major stimulators and inhibitors of growth hormone secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old male collegiate swimmer undergoes a polysomnographic sleep study as part of a research protocol. Blood is drawn every 30 minutes overnight for GH measurement. The highest GH pulse is recorded during stage N3 deep slow-wave sleep at 01:30. A second smaller but significant GH pulse is recorded at 23:00, before sleep onset. The researcher correlates this second pulse with events recorded on a diary form the subject completed. Which of the following events most likely explains the pre-sleep GH pulse in this subject?","choices":{"A":"Intense evening swim practice completed shortly before the 23:00 blood draw, since exercise independently stimulates GH secretion through catecholamine-mediated and metabolic signals","B":"A pre-study high-fat snack containing elevated free fatty acids that directly activated arcuate nucleus GHRH neurons and triggered a GH pulse in the early evening hours","C":"Elevated plasma testosterone from athletic training that inhibited somatostatin neurons through androgen receptor signaling and allowed a GH pulse to emerge before sleep onset","D":"Elevated circulating IGF-1 from chronic training-related hepatic stimulation that provided positive feedback on hypothalamic GHRH neurons to sustain GH secretion throughout the evening","E":"A carbohydrate-loading meal consumed 2 hours before the study that stimulated pancreatic glucagon secretion and indirectly activated GHRH release via portal vein signals"},"explanation":"Exercise is a well-established physiological stimulator of GH secretion independent of sleep. Intense aerobic exercise activates the sympathetic nervous system, generates metabolic signals reflecting energy deficit, and produces amino acid availability — all of which stimulate GHRH release and suppress somatostatin tone to produce a GH pulse during and shortly after the exercise bout. The pre-sleep GH pulse in this swimmer is consistent with exercise-induced GH secretion. A high-fat snack would inhibit GH by elevating plasma free fatty acids. Testosterone stimulates GH rather than inhibiting it through androgen receptor effects. Elevated IGF-1 provides negative feedback, not positive. A carbohydrate meal raises blood glucose, which inhibits GH secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The two most important physiological GH stimulators are deep slow-wave sleep and exercise. The largest daily GH pulse occurs during stage N3 sleep. COMLEX: Athletes have higher GH pulsatility; exogenous GH doping suppresses endogenous GH through negative feedback.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — GH protein anabolic and lipolytic effects explain why exercise-induced energy deficit and amino acid availability stimulate GH secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"E":"A carbohydrate meal raises blo"}},{"id":"sdl7_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the roles of GHRH and somatostatin in regulating growth hormone secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher studying somatotrope signal transduction applies GHRH to isolated anterior pituitary cells in culture. She uses a pharmacological adenylyl cyclase inhibitor to block all cAMP production and observes that both acute GH secretion and long-term GH synthesis are nearly abolished, confirming the central role of cAMP in GHRH signaling. She then asks a student to describe the two downstream effects of GHRH-induced cAMP elevation. Which of the following correctly describes both the short-term and long-term effects of elevated cAMP in somatotropes after GHRH stimulation?","choices":{"B":"Short-term: elevated cAMP activates phospholipase C to generate IP3 and DAG; IP3 releases ER calcium for vesicle fusion while DAG activates PKC to phosphorylate GH secretory machinery","A":"Short-term: elevated cAMP directly activates JAK2, which phosphorylates STAT5 to drive immediate GH release; long-term: STAT5 nuclear translocation drives GH gene transcription within the somatotrope nucleus","C":"Short-term: elevated cAMP promotes calcium influx through voltage-gated channels, driving GH-containing vesicle fusion and immediate GH exocytosis; long-term: PKA-mediated phosphorylation of transcription factors increases GH gene expression and builds secretory capacity","D":"Short-term: elevated cAMP activates the Ras-Raf-MEK-ERK MAPK cascade to phosphorylate cytoskeletal proteins and mobilize GH vesicles; long-term: ERK phosphorylates CREB to drive GH transcription","E":"Short-term: elevated cAMP directly phosphorylates myosin light chain kinase to reorganize cytoskeletal actin filaments for vesicle exocytosis; long-term: sustained cAMP activates nuclear export of GH mRNA"},"explanation":"GHRH-driven cAMP elevation in somatotropes mediates GH secretion through two temporally distinct mechanisms: in the short term, cAMP activates protein kinase A, which promotes calcium influx through voltage-gated calcium channels; the rise in intracellular calcium triggers fusion of GH-laden secretory vesicles with the plasma membrane and immediate GH exocytosis occurring within minutes. In the long term, sustained PKA activity phosphorylates CREB and other nuclear transcription factors, increasing transcription of the GH gene and building the secretory reserve over hours. JAK2-STAT5 is the GH receptor signal transduction mechanism at target tissues, not the GHRH mechanism in somatotropes. PLC-IP3-DAG and MAPK pathways are not the primary transduction routes for Gs-cAMP signaling in this context.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GHRH → Gs → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA → calcium influx and GH transcription. This dual short-term and long-term cAMP effect is a testable mechanistic concept. COMLEX: Contrast with somatostatin Gi → decreased cAMP → less GH — the opposite effector pathway.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — JAK2-STAT5 signaling is the GH receptor mechanism at target tissues, distinct from GHRH-Gs-cAMP signaling at somatotropes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"D":"PLC-IP3-DAG and MAPK pathways "}},{"id":"sdl7_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the roles of GHRH and somatostatin in regulating growth hormone secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-year-old girl undergoes resection of a hypothalamic tumor. Over the following months she develops marked hyperphagia and weight gain, and her growth velocity falls. GH response to provocative testing is low, but GH rises normally when exogenous GHRH is injected. MRI shows postoperative damage centered on the arcuate and ventromedial nuclei, with the periventricular region spared. Which best explains her low GH secretion?","choices":{"C":"Loss of GHRH-secreting neurons, which lie in the arcuate and ventromedial nuclei, the same glucose-sensitive area that controls satiety and hunger","A":"Overactivity of surviving periventricular somatostatin neurons, released from arcuate restraint, which suppresses GH secretion by intact somatotropes","B":"Destruction of somatotropes in the tumor bed, because the arcuate nucleus lies within the pituitary stalk and its injury damages the anterior lobe","D":"Loss of ghrelin-secreting neurons in the ventromedial nucleus, because ghrelin made in the hypothalamus is the main stimulus for GH release","E":"Interruption of magnocellular axons from the supraoptic nucleus, which carry GHRH down the stalk for release from the posterior lobe"},"explanation":"Guyton (Ch 76) states that neurons in the arcuate and ventromedial nuclei secrete GHRH, and that this is the same area of the hypothalamus that is sensitive to blood glucose, causing satiety in hyperglycemic states and hunger in hypoglycemic states. Somatostatin is secreted by the nearby periventricular neurons. Damage to this region therefore explains both her hyperphagia and her low GH, and the normal GH rise to exogenous GHRH shows that the somatotropes themselves are intact. Increased somatostatin activity from released restraint is not the mechanism described. The arcuate nucleus is in the hypothalamus, not the pituitary, and the intact GHRH response argues against somatotrope loss. Ghrelin is secreted by the stomach before meals, not by hypothalamic neurons. Supraoptic magnocellular axons carry ADH and oxytocin, not GHRH, which reaches the anterior pituitary through portal vessels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GHRH neurons = arcuate and ventromedial nuclei; somatostatin neurons = periventricular nucleus; ADH = mainly supraoptic, oxytocin = mainly paraventricular (Guyton Ch 76). GHRH acts through Gs-cAMP. COMLEX: A normal GH rise after exogenous GHRH points to a hypothalamic rather than a pituitary cause of low GH.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — blood glucose and fatty acid levels are major regulators of growth hormone secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Increased somatostatin activit","B":"The arcuate nucleus is in the "}},{"id":"sdl7_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the roles of GHRH and somatostatin in regulating growth hormone secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A physiology student reviews how the hypothalamus controls growth hormone. Both GHRH and somatostatin are polypeptides carried to the anterior pituitary in the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal vessels, and both act on somatotropes. Which statement best summarizes the relative roles of these two hormones in the hypothalamic control of GH secretion?","choices":{"D":"Most hypothalamic control is mediated through stimulatory GHRH, with somatostatin providing a lesser inhibitory influence on somatotropes","A":"Most hypothalamic control comes from inhibitory somatostatin, with GHRH mainly restoring GH after a fall, the same pattern dopamine sets for prolactin","B":"GHRH and somatostatin come from the same arcuate neurons in alternating pulses, and their ratio is the main signal to somatotropes","C":"GHRH and somatostatin act on the liver rather than the pituitary, changing IGF-1 output, which then sets GH secretion entirely through feedback","E":"Neither hormone has much effect, because GH secretion is set mainly by hypothalamic nerve endings on somatotropes, as in the posterior lobe"},"explanation":"Guyton (Ch 76) states that GH secretion is controlled by two hypothalamic polypeptides carried in the portal vessels, GHRH (44 amino acids) and growth hormone inhibitory hormone, somatostatin (14 amino acids), and that most of the control of GH secretion is probably mediated through GHRH rather than through somatostatin. GHRH binds specific membrane receptors on somatotropes and raises cAMP. Prolactin is the hormone for which an inhibitory hormone, dopamine, exerts more control, so applying that pattern to GH is wrong. GHRH comes from arcuate and ventromedial neurons, while somatostatin comes from periventricular neurons, so they are not released by the same cells. Both act on the pituitary, not the liver. Anterior pituitary cells are controlled by hormones in portal blood, not by direct nerve endings, which is the posterior lobe mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH has both a releasing hormone (GHRH, dominant) and an inhibiting hormone (somatostatin); prolactin is controlled mainly by inhibition through dopamine (Guyton Ch 76). COMLEX: Hypothalamic catecholamines, dopamine, and serotonin all increase GH secretion (Guyton Ch 76); clonidine and levodopa raise GHRH and are used as provocative tests (Katzung Ch 37).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — matching anterior pituitary hormones with their hypothalamic releasing and inhibitory hormones","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"B":"GHRH comes from arcuate and ve","C":"Both act on the pituitary, not"}},{"id":"sdl7_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the clinical features of growth hormone deficiency (dwarfism), gigantism, and acromegaly.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman presents with a 4-year history of progressive enlargement of her hands and feet; she has required larger ring sizes and wider shoes each year. She also describes coarsening of her facial features, deepening voice, hyperhidrosis, and joint pains. Her serum IGF-1 is 685 ng/mL (markedly elevated for age). GH level 2 hours after 75 g oral glucose load is 4.2 ng/mL and does not fall below 1 ng/mL. MRI shows a 1.5 cm pituitary adenoma. Her height is 5 feet 6 inches, unchanged from age 24. Which of the following best explains why this patient's GH excess has not caused an increase in her height despite being severely elevated for years?","choices":{"C":"Her epiphyseal growth plates fused in early adulthood, so elevated GH cannot drive longitudinal bone elongation and instead acts only on membranous bones that retain lifelong growth capacity","A":"Her growth plates remain patent but chronically elevated IGF-1 has downregulated IGF-1 receptors on epiphyseal chondrocytes, creating receptor insensitivity that prevents further longitudinal growth","B":"Her pituitary adenoma produces a structurally abnormal GH isoform with reduced binding affinity for epiphyseal GH receptors, limiting its action to membranous bone periosteal cells","D":"Her elevated estrogen from normal ovarian function causes annual closure of any newly formed epiphyseal cartilage, preventing GH-driven longitudinal growth from proceeding despite elevated IGF-1","E":"Her GH receptor expression is constitutively absent at epiphyseal plate chondrocytes in adults but preserved on membranous bone periosteal cells, restricting GH actions to acral and craniofacial bones"},"explanation":"In acromegaly, GH excess develops after epiphyseal plate fusion; once fused, growth plates cannot be reopened by any level of GH or IGF-1, making longitudinal bone growth impossible. GH instead stimulates bones that retain growth capacity throughout adulthood, primarily membranous bones such as the jaw, frontal skull, supraorbital ridges, nose, and acral bones of the hands and feet. The unchanged height despite years of GH excess confirms that epiphyseal plates are fused and only membranous bone growth is occurring. IGF-1 receptor downregulation at fused growth plates is not the mechanism; the plates are simply absent. The adenoma produces structurally normal GH. Estrogen causes one-time growth plate fusion during puberty but does not annually re-close plates. GH receptor expression patterns do not explain the difference; the physical absence of the growth plate is the correct explanation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diagnosis of acromegaly requires elevated IGF-1 plus GH failure to suppress below 1 ng/mL on OGTT. Classic features include coarsened facial features, enlarged hands and feet, organomegaly, carpal tunnel, and kyphosis. COMLEX: Acromegaly is listed in COMLEX CP3 as a named physical exam finding; gigantism occurs before fusion and allows height increase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — Hyperpituitarism from GH-secreting somatotroph adenomas produces acromegaly when excess occurs after epiphyseal closure","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"IGF-1 receptor downregulation ","D":"Estrogen causes one-time growt","E":"GH receptor expression pattern"}},{"id":"sdl7_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the clinical features of growth hormone deficiency (dwarfism), gigantism, and acromegaly.","batch":2,"stem":"A 13-year-old male is referred for evaluation of exceptionally rapid growth; he has grown 6 inches in the past year and now stands 6 feet 5 inches. His parents are both of average height. Physical examination shows normal body proportions and early puberty with Tanner stage 2 findings. Fasting glucose is 148 mg/dL; random GH is markedly elevated; IGF-1 is 5 times the upper limit of normal. MRI of the pituitary shows a 2.2 cm macroadenoma with suprasellar extension. Which of the following correctly describes the metabolic complication expected in approximately 10 percent of patients with this condition and its underlying mechanism?","choices":{"D":"Frank diabetes mellitus resulting from GH chronically inhibiting glucose uptake in peripheral skeletal muscle and adipose tissue while stimulating hepatic glucose output, producing insulin resistance and eventual beta-cell failure","A":"Primary adrenal insufficiency caused by ACTH deficiency from corticotroph cell compression by the macroadenoma, with resulting loss of cortisol and aldosterone production","B":"Central hypothyroidism caused by TSH deficiency from thyrotroph cell compression, producing fatigue, cold intolerance, and bradycardia from reduced thyroid hormone levels","C":"Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism from gonadotroph compression, causing delayed puberty and absent secondary sexual characteristics in this adolescent patient","E":"Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone from posterior pituitary compression by the expanding adenoma causing inappropriate water retention and dilutional hyponatremia"},"explanation":"This patient has gigantism from a GH-secreting macroadenoma developing before epiphyseal fusion. The classic metabolic complication of chronic GH excess is diabetes mellitus, occurring in approximately 10% of gigantism cases. GH is diabetogenic through three mechanisms: it directly inhibits glucose uptake in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue by blocking insulin receptor signaling, it stimulates hepatic glucose output, and it reduces peripheral insulin sensitivity collectively producing insulin resistance; sustained insulin resistance leads to beta-cell exhaustion and frank diabetes mellitus. The elevated fasting glucose of 148 mg/dL reflects early progression in this patient. Adrenal insufficiency, central hypothyroidism, and hypogonadism can occur from mass effect in any large pituitary adenoma but are not the metabolic complication attributable specifically to GH excess. SIADH is not characteristic of GH-secreting adenoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH excess causes hyperglycemia and insulin resistance; approximately 10% of untreated gigantism develops frank diabetes mellitus, reflecting GH's diabetogenic effects. This connects SDL 6 Obj 1 mechanism directly to clinical disease. COMLEX: Large pituitary adenomas can cause panhypopituitarism through mass effect; untreated gigantism can progress to panhypopituitarism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — GH decreases peripheral glucose utilization and is diabetogenic; this mechanism drives the hyperglycemia and diabetes complicating GH-secreting adenoma","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"B":"Adrenal insufficiency, central","C":"Adrenal insufficiency, central","E":"SIADH is not characteristic of"}},{"id":"sdl7_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the clinical features of growth hormone deficiency (dwarfism), gigantism, and acromegaly.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old male from a consanguineous family presents with severe proportionate short stature; he is at the less than 1st percentile for height with a chubby, cherubic facial appearance and prominent forehead. His parents report he has always been much shorter than peers. Laboratory evaluation shows markedly elevated serum GH at 45 ng/mL and very low IGF-1 at 20 ng/mL (low for age). An endocrinologist performs a 4-day recombinant human GH stimulation test; IGF-1 fails to rise and remains below 30 ng/mL throughout. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of this child's condition?","choices":{"A":"Laron syndrome from a GH receptor loss-of-function mutation, causing GH insensitivity in which GH cannot signal through its receptor to stimulate hepatic IGF-1 production despite high circulating GH","B":"Panhypopituitarism with complete absence of pituitary GH secretion, producing undetectable serum GH and consequently low IGF-1 that responds to exogenous recombinant GH administration","C":"Constitutional growth delay with delayed bone age and entirely normal GH axis function, in which final adult height will reach the normal range without GH replacement therapy","D":"African pygmy growth variant with normal GH levels, normal GH receptor function, and normal IGF-1 generation but constitutively reduced tissue responsiveness to IGF-1 through post-receptor signaling differences","E":"Isolated GHRH deficiency from hypothalamic dysfunction causing undetectable GH that produces secondary low IGF-1 responding to exogenous GH administration by rising into the normal range"},"explanation":"This clinical picture is the hallmark of Laron syndrome: an autosomal recessive condition caused by loss-of-function mutations in the GH receptor gene. Because GH cannot signal through its receptor, the liver cannot generate IGF-1 in response to GH, creating a state of GH insensitivity. Circulating GH is markedly elevated due to absent negative feedback from IGF-1, yet IGF-1 remains very low. The definitive diagnostic feature is failure of exogenous GH administration to raise IGF-1, demonstrating receptor-level resistance rather than deficient GH secretion. Panhypopituitarism produces very low GH that would respond to exogenous GH by raising IGF-1. Constitutional growth delay has normal GH and normal IGF-1 response. African pygmies have normal GH and GH receptor function with modestly reduced IGF-1 generation through separate hepatic mechanisms; they show partial IGF-1 response to exogenous GH. Isolated GHRH deficiency produces low GH that responds to exogenous GH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Laron syndrome equals GH receptor mutation with markedly elevated GH, very low IGF-1, and no IGF-1 response to exogenous GH. Treatment is recombinant IGF-1, not GH. Contrast with pituitary dwarfism: low GH, low IGF-1, responds to exogenous GH. COMLEX: Distinguish receptor-level resistance from secretory deficiency for boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — GH receptor JAK2-STAT5 signaling drives hepatic IGF-1 production; loss-of-function GH receptor mutation in Laron syndrome abolishes this entire downstream pathway","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Panhypopituitarism produces ve","C":"Constitutional growth delay ha"}}]};
